; ======================================================================
;  anvilqgpuprobe.unoq - Arduino UNO Q (QCM2290): THE GPU CLOCK LADDER.
;
;  Milestone 1 of the bare-metal Adreno 702 bring-up. The full plan, with
;  a citation for every number below, is in the vault at
;  "Arduino UNO Q/Adreno 702 bring-up plan.md".
;
;  ------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHY THIS PROGRAM IS SHAPED THE WAY IT IS
;  ------------------------------------------------------------------
;  On this SoC an MMIO access to a block whose clock is off does NOT
;  return 0xFFFFFFFF and does NOT fault. It stalls the AXI bus. The core
;  wedges, the board goes dark, SSH disappears, and the only cure is
;  pulling the power.
;
;  That is not a theory. It happened here, twice. The transcript on the
;  EFI System Partition survived and named the instruction: the very
;  first read of the serial-engine wrapper's own version register. Not a
;  sub-block - the wrapper. Meanwhile the pin controller, which lives in
;  the always-on domain, read and drove perfectly in the same session.
;
;  So the working assumption for the graphics block is the pessimistic
;  one: it is dark, its clock controller is dark, and the path to its
;  clock controller is dark. Three gates deep. Every one of them has to
;  be EARNED, in order, and the moment one says "off" this program stops.
;
;  ------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE THREE RULES THIS FILE OBEYS
;  ------------------------------------------------------------------
;  1. PRINT BEFORE YOU ACT. Every step announces the address it is about
;     to touch, and the transcript is pushed to the EFI System Partition
;     BEFORE the access happens. If the board dies, the last line of that
;     file is the instruction that killed it. A program that prints its
;     results afterwards tells you nothing about the step that hung.
;
;  2. A FAILED CHECK PRINTS AND STOPS. It never falls through to the
;     access it was guarding. gStop latches, and every later step refuses
;     by name rather than quietly doing nothing.
;
;  3. OBSERVE FIRST, ENABLE SECOND, AND NEVER IN THE SAME RUN AS A NEW
;     BLOCK. See #GPUPROBE_ENABLE below.
;
;  ------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHAT THIS PROBE CAN AND CANNOT PROVE
;  ------------------------------------------------------------------
;  It CANNOT read a chip id. There is no chip-identity register anywhere
;  in the open register map for this part - the kernel driver takes the
;  value out of the device tree and never asks the hardware. Any report
;  claiming to have read one back off this silicon is quoting a constant
;  that was compiled in. What this probe can honestly establish is:
;
;    - the clock and power-domain state, read from the controllers
;      themselves, printed as raw register values;
;    - whether the graphics register window answers at all (a read that
;      is neither all-zeroes nor all-ones is a live block);
;    - whether a value WRITTEN to a scratch register survives a read
;      back, which is the difference between "responding" and "alive";
;    - whether the secure-mode control register can be read and written,
;      which decides whether the secure-world image is needed at all.
;
;  BUILD:  bash tools/build_unoq_gpuprobe.sh
; ======================================================================

EnableExplicit

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  #GPUPROBE_ENABLE - 0 = OBSERVE ONLY (the default, and the only value
;  that should ever be flashed onto a board whose state is unknown).
;
;  At 0 this program writes NOTHING to any clock or power register. It
;  reads the controllers, prints what it found, and stops before the
;  graphics window. That run is what tells us what the firmware left
;  behind, and it is the run that has to happen first.
;
;  At 1 it will additionally bring the domain up - and it will still
;  refuse to do so unless the observation phase agreed the path to the
;  clock controller is live. Do not set this until a run at 0 has come
;  back and its numbers are in the vault.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#GPUPROBE_ENABLE = 1

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  There is deliberately NO #GPUPROBE_SECVID constant any more.
;
;  There used to be one, set to 0, with a note saying the secure-mode
;  register was never written. It guarded one write site correctly and a
;  second site was added later that never went under it - so the write
;  kept happening while the documentation said it did not, and it hung
;  the board twice more.
;
;  A switch invites a second site. The write is now simply absent from
;  the source, and tools/check_no_secvid.sh proves it is absent from the
;  assembled listing as well, because the constant was never the proof.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  #GPUPROBE_ZAP - 1 = hand the signed image to the secure world, then,
;  ONLY if it authenticates, write the secure-mode register.
;
;  This is the one operation in this program that leaves the processor.
;  Everything else is a load or a store; this asks the secure monitor to
;  authenticate a signed image and unlock the GPU. The call ids, the
;  argument encodings and the image layout were all written down and
;  reviewed BEFORE any of it was implemented - that was the condition on
;  which it was allowed - and they are in the vault note, section 9.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#GPUPROBE_ZAP = 1

; ---- The secure-monitor calls. Function id is
;      (type<<31) | (SMC64<<30) | (SIP<<24) | (svc<<8) | cmd,
;      with SIP = 2. type 0 = yielding, 1 = fast.
;      include/linux/arm-smccc.h:25-54, qcom_scm.h:101-124.
#SMC_IS_CALL_AVAIL   = $C2000601   ; fast:    svc 06 cmd 01
#SMC_PAS_INIT_IMAGE  = $42000201   ; yielding: svc 02 cmd 01
#SMC_PAS_MEM_SETUP   = $42000202   ; yielding: svc 02 cmd 02
#SMC_PAS_AUTH_RESET  = $42000205   ; yielding: svc 02 cmd 05

; ---- arginfo: (nargs & 0xf) | (type0<<4) | (type1<<6) | ...
;      types: 0 VAL, 1 RO, 2 RW, 3 BUFVAL.  qcom_scm.h:22-42.
#ARGINFO_INIT_IMAGE  = $82         ; 2 args: VAL, RW
#ARGINFO_MEM_SETUP   = $03         ; 3 args: all VAL
#ARGINFO_AUTH_RESET  = $01         ; 1 arg:  VAL

#PAS_ID_GPU          = 13          ; a6xx_gpu.c:17 - hardcoded, not from DT

; ---- the carve-out the signed image is authenticated in.
;      agatti.dtsi:355, and the live tree agrees.
#PIL_GPU_BASE        = $53615000
#PIL_GPU_SIZE        = $2000

; ---- a702_zap.mbn, parsed program header by program header. Every one
;      of these was read out of the blob in the repo and is re-checked at
;      run time before use, so a different image staged by mistake is a
;      named refusal rather than a secure-world fault.
#ZAP_FILE_LEN        = 13804
#ZAP_MD_HDR_LEN      = 148         ; ELF header + 3 program headers
#ZAP_MD_HASH_OFF     = $1000       ; the hash segment lives here
#ZAP_MD_HASH_LEN     = 6712
#ZAP_MD_TOTAL        = 6860        ; 148 + 6712
#ZAP_SEG_OFF         = $3000       ; the one PT_LOAD segment
#ZAP_SEG_LEN         = 1516
#ZAP_REGION_LEN      = $1000       ; max_addr - min_addr, 4 KiB

; ---- secure-monitor return codes. qcom_scm.h:168-176. NOT errno.
#SCM_OK              = 0
#SCM_ERROR           = -1
#SCM_EINVAL_ARG      = -2
#SCM_EINVAL_ADDR     = -3
#SCM_EOPNOTSUPP      = -4
#SCM_ENOMEM          = -5
#SCM_V2_EBUSY        = -12
#SCM_INTERRUPTED     = 1
#SCM_WAITQ_SLEEP     = 2

; ---- the GPU-IOMMU aperture call. Documented in full in section 9b of
;      the vault note BEFORE it was implemented, which is the standing
;      condition on issuing one at all.
;      qcom_scm.c:1196-1220, qcom_scm.h:117,124.
#SMC_CP_SMMU_APERTURE = $42000C1B   ; yielding, SMC64, SIP, svc 0c cmd 1b
#ARGINFO_APERTURE     = $04         ; four arguments, all plain values
#APERTURE_CTX_BANK0   = $FFFF0000   ; 0xffff0000 | context bank, bank 0
#APERTURE_FILL        = $FFFFFFFF   ; args[1..3], all ones
#APERTURE_AVAIL_ARG   = $02000C1B   ; the call's own id, for the probe

; ---- the IOMMU's CONTEXT BANKS, a different address space from the
;      global registers that were refused.
;
;      ID1 read 0x20000005: bit 31 clear so pages are 4 KiB, and
;      bits 30:28 = 2 so there are 1 << (2+1) = 8 global pages. The
;      context banks start at page 8.
;         0x059A0000 + (8 * 0x1000) = 0x059A8000, stride 0x1000.
;      arm-smmu.c derives it exactly that way from the same two fields.
;
;      Register offsets within a bank: arm-smmu.h:145,169,193.
#SMMU_CB0             = $059A8000
#SMMU_CB_SCTLR        = $00         ; bit 0 = M, translation enable
#SMMU_CB_TTBR0        = $20         ; 64-bit
#SMMU_CB_CONTEXTIDR   = $34
#SMMU_CB_FSR          = $58         ; arm-smmu.h:200 - context fault status
#SMMU_CB_FAR          = $60         ; arm-smmu.h:226 - faulting address
#SMMU_CB_FSYNR0       = $68         ; arm-smmu.h:228
#SMMU_GR0_SGFSR       = $48         ; arm-smmu.h:83 - global fault status

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  #GPUPROBE_CP - milestone 2: microcode, ring buffer, and the first
;  packets the command processor ever executes on this board.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#GPUPROBE_CP = 1

; ---- the GPU IOMMU. agatti.dtsi:1821. Read-only here: this run finds
;      out whether it is translating or passing addresses through, and
;      REFUSES to set a ring buffer up if it is translating, because the
;      command processor's first fetch would fault against page tables
;      that do not exist.
#SMMU_BASE           = $059A0000
#SMMU_SCR0           = $059A0000   ; bit 0 CLIENTPD: 1 = SMMU off, all bypass
#SMMU_SCR1           = $059A0004   ; NSNUMSMRGO [7:0], NSNUMCBO [15:8]
#SMMU_NS_ALIAS       = $059A0400   ; exploratory only - see the note in step 7
#SMMU_ID0            = $059A0020   ; [7:0] number of stream-match groups
#SMMU_ID1            = $059A0024   ; [7:0] context banks, [31] page size
#SMMU_SMR0           = $059A0800   ; +4n
#SMMU_S2CR0          = $059A0C00   ; +4n

; ---- THE AUXILIARY CONFIGURATION REGISTER AND THE VERSION REGISTER.
;      READ ONLY. This program never writes either of them.
;
;      arm-smmu.h:42 puts sACR at GR0 + 0x10 and arm-smmu.h:79 puts ID7
;      at GR0 + 0x3c, with the MMU-500 revision in ID7[7:4]. The bit
;      names are arm-smmu-impl.c:106-108.
;
;      Why they are worth reading: arm_mmu500_reset()'s own comment
;      (arm-smmu-impl.c:113-117) says CACHE_LOCK must be clear before
;      writes to the context-bank ACTLRs will stick, "and we just hope
;      that Secure has also cleared SACR.CACHE_LOCK for this to take
;      effect". That is the only documented mechanism on this IP where a
;      write is silently discarded, and it names the secure world as the
;      holder of the copy that decides.
;
;      It CANNOT be the explanation for the stream-table refusals -
;      impl->reset() runs AFTER the stream-match writes in
;      arm_smmu_device_reset(), so the reference driver programs the whole
;      table with this bit in whatever state firmware left it and those
;      writes stick. This is a reading, not a theory, and must not be
;      written up as a cause.
#SMMU_SACR             = $059A0010   ; CACHE_LOCK bit 26, TLBEN bits 8 and 10
#SMMU_ID7              = $059A003C   ; MAJOR [7:4], MINOR [3:0]
#SMMU_SGFSR            = $059A0048   ; global fault status, write-1-to-clear
#SACR_CACHE_LOCK_BIT   = 26
#SACR_SMTNMB_TLBEN_BIT = 8
#SACR_S2CRB_TLBEN_BIT  = 10

; ---- the +0x400 window. NOT exploratory any more.
;      arm-smmu-impl.c:13-25 (arm_smmu_gr0_ns) maps sCR0, sACR, sGFSR and
;      the three sGFSYNR registers to offset + 0x400 for the Calxeda
;      integration, so a non-secure alias of exactly these registers IS a
;      shape this driver knows. Our device does not use that hook - the
;      Adreno implementation reaches the secure offsets directly - but the
;      window costs three reads and says whether this integration provides
;      one.
#SMMU_NSA_SCR0       = $059A0400
#SMMU_NSA_SACR       = $059A0410
#SMMU_NSA_SGFSR      = $059A0448

; ---- section 11u: the registers arm_smmu_device_reset() touches that
;      nothing in this program has touched before. Offsets from
;      arm-smmu.h:92-97 and :145,:218.
#SMMU_ID2            = $059A0028   ; VMID16 in bit 15
#SMMU_TLBIALLNSNH    = $059A0068
#SMMU_TLBIALLH       = $059A006C
#SMMU_STLBGSYNC      = $059A0070
#SMMU_STLBGSTATUS    = $059A0074   ; GSACTIVE bit 0
#SMMU_CB_ACTLR       = $04         ; MMU-500 CPRE is bit 1
#SMMU_CB_STRIDE      = $1000
; QCOM_DUMMY_VAL. arm-smmu.c:51 defines it as -1 and every TLB
; invalidate and sync in the driver writes exactly that. The comment
; there explains why it is not zero: some deployed hypervisor firmware
; mishandles a store made from the zero register.
#SMMU_DUMMY_VAL      = $FFFFFFFF
; ARM_SMMU_CB_FSR_FAULT, expanded from arm-smmu.h:218-224:
;   MULTI(31) SS(30) UUT(8) ASF(7) TLBLKF(6) TLBMCF(5) EF(4) PF(3)
;   AFF(2) TF(1)
#SMMU_CB_FSR_FAULT   = $C00001FE
; s2cr_init_val (arm-smmu.c:66-68) with CONFIG_ARM_SMMU_DISABLE_BYPASS_
; BY_DEFAULT=y, which this board's own /boot/config confirms: type =
; S2CR_TYPE_FAULT = 2, every other field zero.
;   FIELD_PREP(ARM_SMMU_S2CR_TYPE, 2) = 2 << 16
#SMMU_S2CR_RESET     = $00020000
; the two bits arm_mmu500_reset ORs into sACR (arm-smmu-impl.c:106-108)
#SACR_TLBEN_BOTH     = $00000500

#GPUCC_HLOS1_VOTE_SMMU_CBCR = $05995000

; ---- command processor
#GPU_CP_SQE_INSTR_BASE = $059020C0   ; 64-bit
#GPU_CP_RB_BASE        = $05902000   ; 64-bit
#GPU_CP_RB_CNTL        = $05902008
#GPU_CP_RB_RPTR_ADDR   = $05902010   ; 64-bit
#GPU_CP_APRIV_CNTL     = $05902110
#GPU_CP_ADDR_MODE_CNTL = $05902108
#GPU_CP_MEM_POOL_SIZE  = $0590230C
#GPU_CP_MEM_POOL_DBG   = $05902438
#GPU_CP_AHB_CNTL       = $05902634
#GPU_CP_CHICKEN_DBG    = $05902104
#GPU_CP_ROQ_THRESH1    = $05902304
#GPU_CP_ROQ_THRESH2    = $05902308
; The SQE's instruction-memory debug port. a6xx.xml:342-343. Writing an
; index to ADDR and reading DATA walks what the command processor ACTUALLY
; holds - which is the only way to tell "the bytes in DRAM are wrong" from
; "the processor never read the bytes in DRAM".
#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_ADDR = $05902440
#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_DATA = $05902444
; A SECOND indexed debug port into the same processor, and the reason it
; is here is that the one above has never had a control. a6xx.xml:334-335,
; declared 0x33 entries deep by the reference driver's own crash dump
; (a6xx_gpu_state.h:430). It reads the SQE's internal state registers. If
; THIS port answers and the microcode port does not, the broken thing is
; the instrument, not the memory it claims to describe.
#GPU_CP_SQE_STAT_ADDR      = $05902420
#GPU_CP_SQE_STAT_DATA      = $05902424
#GPU_CP_SQE_STAT_COUNT     = $33
; The index this build writes into the microcode port's address register
; purely so it can be read back. It is inside the window (0x8000 deep)
; and it has no run of identical nibbles, so a stuck bus or a
; half-decoded address cannot accidentally reproduce it.
#SQE_DBG_ADDR_PROBE        = $00001234
; Section 11x. The two patterns BUILD M2-V pushes into instruction memory
; indices 0 and 1 through the debug port's DATA half. If they come back,
; the processor's instruction memory is loadable FROM THE CPU, over the
; configuration bus - a path that never touches GBIF and therefore never
; touches the memory-management unit whose three held bits section 11v
; measured. Neither pattern has a run of identical nibbles, so a stuck
; bus or a half-decoded address cannot accidentally reproduce either.
#SQE_DBG_DATA_PAT0         = $A5C3F096
#SQE_DBG_DATA_PAT1         = $5EC1BEEF

; ---- section 11z. THE READ-ONLY QUEUE -------------------------------
; The THIRD indexed debug port on this processor, and the only instrument
; that can answer "did the command processor ever fetch the ring". The
; ROQ is where ring-buffer dwords LAND after the processor has read them
; from memory and before it parses them. If our sixteen packet dwords
; appear in here, a transaction left the GPU, reached DRAM and came back,
; and every "nothing is ever requested" sentence in this campaign is
; wrong. If it stays empty while the ring is handed over, they are right.
;   a6xx.xml:338-339, dwords 0x090c / 0x090d.
;   Read by the reference driver as the CP_ROQ entry of
;   a6xx_indexed_reglist[] (a6xx_gpu_state.c), with the same
;   write-address-then-read-data shape this program already uses on the
;   two ports that answered it in M2-U and M2-V.
#GPU_CP_ROQ_DBG_ADDR       = $05902430
#GPU_CP_ROQ_DBG_DATA       = $05902434
; The index written into the ROQ port's address register purely to be
; read back. Inside the window and with no run of identical nibbles, so a
; stuck bus or a half-decoded address cannot reproduce it.
#ROQ_DBG_ADDR_PROBE        = $000001A7
; a6xx_get_cp_roq_size() is CP_ROQ_THRESHOLDS_2 >> 14, in dwords
; (a6xx_gpu_state.c:1475-1478). S8.2 writes $00800060 there, so the depth
; is 512 - but this build READS THAT REGISTER BACK and sweeps what the
; silicon says, because a constant and a register that are supposed to
; agree are exactly the pair that drifts. The whole declared depth is
; swept, never a sample of it: that is section 11w's lesson, which this
; campaign has now had to learn twice.
#ROQ_SHIFT                 = 14
#ROQ_DEPTH_FALLBACK        = 512
#ROQ_DEPTH_MAX             = 2048
#GPU_CP_PROTECT_CNTL   = $0590213C
#GPU_CP_PROTECT_STATUS = $05902090   ; a6xx.xml:184, dword 0x824
#GPU_CP_HW_FAULT       = $05902084
#GPU_CP_INTERRUPT_STAT = $0590208C
#GPU_PC_DBG_ECO_CNTL   = $05927800

; ---- the rest of the init the command processor needs
#GPU_RBBM_GBIF_HALT       = $05900058
#GPU_GBIF_HALT            = $0590F114
#GPU_GBIF_QSB_SIDE0       = $0590F00C
#GPU_RBBM_GBIF_QOS_CNTL   = $05900044

; Section 11ab. The acknowledgement halves of the two GBIF halt registers.
; The reference driver only ever reads these to confirm a halt it just
; REQUESTED (a6xx_gpu.c:2004-2015, spin_until(... HALT_ACK) & 1); nothing in
; the vendored sources says what they read when no halt is outstanding, and
; mesa's reference crash dump shows GBIF_HALT 0 alongside GBIF_HALT_ACK 0x3
; (crash_prefetch.log:329-330). So they are read here as an OBSERVATION and
; the verdict is left to the halt registers' own read-back. Saying which of
; two readings is allowed to decide, before the run, is the whole point.
#GPU_GBIF_HALT_ACK        = $0590F118   ; a6xx.xml:1279, dword 0x3c46
#GPU_RBBM_GBIF_HALT_ACK   = $0590005C   ; a6xx.xml:785,  dword 0x17
#GPU_SECVID_TSB_CNTL      = $0593E00C
#GPU_SECVID_TSB_TR_BASE   = $0593E000   ; 64-bit
#GPU_SECVID_TSB_TR_SIZE   = $0593E008
#GPU_SECVID_TSB_ADDR_MODE = $0593E040
#GPU_UCHE_WRITE_RANGE_MAX = $05903814   ; 64-bit
#GPU_UCHE_TRAP_BASE       = $05903824   ; 64-bit
#GPU_UCHE_WRITE_THRU_BASE = $0590381C   ; 64-bit
#GPU_UCHE_FILTER_CNTL     = $05903860
#GPU_UCHE_CACHE_WAYS      = $0590385C
#GPU_UCHE_CLIENT_PF       = $05903864
#GPU_RBBM_PERFCTR_CNTL    = $05901400
#GPU_RBBM_HANG_INT_CNTL   = $0590007C

; the eleven blocks that need telling addresses are 64 bits wide. This
; matters more here than it looks: the firmware places this image where
; it likes and our buffers came back ABOVE 4 GB on the last run, so a
; block left in 32-bit mode would fetch from the wrong place entirely.
#GPU_VSC_ADDR_MODE   = $05903004
#GPU_GRAS_ADDR_MODE  = $05921804
#GPU_RB_ADDR_MODE    = $05923814
#GPU_PC_ADDR_MODE    = $05927804
#GPU_HLSQ_ADDR_MODE  = $0592F814
#GPU_VFD_ADDR_MODE   = $05929804
#GPU_VPC_ADDR_MODE   = $05925804
#GPU_UCHE_ADDR_MODE  = $05903800
#GPU_SP_ADDR_MODE    = $0592B804
#GPU_TPL1_ADDR_MODE  = $0592D804

; ---- the microcode. Version 0.49 from the repository, NOT the 0.47 the
;      board's own operating system carries - the two differ in size and
;      in their jump table, and a run must say which it used.
#SQE_FILE_LEN        = 21652
#SQE_SKIP            = 4          ; the first dword is NOT microcode
#SQE_LOAD_LEN        = 21648

; ---- ring geometry. msm_gpu.h:305-311.
;      BUFSZ = log2(32768/8) = 12, BLKSZ = log2(32/8) = 2
;      BUFSZ is bits 5:0, BLKSZ is bits 13:8  -> 0x0000020C
#RING_BYTES          = 32768
#RING_DWORDS         = 8192
#RB_CNTL_VALUE       = $0000020C

; ---- packet opcodes, adreno_pm4.xml
#CP_NOP              = $10
#CP_ME_INIT          = $48
#CP_MEM_WRITE        = $3D
#CP_SET_SECURE_MODE  = $66

#MARKER_VALUE        = $5EC1BEEF

; ---- Q-local backends first (they define the seams the core needs) ---
XIncludeFile "ArduinoQ/Board/memmap_q.unoq"
XIncludeFile "ArduinoQ/Board/qsink.pi4"
XIncludeFile "ArduinoQ/Board/hwtimer_q.unoq"
XIncludeFile "ArduinoQ/Board/hwinfo_q.unoq"
; ---- the UEFI plumbing: console, ESP file, stall, reset --------------
XIncludeFile "ArduinoQ/Board/qefi_q.unoq"
; ---- portable core, reused unchanged --------------------------------
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/format.pbi"

; ======================================================================
;  THE REGISTER MAP.
;
;  Every constant carries its source. "agatti.dtsi" and the driver files
;  are in the repo under ArduinoQ/Reference/Adreno702/. The absolute
;  addresses were derived once, in the vault note, and are repeated here
;  as literals so that this file can be checked against the note without
;  running anything.
; ======================================================================

; ---- GCC. The global clock controller. This is the block the CPU is
;      itself clocked out of, which is the whole reason its registers are
;      a defensible first read. agatti.dtsi:775 -------------------------
#GCC_BASE                = $01400000

; The branch that carries the configuration-AHB path to the graphics
; clock controller. gcc-qcm2290.c:2853 lists it as a "critical" branch
; that the driver force-enables at probe rather than modelling as a
; clock - so it may well already be on, and it is the one register in
; this whole ladder whose read cannot itself be the hang.
#GCC_GPU_CFG_AHB_CBCR    = $01436004   ; gcc-qcm2290.c:2853
#GCC_BIMC_GPU_AXI_CBCR   = $01471154   ; gcc-qcm2290.c:1340
#GCC_GPU_MEMNOC_GFX_CBCR = $0143600C   ; gcc-qcm2290.c:1952
#GCC_GPU_SNOC_DVM_CBCR   = $01436018   ; gcc-qcm2290.c:1967
#GCC_APCS_VOTE_79004     = $01479004   ; GPLL0 taps: bit 15 and bit 16
#GCC_GPLL0_MODE          = $01400000   ; gcc-qcm2290.c:57, offset 0
#GCC_GPLL0_L_VAL         = $01400004
#GCC_GPLL0_USER_CTL      = $01400018

; A clock branch control register. clk-branch.c:43-60.
#CBCR_ENABLE_BIT         = 0           ; write 1 to run
#CBCR_HWCG_BIT           = 1           ; hardware clock gating enabled
#CBCR_CLK_OFF_BIT        = 31          ; STATUS: 1 = halted, 0 = running

; ---- GPUCC. The graphics clock controller. agatti.dtsi:1807 ----------
#GPUCC_BASE              = $05990000
#GPUCC_PLL0_MODE         = $05990000   ; gpucc-qcm2290.c:58
#GPUCC_PLL0_L_VAL        = $05990004
#GPUCC_PLL0_USER_CTL     = $0599000C
#GPUCC_PLL0_STATUS       = $05990038
#GPUCC_GX_BCR            = $05991008   ; gpucc-qcm2290.c:339
#GPUCC_GX_GDSCR          = $0599100C   ; gpucc-qcm2290.c:308
#GPUCC_GX_CFG_GDSCR      = $05991010   ; gdscr + 4; the GX status register
#GPUCC_GFX3D_CMD_RCGR    = $0599101C   ; gpucc-qcm2290.c:148
#GPUCC_GFX3D_CFG_RCGR    = $05991020
#GPUCC_GX_GFX3D_CBCR     = $05991054   ; "core"
#GPUCC_GX_CXO_CBCR       = $05991060
#GPUCC_CX_GDSCR          = $0599106C   ; gpucc-qcm2290.c:295
#GPUCC_AHB_CBCR          = $05991078   ; "iface"
#GPUCC_CX_GMU_CBCR       = $05991098   ; "gmu"
#GPUCC_CXO_CBCR          = $0599109C   ; "xo"
#GPUCC_GX_CLAMP_IO       = $05991508   ; gpucc-qcm2290.c:309
#GPUCC_CX_GDS_HW_CTRL    = $05991540   ; gpucc-qcm2290.c:296 - CX STATUS

; A power-domain control register. gdsc.c:21-51.
;   THE TRAP: the two domains are polled through DIFFERENT registers and
;   DIFFERENT bits. CX has a gds_hw_ctrl register and its "powered" bit
;   is bit 31 THERE, not in its own GDSCR. GX has POLL_CFG_GDSCR set and
;   its bit is bit 16 of the CFG register. Reading the GDSCR's own bit 31
;   for CX is simply the wrong register, and it will look plausible.
#GDSCR_SW_COLLAPSE_BIT   = 0           ; 1 = collapsed (OFF)
#GDSCR_HW_CONTROL_BIT    = 1
#GDSCR_RETAIN_FF_BIT     = 11
#GDSCR_PWR_ON_BIT        = 31          ; status
#CFG_GDSCR_PWR_UP_BIT    = 16          ; status, GX only
#CFG_GDSCR_PWR_DN_BIT    = 15

; ---- The graphics register window itself. agatti.dtsi:1699 -----------
;      NOTHING BELOW THIS LINE MAY BE TOUCHED until the ladder above has
;      said the domain is powered and clocked.
#GPU_BASE                = $05900000
#GPU_RBBM_STATUS         = $05900840   ; a6xx.xml:553, dword 0x210
#GPU_RBBM_STATUS1        = $05900844
#GPU_RBBM_STATUS2        = $05900848
#GPU_RBBM_STATUS3        = $0590084C
#GPU_RBBM_CLOCK_CNTL     = $059002B8   ; a6xx.xml:815, dword 0x0ae
#GPU_RBBM_INT_0_STATUS   = $05900804
#GPU_CP_SCRATCH0         = $0590220C   ; a6xx.xml:290, dword 0x883, 8 regs
#GPU_CP_SQE_CNTL         = $05902020
#GPU_CP_RB_RPTR          = $05902018
#GPU_CP_RB_WPTR          = $0590201C
#GPU_RBBM_SECVID_TRUST_CNTL = $0593D000 ; a6xx.xml:774, dword 0x0f400

; The value the reference driver writes to RBBM_CLOCK_CNTL for this exact
; part - a702 and nothing else. a6xx_gpu.c:652-653. If the register
; already reads this, something has run the initialisation sequence
; before us, which would be a finding in itself.
#A702_CLOCK_CNTL_ON      = $AAAAAA82

; A pattern with no run of identical nibbles, so a stuck bus or a
; half-decoded address cannot accidentally reproduce it.
#SCRATCH_PATTERN         = $A5C3F096

; ======================================================================
;  State.
; ======================================================================
Global gStop.i            ; latched: 1 once any check has failed
Global gStopWhy.i         ; the step number that latched it
Global gGpuOk.i           ; 1 once the graphics window is judged safe
Global gFlushes.i

; observations, kept so the summary can restate them together
Global gVGccAhb.i
Global gVGpuccAhb.i
Global gVCxStatus.i
Global gVCxGdscr.i
Global gVGxCfg.i
Global gVGxGdscr.i
Global gVRbbmStatus.i
Global gVClockCntl.i
Global gVScratchBack.i
Global gVSecvid.i
Global gSecvidWritable.i

; ---- the secure-monitor call registers. They cross into the ASM block
;      in globals because inline assembly here can only name
;      global_<lowercase>; there is no local-variable symbol.
Global gSmc0.i
Global gSmc1.i
Global gSmc2.i
Global gSmc3.i
Global gSmc4.i
Global gSmc5.i
Global gSmcR0.i
Global gSmcR1.i
Global gSmcR2.i
Global gSmcR3.i
Global gFlushA.i
Global gCurEl.i
Global gPfr0.i
Global gHvcRet.i
Global gAtAddr.i
Global gPar.i
Global gSctlr.i

; ---- the signed image, read off the boot partition, and the metadata
;      blob built out of it. In our own uninitialised data rather than at
;      a guessed DRAM address: guessing is how another worker lost a
;      board this week, and this memory is ours by construction.
Global Dim gZap.b[16384]
Global Dim gMeta.b[12288]          ; 6860 needed, plus room to 4 KiB-align
Global gMetaPhys.i
Global gZapOk.i
Global gZapStage.i

; ---- milestone 2 storage. In our own uninitialised data, for the same
;      reason as the metadata blob: the firmware decides where this image
;      lands, so a chosen address would be a guess. Each is over-allocated
;      so it can be aligned by hand.
Global Dim gSqe.b[32768]          ; 21652 for the microcode plus 4096 of
                                  ; alignment slack. 24576 was NOT enough:
                                  ; the read was capped at 24576-4096=20480
                                  ; and returned exactly maxlen, which the
                                  ; length check caught. A short read and a
                                  ; different firmware build look identical
                                  ; once the bytes are in memory, which is
                                  ; why that check exists.
Global Dim gRing.b[65536]         ; 32768 for the ring plus 32768 of alignment
                                  ; slack. 40960 was NOT ENOUGH and the shortfall
                                  ; was invisible: the base is rounded UP to a
                                  ; 32 KiB boundary, so up to 32767 bytes of the
                                  ; array can be skipped before the ring even
                                  ; starts - and the code then writes a full
                                  ; 32768 from there, running as much as 24 KiB
                                  ; PAST THE END of the array and over whatever
                                  ; globals follow. To align N bytes you must
                                  ; reserve N + (alignment - 1); reserving less
                                  ; is a buffer overrun that only appears when
                                  ; the allocator happens to place you badly.
Global Dim gAux.b[8192]
Global gSqeBase.i
Global gRingBase.i
Global gRptrShadow.i
Global gMarker.i
Global gRingPos.i          ; next free dword index in the ring
Global gSmmuBypass.i
Global gCpAlive.i
Global gMarkerLanded.i
Global gRptrSeen.i
; -1 = never read. NOT 0, because 0 is a real and very interesting value
; for this register and a global starts at 0. On the M2-C run step 8
; halted before reading it, the variable kept its initial 0, and the
; summary announced "secure mode LEFT" on a run where the command
; processor had never executed a single packet.
;
; That is the same defect as the stale power-domain snapshot earlier in
; this file: a summary that contradicts its own body, in the line a
; reader in a hurry trusts. The fix is a sentinel that cannot be
; confused with a reading.
Global gSecvidAfterCp.i = -1

; ---- the sACR / CACHE_LOCK reading (section 11t). Same sentinel
;      discipline as gSecvidAfterCp above: -1 means NEVER READ, because 0
;      is a real and interesting value for every one of these registers.
;      A summary that reports "CACHE_LOCK clear" on a run where the block
;      was never reached is the exact defect that cost round M2-C.
Global gSmmuClocked.i        ; 1 once all three IOMMU clocks are proven running
Global gId7.i = -1
Global gSacrBefore.i = -1
Global gSacrAfter.i = -1
Global gSgfsrBefore.i = -1
Global gSgfsrAfter.i = -1

; ---- section 11u, the byte-for-byte replication of arm_smmu_device_reset.
;      Same sentinel discipline: -1 is NEVER READ.
Global gLrRan.i              ; 1 once the replication actually executed
Global gLrSacrWant.i = -1    ; what the sACR write asked for
Global gLrSacrGot.i = -1     ; what it read back
Global gLrScr0Want.i = -1
Global gLrScr0Got.i = -1
; What sCR0 read BEFORE the sequence wrote it. This exists because the
; first version of this instrument would have LIED. It compared the
; read-back against the requested value and printed "discarded" on any
; difference - but bits 4 and 5 (GCFGFRE, GCFGFIE) are ORed in by the
; reference driver and read back CLEAR ON THIS DEVICE EVEN FOR LINUX,
; whose sCR0 is 002A1C06 and not 002A1C36. A successful write would
; therefore have been reported as a refusal. "Did anything change" is
; the question that can be answered honestly; "did it match exactly" is
; not, on a register with read-as-zero bits.
Global gLrScr0Found.i = -1
Global gLrS2crStuck.i = -1   ; how many of the four S2CRs took the reset value
Global gLrSyncTimedOut.i     ; 1 if the global TLB sync never went idle
Global gCbStale.i = -1       ; the context-bank stale-read verdict, see step 7c
Global gUsfcfgCleared.i = -1 ; section 11v: did USFCFG clear? -1 = never tried

; ---- section 11w. THE CONTROL ON THE DEBUG PORT ITSELF.
;      Every statement since 11l that "the command processor's instruction
;      memory is empty" comes from ONE instrument that has never been
;      checked against anything. These carry its verdict into the summary.
;      -1 is NEVER READ, for the same reason as every sentinel above: 0 is
;      a real and very interesting value for all of them.
Global gDbgAddrGot.i    = -1  ; what CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_ADDR read back after
                              ; being handed SQE_DBG_ADDR_PROBE. If this is
                              ; not 00001234 the port is not a window and
                              ; the campaign's central measurement is void.
Global gDbgAutoInc.i    = -1  ; ADDR after ONE data read from index 0.
                              ; 1 = the port auto-increments (11l's caveat
                              ; closed); 0 = 11l read index 0 six times.
Global gDbgNonZero.i    = -1  ; how many of the swept indices read non-zero
                              ; at the LAST sweep of the run
Global gSqeStatNonZero.i = -1 ; how many of CP_SQE_STAT's 0x33 words did
Global gUcode0Before.i  = -1  ; index 0 BEFORE CP_SQE_CNTL was written
Global gUcode0After.i   = -1  ; index 0 one millisecond after
Global gUcode0Late.i    = -1  ; index 0 after the whole 500 ms ring wait
; ---- section 11x. IS THE INSTRUCTION MEMORY WRITABLE FROM THE CPU?
;      Same sentinel discipline: -1 is NEVER TRIED.
Global gImemWrote.i     = -1  ; index 0 read back straight after the write
Global gImemWrote1.i    = -1  ; index 1 read back straight after the write
Global gImemAfterStart.i = -1 ; index 0 read back AFTER CP_SQE_CNTL = 1
; ---- section 11z. IS THE COMMAND PROCESSOR EXECUTING?
;      Same sentinel discipline: -1 is NEVER TRIED, and it matters more
;      here than anywhere, because the whole point of this section is
;      that CP_INTERRUPT_STATUS has been quoted since M2-T with no idea
;      what it read before the run started.
Global gIntStatS5.i      = -1 ; CP_INTERRUPT_STATUS at step 5, COLD, before
                              ; the secure step, the IOMMU and the whole of
                              ; step 8. THIS IS THE CONTROL. If it already
                              ; reads $80 here, then CP_ILLEGAL_INSTR_ERROR
                              ; is a power-on artefact and three sections
                              ; have been quoting a constant.
Global gIntStatPreStart.i = -1 ; ... immediately BEFORE CP_SQE_CNTL = 1
Global gIntStat1ms.i     = -1 ; ... one millisecond after the start bit and
                              ; BEFORE the ring is handed over. This is what
                              ; separates "the start bit did it" from "the
                              ; write-pointer did it" - no run has ever had
                              ; a reading between those two writes.
Global gIntStatAfter.i   = -1 ; ... after the 500 ms ring wait (the only
                              ; point any previous run has ever read)
Global gRbbmPreStart.i   = -1 ; RBBM_STATUS immediately before the start bit
Global gRoqAddrOk.i      = -1 ; 1 = the ROQ port's address half stored the
                              ; probe pattern and gave it back. Without this
                              ; the sweep below is a rumour, which is the
                              ; mistake M2-U was built to stop repeating.
Global gRoqDepth.i       = -1 ; the depth the silicon declares, from
                              ; CP_ROQ_THRESHOLDS_2 >> 14
Global gRoqNzStart.i     = -1 ; non-zero ROQ dwords one millisecond after
                              ; the start bit
Global gRoqNzWptr.i      = -1 ; ... and after the ring was handed over
Global gRoqFoundRing.i   = -1 ; how many of the ring's OWN dwords were found
                              ; in the queue after the hand-over. Non-zero
                              ; means the processor fetched from DRAM.
; ---- section 11ab: the step-8.2 configuration block, read back ------
; Twenty-eight writes have been made at step 8.2 on every run since M2-R
; and not one of them has ever been read back. These count what the block
; actually accepted. -1 means the block never ran.
Global gCfgWrites.i     = -1  ; how many registers step 8.2 wrote and checked
Global gCfgTook.i       = -1  ; ... how many read back the value they were given
Global gCfgZero.i       = -1  ; ... how many read back zero instead
Global gCfgOther.i      = -1  ; ... how many read back something else again
Global gCfgAddrMode.i   = -1  ; of the twelve *_ADDR_MODE_CNTL writes, how
                              ; many read back 1. THIS IS THE ONE THAT
                              ; MATTERS: the buffers are above 4 GB, so a
                              ; block left in 32-bit mode fetches from the
                              ; wrong address with no complaint from anything.
Global gGbifHaltGot.i   = -1  ; GBIF_HALT read back after being written 0
Global gRbbmGbifHaltGot.i = -1 ; RBBM_GBIF_HALT read back after being written 0
Global gGbifAckBefore.i = -1  ; GBIF_HALT_ACK before the halt writes
Global gGbifAckAfter.i  = -1  ; ... and after
Global gRbbmAckBefore.i = -1  ; RBBM_GBIF_HALT_ACK before the halt writes
Global gRbbmAckAfter.i  = -1  ; ... and after

Global gStatus3After.i  = -1  ; RBBM_STATUS3 after the failure. Bit 24 is
                              ; SMMU_STALLED_ON_FAULT (a6xx.xml:582) - the
                              ; GPU's own answer to the question this
                              ; campaign has spent five rounds inferring
                              ; from the IOMMU's fault registers instead.

; ======================================================================
;  THE TRANSCRIPT, AND WHY IT IS FLUSHED SO OFTEN.
;
;  Flush() rewrites the whole growing buffer to the EFI System Partition.
;  That is deliberately wasteful: it is the only way a hang leaves
;  evidence, because a bus stall does not unwind and nothing runs after
;  it. The buffer only ever grows, so each rewrite is at least as long as
;  the last and no stale tail can survive - which matters, because
;  opening a file with CREATE does not truncate it.
; ======================================================================
Procedure Flush()
  gFlushes = gFlushes + 1
  QEspWrite("\EFI\anvil\qgpu11.log", @gQOut[0], gQOutLen)
EndProcedure

; Say(*s) - one line to the transcript AND to the firmware console, then
; flush. The console is invisible on this bench (no display device, and
; the firmware's serial stdout is an unshifted 1.8 V line nobody is
; listening to) but writing to it costs nothing and anyone who attaches
; a level shifter sees the probe live.
Procedure Say(*s)
  ; UartWriteStr, NOT PrintN. Print/PrintN pick their formatter from the
  ; argument's TYPE, and an untyped pointer is not a string - so PrintN(*s)
  ; prints the ADDRESS IN DECIMAL and says nothing at all. That trap is
  ; written out in capitals in Anvil/Core/argfmt.pbi and it still caught
  ; this file on its first silicon run: every label in the transcript came
  ; back as a ten-digit number. A string LITERAL is fine; a string
  ; PARAMETER is not.
  UartWriteStr(*s)
  PrintNl()
  QEfiOutStr(*s)
  Flush()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Announce(step, *what, addr) - the print-before-you-act primitive.
;  After this returns, the transcript on the ESP already contains the
;  address. If the board dies on the next instruction, that line is the
;  last one in the file and it names the killer.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure Announce(step.i, *what, addr.i)
  Print("S")
  PrintDec(step)
  Print(" about to read ")
  UartWriteStr(*what)
  Print(" at ")
  PutHex8(addr)
  PrintNl()
  Flush()
EndProcedure

; Report the value that came back, and the two readings that mean "this
; block is not really there".
Procedure Got(*what, v.i)
  Print("   ")
  UartWriteStr(*what)
  Print(" = ")
  PutHex8(v)
  If v = 0
    ; Not necessarily bad: several of these registers are legitimately
    ; zero at reset. It is only evidence when the register is one that
    ; must not be zero.
    Print("   (zero)")
  EndIf
  If v = $FFFFFFFF
    Print("   (all ones - an absent block reads like this)")
  EndIf
  ; DEAFBEAD is this part's OWN marker for a read that did not land. The
  ; reference crash-dumper checks for it and SKIPS the register
  ; (a6xx_gpu_state.c:1765, 1787, 1905) and mesa's reference dump has
  ; whole regions of it; nothing in the vendored sources ever writes it,
  ; so it comes from the hardware. Two UCHE registers returned it on the
  ; M2-X run, at addresses that check out against a6xx.xml. Without this
  ; line the next reader takes it for data. Section 11ac.
  If v = $DEAFBEAD
    Print("   (DEAFBEAD - the hardware's marker for a read that did not land, NOT a value)")
  EndIf
  PrintNl()
  Flush()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Rd32 / Wr32 - the ONLY way this file touches a register.
;
;  PeekN / PokeN, never Peek / Poke. On this target .i and a bare pointer
;  are EIGHT bytes, so a plain Peek at a 32-bit register returns that
;  register glued to its neighbour and looks entirely plausible. The same
;  warning is written in capitals at the top of this board's pin
;  controller and its serial-engine driver, which is where it was
;  learned.
;
;  Both refuse once gStop is latched. That is the teeth behind rule 2:
;  a guard that only prints, while the access happens anyway, is not a
;  guard.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Rd32(addr.i)
  If gStop <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(addr) & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

Procedure Wr32(addr.i, v.i)
  If gStop <> 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  PokeN(addr, v & $FFFFFFFF)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i BitOf(v.i, b.i)
  ProcedureReturn (v >> b) & 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  CfgWr - section 11ab. Write a configuration register, READ IT BACK
;  IMMEDIATELY, print both, and tally the verdict.
;
;  Step 8.2 has made twenty-eight writes on every run since M2-R and
;  printed one line about all of them: "done." That is the same omission
;  this campaign has now corrected three times - the two-bit IOMMU sample
;  (11v), the debug port's address register (11w), the read-only queue's
;  address half (11z) - and it sits on the exact path section 11aa
;  narrowed the failure to. A write with no read-back is a hope.
;
;  The tallies are global so the summary can state them without the
;  reader having to count lines of transcript.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i CfgWr(*name, addr.i, val.i)
  Define got.i
  If gStop <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  Wr32(addr, val)
  got = Rd32(addr)
  gCfgWrites = gCfgWrites + 1
  Print("   ")
  UartWriteStr(*name)
  Print(" wrote ")
  PutHex8(val)
  Print(" reads ")
  PutHex8(got)
  If got = val
    Print("   took")
    gCfgTook = gCfgTook + 1
  ElseIf got = 0
    Print("   !! READ BACK ZERO - the write did not take")
    gCfgZero = gCfgZero + 1
  ElseIf got = $DEAFBEAD
    Print("   !! DEAFBEAD - the read did not land. UNDECIDED: this says")
    Print(" NOTHING about whether the write took. Section 11ac.")
    gCfgOther = gCfgOther + 1
  Else
    Print("   !! NEITHER - undecided, printed as-is")
    gCfgOther = gCfgOther + 1
  EndIf
  PrintNl()
  Flush()
  ProcedureReturn got
EndProcedure

; The twelve 64-bit-addressing switches get their own wrapper so the count
; of how many actually took is kept by the code that reads them back,
; rather than by a caller that could forget one. Eleven of twelve counted
; correctly and one forgotten is exactly how the two-bit IOMMU sample of
; section 11v happened.
Procedure CfgWrAddrMode(*name, addr.i)
  If CfgWr(*name, addr, 1) = 1
    gCfgAddrMode = gCfgAddrMode + 1
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Halt(step, *why) - the loud stop. Prints what was expected, what was
;  found and what the operator should do, then latches so that nothing
;  downstream runs.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure Halt(step.i, *why)
  If gStop <> 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  gStop    = 1
  gStopWhy = step
  PrintNl()
  ; House rule, 2026-09-03: an error is a full sentence that KEEPS the
  ; code, says what the code means, and names the first thing to check.
  ; Never a bare number - a number alone sends the reader to the source.
  ; Here the "code" is the step, and every caller's sentence carries the
  ; register address and the raw value it actually read.
  Print("!! STOPPED at step ")
  PrintDec(step)
  Print(": ")
  UartWriteStr(*why)
  PrintNl()
  PrintN("   Nothing further was touched. This is a refusal, not a crash -")
  PrintN("   the board should come back on its own.")
  PrintNl()
  Flush()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Branch(step, *name, addr) - read one clock branch control register and
;  say what it means. Returns 1 if the branch is running.
;
;  The status bit is INVERTED: bit 31 is CLK_OFF, so 1 means halted.
;  And the hardware-clock-gating bit changes what a halted reading means
;  - the reference driver skips its own halt check entirely when gating
;  is enabled, because the block legitimately parks itself. So a halted
;  reading with gating on is reported as inconclusive rather than as off.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Branch(step.i, *name, addr.i)
  Define v.i
  Define off.i
  Define hwcg.i
  Announce(step, *name, addr)
  v = Rd32(addr)
  Got(*name, v)
  If gStop <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  off  = BitOf(v, #CBCR_CLK_OFF_BIT)
  hwcg = BitOf(v, #CBCR_HWCG_BIT)
  Print("   enable=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(v, #CBCR_ENABLE_BIT))
  Print("  hwcg=")
  PrintDec(hwcg)
  Print("  CLK_OFF=")
  PrintDec(off)
  If off = 0
    PrintN("  -> RUNNING")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  If hwcg = 1
    PrintN("  -> halted, but clock gating is on, so this is INCONCLUSIVE")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  PrintN("  -> HALTED")
  Flush()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 1. The first MMIO access this program makes, and the only one
;  that can be defended before anything has been measured.
;
;  It is a global-clock-controller register. The processor running this
;  code is clocked out of that same controller, and the earlier session
;  that stalled on the serial-engine wrapper never had trouble with the
;  global controller. That is an argument, not a proof, which is why it
;  is announced like everything else.
; ======================================================================
Procedure Step1_GccPath()
  ReadCurrentEl()
  ReadPfr0()
  Print("   ID_AA64PFR0_EL1 = ")
  PutHex16(gPfr0)
  Print("   EL2 field = ")
  PrintDec((gPfr0 >> 8) & 15)
  PrintNl()
  If ((gPfr0 >> 8) & 15) = 0
    PrintN("   EL2 IS NOT IMPLEMENTED on this part. Nothing can be running")
    PrintN("   in it, so no hypervisor is refusing anything - and a")
    PrintN("   hypervisor call would be an undefined instruction. NOT")
    PrintN("   attempting one.")
  Else
    ; ANSWERED 2026-09-04, and never to be asked again on this board.
    ; ID_AA64PFR0_EL1 read 0x1002222 - EL2 field 2, so EL2 IS
    ; implemented - and the hypervisor call then hung the board: nothing
    ; is listening in it. That was the predicted first case and it cost
    ; power cycle 7. The call is compiled out; the finding is kept.
    PrintN("   EL2 EXISTS but NOTHING IS LISTENING in it - measured")
    PrintN("   2026-09-04, and the measurement cost a power cycle. The")
    PrintN("   hypervisor call is compiled out and must never run again.")
    Flush()
  EndIf
  Flush()
  Print("   CurrentEL raw = ")
  PutHex8(gCurEl)
  Print("   -> running at EL")
  PrintDec((gCurEl >> 2) & 3)
  PrintNl()
  PrintN("   (Linux runs at EL1 under a hypervisor at EL2 on this part. If")
  PrintN("    this says EL1 too, then privilege level alone cannot explain")
  PrintN("    why our IOMMU writes vanish and the operating system's do not.)")
  Flush()

  Say("--- S1: the global clock controller, and the path to the GPU ---")
  gVGccAhb = 0
  If Branch(1, "GCC_GPU_CFG_AHB_CLK", #GCC_GPU_CFG_AHB_CBCR) = 1
    gVGccAhb = 1
  EndIf
  ; Read it back raw as well, because the decision below turns on it.
  gVGccAhb = Rd32(#GCC_GPU_CFG_AHB_CBCR)

  Branch(1, "GCC_BIMC_GPU_AXI_CLK", #GCC_BIMC_GPU_AXI_CBCR)
  Branch(1, "GCC_GPU_MEMNOC_GFX_CLK", #GCC_GPU_MEMNOC_GFX_CBCR)
  Branch(1, "GCC_GPU_SNOC_DVM_GFX_CLK", #GCC_GPU_SNOC_DVM_CBCR)

  Announce(1, "GCC APCS vote register", #GCC_APCS_VOTE_79004)
  Got("vote", Rd32(#GCC_APCS_VOTE_79004))
  Print("   GPLL0 tap bit15=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(Rd32(#GCC_APCS_VOTE_79004), 15))
  Print("  div tap bit16=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(Rd32(#GCC_APCS_VOTE_79004), 16))
  PrintNl()

  ; GPLL0 is never programmed by the reference driver - it relies on the
  ; boot firmware entirely. The graphics clock tables assume its main
  ; output is 600 MHz. Read it rather than assume it.
  Announce(1, "GPLL0 mode", #GCC_GPLL0_MODE)
  Got("GPLL0_MODE", Rd32(#GCC_GPLL0_MODE))
  Announce(1, "GPLL0 L value", #GCC_GPLL0_L_VAL)
  Got("GPLL0_L_VAL", Rd32(#GCC_GPLL0_L_VAL))
  Announce(1, "GPLL0 user control", #GCC_GPLL0_USER_CTL)
  Got("GPLL0_USER_CTL", Rd32(#GCC_GPLL0_USER_CTL))
  Flush()
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 2. The gate in front of the graphics clock controller.
;
;  The controller's own registers sit behind the branch read in step 1.
;  If that branch is halted, reading the controller to find out whether
;  the power domain is up IS the hang. This is the step that exists
;  purely to refuse.
; ======================================================================
Procedure Step2_Gate()
  Say("--- S2: may the graphics clock controller be read? ---")
  If gStop <> 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If BitOf(gVGccAhb, #CBCR_CLK_OFF_BIT) = 1
    If BitOf(gVGccAhb, #CBCR_HWCG_BIT) = 0
      Print("   the branch at 01436004 read ")
      PutHex8(gVGccAhb)
      PrintNl()
      Halt(2, "the configuration path to the graphics clock controller is HALTED, so reading that controller would stall the bus and cost a power cycle. Check that the boot firmware left this branch enabled, and that bit 31 (CLK_OFF) really is set rather than bit 1 (hardware gating) making the reading inconclusive.")
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
    PrintN("   the branch reads halted but its gating bit is set, so the")
    PrintN("   reading is inconclusive. Continuing is a JUDGEMENT CALL and")
    PrintN("   it is recorded here as one.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  PrintN("   the configuration path is running. The controller may be read.")
  Flush()
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 3. The graphics clock controller: branches, both power domains,
;  the core-clock generator and the PLL.
; ======================================================================
Procedure Step3_Gpucc()
  Say("--- S3: the graphics clock controller ---")
  If gStop <> 0
    PrintN("   refused: an earlier step stopped.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  gVGpuccAhb = 0
  If Branch(3, "GPU_CC_AHB_CLK", #GPUCC_AHB_CBCR) = 1
    gVGpuccAhb = 1
  EndIf
  Branch(3, "GPU_CC_CXO_CLK", #GPUCC_CXO_CBCR)
  Branch(3, "GPU_CC_GX_CXO_CLK", #GPUCC_GX_CXO_CBCR)
  Branch(3, "GPU_CC_CX_GMU_CLK", #GPUCC_CX_GMU_CBCR)
  Branch(3, "GPU_CC_GX_GFX3D_CLK", #GPUCC_GX_GFX3D_CBCR)

  ; ---- the CX power domain. Status comes from gds_hw_ctrl, NOT from the
  ;      control register's own bit 31. See the note by the constants.
  Say("   CX power domain:")
  Announce(3, "CX GDSC control", #GPUCC_CX_GDSCR)
  gVCxGdscr = Rd32(#GPUCC_CX_GDSCR)
  Got("CX GDSCR", gVCxGdscr)
  Print("   SW_COLLAPSE=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(gVCxGdscr, #GDSCR_SW_COLLAPSE_BIT))
  Print("  HW_CONTROL=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(gVCxGdscr, #GDSCR_HW_CONTROL_BIT))
  Print("  RETAIN_FF=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(gVCxGdscr, #GDSCR_RETAIN_FF_BIT))
  PrintNl()

  Announce(3, "CX GDSC status (gds_hw_ctrl)", #GPUCC_CX_GDS_HW_CTRL)
  gVCxStatus = Rd32(#GPUCC_CX_GDS_HW_CTRL)
  Got("CX status", gVCxStatus)
  Print("   PWR_ON=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(gVCxStatus, #GDSCR_PWR_ON_BIT))
  If BitOf(gVCxStatus, #GDSCR_PWR_ON_BIT) = 1
    PrintN("  -> the CX domain is POWERED")
  Else
    PrintN("  -> the CX domain is COLLAPSED")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  ; ---- the GX power domain. Different register, different bit.
  Say("   GX power domain:")
  Announce(3, "GX GDSC control", #GPUCC_GX_GDSCR)
  gVGxGdscr = Rd32(#GPUCC_GX_GDSCR)
  Got("GX GDSCR", gVGxGdscr)
  Announce(3, "GX GDSC status (CFG_GDSCR)", #GPUCC_GX_CFG_GDSCR)
  gVGxCfg = Rd32(#GPUCC_GX_CFG_GDSCR)
  Got("GX CFG_GDSCR", gVGxCfg)
  Print("   POWER_UP_COMPLETE=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(gVGxCfg, #CFG_GDSCR_PWR_UP_BIT))
  Print("  POWER_DOWN_COMPLETE=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(gVGxCfg, #CFG_GDSCR_PWR_DN_BIT))
  PrintNl()
  Announce(3, "GX clamp control", #GPUCC_GX_CLAMP_IO)
  Got("GX clamp_io", Rd32(#GPUCC_GX_CLAMP_IO))

  ; ---- the core-clock generator. Bit 31 of the command register is
  ;      ROOT_OFF; the configuration register's bits 10:8 are the source
  ;      select and 7:0 are twice the divider minus one.
  Say("   core clock generator:")
  Announce(3, "GFX3D command", #GPUCC_GFX3D_CMD_RCGR)
  Got("CMD_RCGR", Rd32(#GPUCC_GFX3D_CMD_RCGR))
  Print("   ROOT_OFF=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(Rd32(#GPUCC_GFX3D_CMD_RCGR), 31))
  PrintNl()
  Announce(3, "GFX3D configuration", #GPUCC_GFX3D_CFG_RCGR)
  Got("CFG_RCGR", Rd32(#GPUCC_GFX3D_CFG_RCGR))
  Print("   src_sel=")
  PrintDec((Rd32(#GPUCC_GFX3D_CFG_RCGR) >> 8) & 7)
  Print("  div_field=")
  PrintDec(Rd32(#GPUCC_GFX3D_CFG_RCGR) & $FF)
  PrintN("   (source 3 with divider field 3 is the 355.2 MHz point)")
  Flush()

  ; ---- the PLL. Bit 31 of the mode register is the lock detector.
  Say("   PLL:")
  Announce(3, "PLL mode", #GPUCC_PLL0_MODE)
  Got("PLL_MODE", Rd32(#GPUCC_PLL0_MODE))
  Print("   OUTCTRL=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(Rd32(#GPUCC_PLL0_MODE), 0))
  Print("  BYPASSNL=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(Rd32(#GPUCC_PLL0_MODE), 1))
  Print("  RESET_N=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(Rd32(#GPUCC_PLL0_MODE), 2))
  Print("  LOCK_DET=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(Rd32(#GPUCC_PLL0_MODE), 31))
  PrintNl()
  Announce(3, "PLL L value", #GPUCC_PLL0_L_VAL)
  Got("PLL_L_VAL", Rd32(#GPUCC_PLL0_L_VAL))
  PrintN("   (0x25 = 37 is what the reference driver programs: 710.4 MHz)")
  Announce(3, "PLL user control", #GPUCC_PLL0_USER_CTL)
  Got("PLL_USER_CTL", Rd32(#GPUCC_PLL0_USER_CTL))
  Flush()
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 3b. BRING THE CX POWER DOMAIN UP.
;
;  Only compiled when #GPUPROBE_ENABLE is 1, and only ever reached when
;  step 2 said the path to this controller is live - so every register
;  touched here has already been READ successfully in this same run.
;  Nothing is written to an address that has not first answered a read.
;
;  The sequence is gdsc.c:312-319 and :177-189, for a domain whose flags
;  are RETAIN_FF_ENABLE | VOTABLE and whose gds_hw_ctrl register is
;  non-zero:
;
;    1. clear SW_COLLAPSE in the control register
;    2. udelay(1) - MANDATORY. The status bit lies for about eight
;       reference-clock cycles after the write.
;    3. poll PWR_ON in GDS_HW_CTRL - NOT in the control register. This
;       domain reports through the hardware-control register because that
;       register exists; the control register's own bit 31 is the wrong
;       place to look and it will read plausibly.
;    4. udelay(1) before enabling any clock in the domain - the driver
;       comment is that clocks must not be enabled within 400 ns of the
;       memories getting power.
;    5. set RETAIN_FF.
;
;  The driver allows 2000 us. This waits up to 20000 us and prints how
;  long it actually took, because a number is worth more than a verdict
;  the first time anything does this on a part nobody has done it on.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i CxPowerUp()
  Define v.i
  Define waited.i
  Define ok.i

  Say("--- S3b: bringing the CX power domain UP ---")
  If gStop <> 0
    PrintN("   refused: an earlier step stopped.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  v = Rd32(#GPUCC_CX_GDSCR)
  Print("   CX GDSCR before = ")
  PutHex8(v)
  PrintNl()
  If BitOf(gVCxStatus, #GDSCR_PWR_ON_BIT) = 1
    PrintN("   the domain is ALREADY powered. Leaving it exactly as it is -")
    PrintN("   cycling a live domain destroys every register in it.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  Print("S3b about to WRITE ")
  PutHex8(v & $FFFFFFFE)
  Print(" to CX GDSCR at ")
  PutHex8(#GPUCC_CX_GDSCR)
  PrintN("   (clearing SW_COLLAPSE - this is the first write this campaign")
  PrintN("    has ever made to this board's graphics subsystem)")
  Flush()
  Wr32(#GPUCC_CX_GDSCR, v & $FFFFFFFE)

  ; Step 2 of the sequence. QEfiStall is the firmware's own microsecond
  ; wait, so it does not depend on any timer this program has set up.
  QEfiStall(2)

  waited = 0
  ok = 0
  While waited < 20000
    v = Rd32(#GPUCC_CX_GDS_HW_CTRL)
    If BitOf(v, #GDSCR_PWR_ON_BIT) = 1
      ok = 1
      Break
    EndIf
    QEfiStall(50)
    waited = waited + 50
  Wend

  Print("   CX GDS_HW_CTRL now = ")
  PutHex8(v)
  Print("   after ")
  PrintDec(waited)
  PrintN(" us")
  Flush()

  If ok = 0
    Halt(31, "the CX power domain did not come up: bit 31 (PWR_ON) of the status register at 05991540 never set within 20000 us, which is ten times the reference driver's own limit. Check the value printed just above - if it is still the collapsed pattern the write to 0599106C did not take, and if it is all-ones the controller itself is not answering. Nothing downstream was touched.")
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  PrintN("   PWR_ON is SET. The CX domain is UP.")
  QEfiStall(2)
  v = Rd32(#GPUCC_CX_GDSCR)
  Wr32(#GPUCC_CX_GDSCR, v | (1 << #GDSCR_RETAIN_FF_BIT))
  Print("   RETAIN_FF set; CX GDSCR now = ")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPUCC_CX_GDSCR))
  PrintNl()
  Flush()

  ; ---- re-read the branches. This is the evidence that matters: a
  ;      branch whose enable bit was already set but which read HALTED
  ;      should now read RUNNING, because the thing that was missing was
  ;      never the enable bit - it was the power.
  Say("   re-reading the branches now the domain is powered:")
  gVGpuccAhb = 0
  If Branch(31, "GPU_CC_AHB_CLK", #GPUCC_AHB_CBCR) = 1
    gVGpuccAhb = 1
  EndIf
  Branch(31, "GPU_CC_CXO_CLK", #GPUCC_CXO_CBCR)
  Branch(31, "GPU_CC_CX_GMU_CLK", #GPUCC_CX_GMU_CBCR)

  gVCxStatus = Rd32(#GPUCC_CX_GDS_HW_CTRL)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 3c. ENABLE ONE CLOCK BRANCH, AND PROVE IT TICKS.
;
;  Set bit 0, then poll CLK_OFF until it clears. The reference driver
;  allows 200 us; this allows 2000 and prints what it actually took.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i EnableBranch(step.i, *name, addr.i)
  Define v.i
  Define waited.i

  v = Rd32(addr)
  If BitOf(v, #CBCR_CLK_OFF_BIT) = 0
    Print("   ")
    UartWriteStr(*name)
    PrintN(" is already running - left alone")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  Print("S3c about to WRITE enable to ")
  UartWriteStr(*name)
  Print(" at ")
  PutHex8(addr)
  PrintNl()
  Flush()
  Wr32(addr, v | 1)

  waited = 0
  While waited < 2000
    v = Rd32(addr)
    If BitOf(v, #CBCR_CLK_OFF_BIT) = 0
      Print("   ")
      UartWriteStr(*name)
      Print(" = ")
      PutHex8(v)
      Print("  -> RUNNING after ")
      PrintDec(waited)
      PrintN(" us")
      Flush()
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
    QEfiStall(10)
    waited = waited + 10
  Wend

  Print("   ")
  UartWriteStr(*name)
  Print(" = ")
  PutHex8(v)
  PrintN("  -> STILL HALTED after 2000 us")
  Flush()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 3d. BRING THE GX POWER DOMAIN UP.
;
;  WHY THIS EXISTS - the whole lesson of the previous run.
;
;  That run brought the CX domain up and it genuinely worked: two clock
;  branches whose enable bit was ALREADY set went from HALTED to RUNNING
;  the moment the domain powered, which is exactly the evidence that
;  "the missing thing was power, not the enable bit" would produce.
;
;  The gate then allowed the graphics window to be read - and every
;  register in it answered ZERO, and a pattern written to a scratch
;  register did not come back.
;
;  A window that answers but does not store is not a working GPU. It is
;  the signature of logic clocked enough to complete a bus cycle and not
;  powered enough to hold a bit. The blocks being read - RBBM, and the
;  command processor - live in the GX domain, and GX was never touched:
;  its collapse bit was still set and its I/O clamp was still on.
;
;  So CX was necessary and is not sufficient.
;
;  The sequence for a domain with SW_RESET | AON_RESET | CLAMP_IO |
;  POLL_CFG_GDSCR | RETAIN_FF_ENABLE (gdsc.c:293-330,
;  gpucc-qcm2290.c:305-320):
;
;    1. pulse the block reset
;    2. pulse the always-on reset in the clamp register
;    3. release the I/O clamp
;    4. clear SW_COLLAPSE
;    5. poll POWER_UP_COMPLETE - bit 16 of the CFG register, NOT bit 31
;       of the control register. This domain sets POLL_CFG_GDSCR and has
;       no hardware-control register, which is the exact OPPOSITE of how
;       CX reports. Two domains, two registers, two bit positions, and
;       reading the wrong one gives a plausible answer.
;    6. set RETAIN_FF
;
;  ON CLOCK SPEED: the core clock generator is left where the firmware
;  leaves it, parked on the 19.2 MHz reference, and the phase-locked loop
;  is not touched at all. That is DELIBERATE. Every faster operating
;  point needs a higher voltage corner, and the only way to ask for one
;  is a message to a power manager this monitor cannot send. A register
;  write and a read-back do not care how fast the core runs. Speed is
;  milestone 2's problem and the corner question has to be answered
;  honestly then rather than ignored now.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i GxPowerUp()
  Define v.i
  Define waited.i
  Define ok.i

  Say("--- S3d: bringing the GX power domain UP ---")
  If gStop <> 0
    PrintN("   refused: an earlier step stopped.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  v = Rd32(#GPUCC_GX_CFG_GDSCR)
  If BitOf(v, #CFG_GDSCR_PWR_UP_BIT) = 1
    PrintN("   GX is ALREADY powered. Leaving it exactly as it is.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; ---- 1. block reset pulse
  Print("S3d about to PULSE the GX block reset at ")
  PutHex8(#GPUCC_GX_BCR)
  PrintNl()
  Flush()
  Wr32(#GPUCC_GX_BCR, 1)
  QEfiStall(2)
  Wr32(#GPUCC_GX_BCR, 0)
  QEfiStall(2)

  ; ---- 2. always-on reset pulse, bit 4 of the clamp register.
  ;      The reference driver has a DEFECT here: it builds this write
  ;      with a mask helper and ends up writing zero for both the assert
  ;      and the release, so upstream never actually pulses it. The bit
  ;      is written explicitly here. That Linux works without the pulse
  ;      is recorded in the vault plan - if this run misbehaves, this
  ;      line is the first thing to take back out.
  v = Rd32(#GPUCC_GX_CLAMP_IO)
  Print("S3d about to PULSE the GX AON reset at ")
  PutHex8(#GPUCC_GX_CLAMP_IO)
  Print("  (was ")
  PutHex8(v)
  PrintN(")")
  Flush()
  Wr32(#GPUCC_GX_CLAMP_IO, v | (1 << 4))
  QEfiStall(2)
  Wr32(#GPUCC_GX_CLAMP_IO, v & $FFFFFFEF)
  QEfiStall(2)

  ; ---- 3. release the I/O clamp
  v = Rd32(#GPUCC_GX_CLAMP_IO)
  Print("S3d about to RELEASE the GX clamp at ")
  PutHex8(#GPUCC_GX_CLAMP_IO)
  PrintNl()
  Flush()
  Wr32(#GPUCC_GX_CLAMP_IO, v & $FFFFFFFE)
  Print("   clamp_io now = ")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPUCC_GX_CLAMP_IO))
  PrintNl()
  Flush()

  ; ---- 4. clear the collapse bit
  v = Rd32(#GPUCC_GX_GDSCR)
  Print("S3d about to WRITE ")
  PutHex8(v & $FFFFFFFE)
  Print(" to the GX GDSCR at ")
  PutHex8(#GPUCC_GX_GDSCR)
  PrintN("   (clearing SW_COLLAPSE)")
  Flush()
  Wr32(#GPUCC_GX_GDSCR, v & $FFFFFFFE)
  QEfiStall(2)

  ; ---- 5. poll the CFG register, bit 16
  waited = 0
  ok = 0
  While waited < 20000
    v = Rd32(#GPUCC_GX_CFG_GDSCR)
    If BitOf(v, #CFG_GDSCR_PWR_UP_BIT) = 1
      ok = 1
      Break
    EndIf
    QEfiStall(50)
    waited = waited + 50
  Wend

  Print("   GX CFG_GDSCR now = ")
  PutHex8(v)
  Print("   after ")
  PrintDec(waited)
  PrintN(" us")
  Print("   GX GDSCR now = ")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPUCC_GX_GDSCR))
  PrintNl()
  Flush()

  If ok = 0
    Halt(32, "the GX power domain did not come up: bit 16 (POWER_UP_COMPLETE) of the CFG register at 05991010 never set within 20000 us. The graphics window will read zeroes and silently drop every write without this domain, which is the failure that cost run 2. Check that the I/O clamp at 05991508 was released (bit 0 clear) and that the collapse bit at 0599100C was cleared. Nothing downstream was touched.")
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  PrintN("   POWER_UP_COMPLETE is SET. The GX domain is UP.")
  QEfiStall(2)
  v = Rd32(#GPUCC_GX_GDSCR)
  Wr32(#GPUCC_GX_GDSCR, v | (1 << #GDSCR_RETAIN_FF_BIT))
  ; REFRESH THE SNAPSHOT THE SUMMARY PRINTS. Without this the summary
  ; reported the value captured back in step 3, BEFORE the power-up, and
  ; so printed POWER_UP_COMPLETE=0 on a run whose body had just proved it
  ; was 1. A summary that contradicts the evidence above it is a silent
  ; wrong answer, and it is worse than no summary at all - it is the part
  ; a reader in a hurry trusts.
  gVGxCfg = Rd32(#GPUCC_GX_CFG_GDSCR)
  Flush()
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 3e. The six clocks the reference driver enables for this GPU.
;
;  Exactly the clock-names list on the device-tree node, in its order:
;  core, iface, mem_iface, alt_mem_iface, gmu, xo. Two live in the global
;  controller and four in the graphics one.
;
;  Matching the reference rather than enabling a guessed minimum is
;  deliberate. A minimal set that fails costs a whole bench cycle to
;  diagnose, and every one of these is a single bit in a register that
;  has already been proven readable on this board.
; ======================================================================
Procedure Step3e_Clocks()
  Say("--- S3e: enabling the six clocks the reference driver enables ---")
  If gStop <> 0
    PrintN("   refused: an earlier step stopped.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  Print("   core generator before: CMD_RCGR = ")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPUCC_GFX3D_CMD_RCGR))
  Print("  CFG_RCGR = ")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPUCC_GFX3D_CFG_RCGR))
  PrintN("   (left on the 19.2 MHz reference on purpose - see S3d)")
  Flush()

  ; THE CORE CLOCK IS NOT OPTIONAL AND ITS FAILURE MUST STOP THE LADDER.
  ; This was a real gap: the return value was being discarded, so a core
  ; clock that never ticked would have been walked straight past - step 4
  ; only checks the domain and the interface clock, so the run would have
  ; reached the window half-powered and produced exactly run 2's
  ; "answers but does not store" result with no indication why.
  If EnableBranch(3, "GPU_CC_GX_GFX3D_CLK core", #GPUCC_GX_GFX3D_CBCR) = 0
    Halt(33, "the graphics core clock never started: bit 31 (CLK_OFF) of 05991054 was still set 2000 us after its enable bit was written, ten times the reference driver's limit. The command processor cannot sequence without it and the register window will answer without storing. Check that the GX domain came up at step 3d, and check ROOT_OFF in the clock generator at 0599101C - the root only starts when a branch downstream asks for it.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  EnableBranch(3, "GPU_CC_AHB_CLK iface", #GPUCC_AHB_CBCR)
  EnableBranch(3, "GCC_BIMC_GPU_AXI_CLK mem_iface", #GCC_BIMC_GPU_AXI_CBCR)
  EnableBranch(3, "GCC_GPU_MEMNOC_GFX_CLK alt_mem_iface", #GCC_GPU_MEMNOC_GFX_CBCR)
  EnableBranch(3, "GPU_CC_CX_GMU_CLK gmu", #GPUCC_CX_GMU_CBCR)
  EnableBranch(3, "GPU_CC_CXO_CLK xo", #GPUCC_CXO_CBCR)

  Print("   core generator after: CMD_RCGR = ")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPUCC_GFX3D_CMD_RCGR))
  Print("  ROOT_OFF=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(Rd32(#GPUCC_GFX3D_CMD_RCGR), 31))
  PrintNl()
  Flush()
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE SECURE-MONITOR CALL.
;
;  x0 = function id, x1 = arginfo, x2..x5 = args[0..3]; returns in
;  x0..x3. qcom_scm-smc.c:162-212. We never pass more than four
;  arguments, so the overflow buffer that would otherwise go in x5 is not
;  needed and is not built.
;
;  The monitor may clobber x0-x17, so x9 is recomputed after the call
;  rather than reused across it.
; ======================================================================
Procedure DoSmc()
  ASM
    adrp x9, global_gsmc0
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gsmc0
    ldr  x0, [x9]
    adrp x9, global_gsmc1
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gsmc1
    ldr  x1, [x9]
    adrp x9, global_gsmc2
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gsmc2
    ldr  x2, [x9]
    adrp x9, global_gsmc3
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gsmc3
    ldr  x3, [x9]
    adrp x9, global_gsmc4
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gsmc4
    ldr  x4, [x9]
    ; x5 is args[3]. It used to be zeroed, because nothing issued here
    ; needed a fourth argument - the aperture call does, and a zero
    ; where a mask belongs is a silently wrong request rather than a
    ; refusal. x6 stays zeroed: the overflow buffer only appears above
    ; four arguments (qcom_scm-smc.c:181) and nothing here has five.
    adrp x9, global_gsmc5
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gsmc5
    ldr  x5, [x9]
    mov  x6, xzr
    smc  #0
    adrp x9, global_gsmcr0
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gsmcr0
    str  x0, [x9]
    adrp x9, global_gsmcr1
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gsmcr1
    str  x1, [x9]
    adrp x9, global_gsmcr2
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gsmcr2
    str  x2, [x9]
    adrp x9, global_gsmcr3
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gsmcr3
    str  x3, [x9]
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Smc(fn.i, arginfo.i, a0.i, a1.i, a2.i, a3.i)
  gSmc0 = fn
  gSmc1 = arginfo
  gSmc2 = a0
  gSmc3 = a1
  gSmc4 = a2
  gSmc5 = a3
  Print("   smc x0=")
  PutHex8(fn)
  Print(" x1=")
  PutHex8(arginfo)
  Print(" x2=")
  PutHex8(a0)
  Print(" x3=")
  PutAddr(a1)
  Print(" x4=")
  PutHex8(a2)
  Print(" x5=")
  PutHex8(a3)
  PrintNl()
  Flush()
  DoSmc()
  ProcedureReturn gSmcR0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  SmcSay(code) - by house rule an error keeps its code AND says what the
;  code means. These are the secure monitor's own numbers and they are
;  NOT errno; printing them as errno would name the wrong fault.
;  qcom_scm.h:168-176.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure SmcSay(code.i)
  Print("   -> returned ")
  PrintDec(code)
  Print(" (")
  PutHex8(code)
  Print("): ")
  Select code
    Case #SCM_OK
      PrintN("success.")
    Case #SCM_ERROR
      PrintN("generic secure-world failure. Check the image is the one this board is signed for.")
    Case #SCM_EINVAL_ARG
      PrintN("invalid argument. Check the argument encoding in x1 and the peripheral id in x2.")
    Case #SCM_EINVAL_ADDR
      PrintN("invalid address. Check the buffer is physically contiguous, 4 KiB aligned, and that its cache lines were cleaned to the point of coherency.")
    Case #SCM_EOPNOTSUPP
      PrintN("not supported. This monitor does not implement the call - which for the authenticate step means this part does not use a signed graphics image at all.")
    Case #SCM_ENOMEM
      PrintN("out of memory in the secure world.")
    Case #SCM_V2_EBUSY
      PrintN("busy. Another secure-world operation holds the resource.")
    Case #SCM_INTERRUPTED
      PrintN("interrupted before completing; the call would need resuming.")
    Case #SCM_WAITQ_SLEEP
      PrintN("the monitor asked us to sleep and retry; this program does not implement the wait queue.")
    Default
      PrintN("an undocumented code - treat it as a failure and read it against the monitor sources.")
  EndSelect
  Flush()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DcCleanRange(lo, hi) - clean the data cache to the POINT OF COHERENCY.
;
;  NOT the board's CacheFlushRange, and the difference is the whole point
;  of this procedure existing. That one issues `dc cvau`, which cleans to
;  the point of UNIFICATION - correct for making our own instruction
;  fetches see data we just wrote, and NOT a guarantee that another agent
;  on the bus can see it. The secure monitor is another agent, and it may
;  read this memory with different cache attributes or none at all.
;
;  `dc civac` is the one that reaches the point of coherency. Handing the
;  secure world a buffer that is still sitting in our data cache is a
;  silent wrong answer: the call fails with an address or authentication
;  error and every visible symptom points at the ids or the image.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHICH EXCEPTION LEVEL ARE WE AT?
;
;  It decides who owns the IOMMU refusals. Linux runs at EL1 under a
;  Gunyah hypervisor at EL2 on this class of part - the firmware driver
;  says so in as many words, and the device tree reserves 6 MiB at
;  0x45700000 for it. If this image is also at EL1 then "our privilege
;  level cannot write it" cannot be the explanation, because Linux plainly
;  can. CurrentEL holds the level in bits 3:2.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure ReadCurrentEl()
  ASM
    mrs x10, CurrentEL
    adrp x9, global_gcurel
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gcurel
    str  x10, [x9]
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  IS EL2 EVEN IMPLEMENTED, AND IS ANYTHING SITTING IN IT?
;
;  ID_AA64PFR0_EL1 bits 11:8 are the EL2 field: 0 means EL2 is NOT
;  IMPLEMENTED on this part, non-zero means it exists. Readable from EL1.
;
;  This matters because CurrentEL = EL1 does not distinguish "running
;  under a hypervisor" from "EL2 exists and nobody is using it" from
;  "EL2 is not implemented at all", and those three have completely
;  different consequences for who is refusing our IOMMU writes.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure ReadPfr0()
  ASM
    mrs x10, ID_AA64PFR0_EL1
    adrp x9, global_gpfr0
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gpfr0
    str  x10, [x9]
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  A hypervisor-call version query. GATED, and deliberately so.
;
;  From EL1, HVC traps to EL2. If EL2 is not implemented the instruction
;  is UNDEFINED and takes an exception this program has no handler for -
;  which on this board means a hang and a power cycle. So it is only
;  ever issued when the identification register above says EL2 exists,
;  and never on the strength of a guess.
;
;  x0 = 0x80000000 is the SMCCC version query - the most harmless thing
;  there is to ask, and the standard way to find out whether anyone is
;  listening at all.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure TryHvc()
  ASM
    movz x0, #0
    movk x0, #32768, lsl #16
    hvc  #0
    adrp x9, global_ghvcret
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_ghvcret
    str  x0, [x9]
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  CAN WE ACTUALLY WRITE THAT PAGE?
;
;  Every round of this investigation has assumed the IOMMU is REFUSING
;  our writes. A mapping that does not permit the write produces exactly
;  the same symptom - reads fine, writes vanish - and has never been
;  ruled out. Until it is, the last several conclusions rest on an
;  untested assumption.
;
;  AT asks the hardware to walk the translation tables for a WRITE to a
;  given address and report the outcome, WITHOUT performing the access.
;  PAR_EL1 then holds:
;     bit 0   F   1 = the translation FAULTED - the write would not land
;     bits 63:56  the memory attributes, when F is 0
;     bits 51:12  the physical address it resolved to
;
;  The assembler had no such instruction until this campaign added it
;  (forum 627); PAR_EL1 was already known.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DoAtWrite()
  ASM
    adrp x9, global_gataddr
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gataddr
    ldr  x9, [x9]
    at   s1e1w, x9
    isb
    mrs  x10, PAR_EL1
    adrp x9, global_gpar
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gpar
    str  x10, [x9]
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure ReadSctlr()
  ASM
    mrs x10, SCTLR_EL1
    adrp x9, global_gsctlr
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gsctlr
    str  x10, [x9]
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; AtWrite(addr, *what) - report whether a WRITE to addr is permitted.
Procedure AtWrite(addr.i, *what)
  gAtAddr = addr
  DoAtWrite()
  Print("   ")
  UartWriteStr(*what)
  Print(" at ")
  PutHex8(addr)
  Print("  PAR_EL1 = ")
  PutHex16(gPar)
  PrintNl()
  If (gPar & 1) = 1
    Print("      -> FAULT. A write here would NOT reach the device.")
    PrintNl()
  Else
    Print("      -> permitted. resolves to ")
    PutAddr(gPar & $000FFFFFFFFFF000)
    Print("   attrs = ")
    PutHex2((gPar >> 56) & $FF)
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  Flush()
EndProcedure

Procedure DcCleanLine()
  ASM
    adrp x9, global_gflusha
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gflusha
    ldr  x9, [x9]
    dc civac, x9
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure DcCleanRange(lo.i, hi.i)
  Define a.i
  If hi < lo
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  ; round DOWN with a shift, never with a 32-bit mask - this board has
  ; DRAM above 4 GB and a mask would clean lines four gigabytes away.
  a = (lo >> 6) << 6
  While a <= hi
    gFlushA = a
    DcCleanLine()
    a = a + 64
  Wend
  ASM
    dsb sy
    isb
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 6. THE SIGNED IMAGE, AND THE SECURE-MODE UNLOCK.
;
;  Milestone 1c. The sequence is section 9 of the vault note, which was
;  written and reviewed before a line of this existed:
;
;    1. probe that the monitor answers at all
;    2. read the signed image off the boot partition and re-check its
;       shape against what was parsed offline
;    3. build the metadata blob: the ELF header and program headers,
;       followed by the hash segment
;    4. authenticate the metadata          (init image)
;    5. tell the monitor where the image goes  (mem setup)
;    6. copy the one loadable segment there
;    7. authenticate and release               (auth and reset)
;    8. and only then, the secure-mode register
;
;  Any non-zero return stops the chain. There is no retry: a retry on a
;  secure-world call with a bad argument just asks the same wrong
;  question twice, and a retry loop is where a code stops being printed.
; ======================================================================
Procedure Step6_Zap()
  Define n.i
  Define i.i
  Define rc.i
  Define src.i
  Define dst.i
  Define e_ph.i

  Say("--- S6: the signed image and the secure-mode unlock ---")
  gZapOk = 0
  gZapStage = 0

  If gStop <> 0
    PrintN("   refused: an earlier step stopped.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If gVScratchBack <> #SCRATCH_PATTERN
    PrintN("   refused: the graphics window did not store a value, so it")
    PrintN("   is not powered and nothing here would mean anything.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  ; ---- 1. does the monitor answer at all? ---------------------------
  Say("   S6.1 asking whether the secure monitor answers:")
  gZapStage = 1
  rc = Smc(#SMC_IS_CALL_AVAIL, $01, $02000601, 0, 0, 0)
  Print("   -> x0=")
  PutHex8(rc)
  Print("  x1=")
  PutHex8(gSmcR1)
  PrintNl()
  If rc <> 0
    SmcSay(rc)
    Halt(61, "the secure monitor did not accept the availability probe, so nothing further should be asked of it. Check that this build is issuing a FAST call (bit 31 set) for this one - the authenticate calls are yielding and the probe is not.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If gSmcR1 <> 1
    PrintN("   the monitor answered but reports the authenticate call as")
    PrintN("   NOT available. That is itself the finding.")
    Halt(61, "the secure monitor does not implement the image-authentication call on this board. If that is true the graphics block is either already unlocked or cannot be unlocked this way, and the plan needs revisiting rather than retrying.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  PrintN("   the monitor answers and the call is available.")
  Flush()

  ; ---- 2. read the image and re-check its shape ---------------------
  Say("   S6.2 reading the signed image off the boot partition:")
  gZapStage = 2
  n = QEspRead("\EFI\anvil\a702_zap.mbn", @gZap[0], 16384)
  Print("   read ")
  PrintDec(n)
  PrintN(" bytes")
  Flush()
  If n <> #ZAP_FILE_LEN
    Halt(62, "the signed image is not the length this program was written against. Check that a702_zap.mbn was staged into the anvil directory on the boot partition, and that it is the copy from the repository - the length is checked because a short read and a different build look identical once the bytes are in memory.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If PeekB(@gZap[0]) <> $7F Or PeekB(@gZap[1]) <> 69 Or PeekB(@gZap[2]) <> 76 Or PeekB(@gZap[3]) <> 70
    Halt(62, "the staged file is not an ELF image - its first four bytes are not the ELF magic. Check what was copied onto the boot partition.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  e_ph = PeekN(@gZap[0] + 28) & $FFFFFFFF
  Print("   ELF ok, e_phoff = ")
  PrintDec(e_ph)
  Print(", segment at file offset ")
  PutHex8(#ZAP_SEG_OFF)
  Print(" length ")
  PrintDec(#ZAP_SEG_LEN)
  PrintNl()
  Flush()

  ; ---- 3. build the metadata blob, 4 KiB aligned --------------------
  Say("   S6.3 building the metadata blob:")
  gZapStage = 3
  gMetaPhys = (@gMeta[0] + $FFF) & (-4096)
  Print("   metadata at ")
  PutAddr(gMetaPhys)
  Print("  length ")
  PrintDec(#ZAP_MD_TOTAL)
  PrintN(" (148 header + 6712 hash)")
  Flush()
  i = 0
  While i < #ZAP_MD_HDR_LEN
    PokeB(gMetaPhys + i, PeekB(@gZap[0] + i) & $FF)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  i = 0
  While i < #ZAP_MD_HASH_LEN
    PokeB(gMetaPhys + #ZAP_MD_HDR_LEN + i, PeekB(@gZap[0] + #ZAP_MD_HASH_OFF + i) & $FF)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  DcCleanRange(gMetaPhys, gMetaPhys + #ZAP_MD_TOTAL + 64)
  PrintN("   built and cleaned to the point of coherency.")
  Flush()

  ; ---- 4. authenticate the metadata ---------------------------------
  Say("   S6.4 authenticate the metadata (init image):")
  gZapStage = 4
  rc = Smc(#SMC_PAS_INIT_IMAGE, #ARGINFO_INIT_IMAGE, #PAS_ID_GPU, gMetaPhys, 0, 0)
  SmcSay(rc)
  If rc <> #SCM_OK
    Halt(64, "the secure world refused to accept the image metadata. THIS IS A RESULT, not a bug to work around - the code above says which refusal it is. Do not retry it and do not touch the secure-mode register.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  ; ---- 5. tell the monitor where the image goes ---------------------
  Say("   S6.5 declare the region (mem setup):")
  gZapStage = 5
  rc = Smc(#SMC_PAS_MEM_SETUP, #ARGINFO_MEM_SETUP, #PAS_ID_GPU, #PIL_GPU_BASE, #ZAP_REGION_LEN, 0)
  SmcSay(rc)
  If rc <> #SCM_OK
    Halt(65, "the secure world refused the memory region. Check that the base is the carve-out the device tree reserves for the graphics image and that the length does not exceed it.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  ; ---- 6. copy the loadable segment ---------------------------------
  Say("   S6.6 copying the loadable segment into the carve-out:")
  gZapStage = 6
  src = @gZap[0] + #ZAP_SEG_OFF
  dst = #PIL_GPU_BASE
  Print("S6 about to WRITE ")
  PrintDec(#ZAP_SEG_LEN)
  Print(" bytes to ")
  PutAddr(dst)
  PrintN("   (the carve-out is marked no-map in the device tree; if the")
  PrintN("    transcript ends here it is not mapped under this firmware)")
  Flush()
  i = 0
  While i < #ZAP_SEG_LEN
    PokeB(dst + i, PeekB(src + i) & $FF)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  DcCleanRange(dst, dst + #ZAP_SEG_LEN + 64)
  PrintN("   copied and cleaned.")
  Flush()

  ; ---- 7. authenticate and release ----------------------------------
  Say("   S6.7 authenticate and release (auth and reset):")
  gZapStage = 7
  rc = Smc(#SMC_PAS_AUTH_RESET, #ARGINFO_AUTH_RESET, #PAS_ID_GPU, 0, 0, 0)
  SmcSay(rc)
  If rc <> #SCM_OK
    Halt(67, "the secure world refused to authenticate the image. THIS IS THE MILESTONE RESULT if it happens - the code above names the refusal. The secure-mode register is NOT to be written after this.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  gZapOk = 1
  PrintN("   THE IMAGE AUTHENTICATED. The secure world has accepted it.")
  Flush()

  ; ---- 8. READ the secure-mode register. DO NOT WRITE IT. ----------
  ;
  ;  THERE IS NO WRITE HERE ANY MORE, AND THERE MUST NEVER BE ONE AGAIN.
  ;  The code is deleted rather than switched off, because switching it
  ;  off is exactly what failed:
  ;
  ;  A constant, #GPUPROBE_SECVID, was added to compile the direct write
  ;  out, and the note above it says "PERMANENTLY 0, THE REGISTER IS
  ;  NEVER WRITTEN". That guard was real and it worked - for the ONE
  ;  write site that existed when it was written. THIS site was added
  ;  afterwards, during the secure-world work, and never went under it.
  ;  So the program went on issuing the write, hung the board twice more,
  ;  and the write-up said the write had been removed.
  ;
  ;  The lesson is not "add the guard here too". It is that "compiled
  ;  out" was ASSERTED and never VERIFIED. The constant is not the proof.
  ;  The assembled listing is: build with -S and check that the address
  ;  0593D000 is never the target of a store. tools/check_no_secvid.sh
  ;  does exactly that and is part of the build now.
  ;
  ;  WHY the write can never work here: on a board with a signed image -
  ;  and this one has one - the transition out of secure mode is done by
  ;  the COMMAND PROCESSOR, executing a CP_SET_SECURE_MODE packet. The
  ;  reference driver only ever writes this register on boards with NO
  ;  signed image (a6xx_gpu.c:1486-1515), under a comment predicting the
  ;  permissions violation we hit. Reading is fine and is kept.
  Say("   S6.8 the secure-mode register - READ ONLY:")
  gZapStage = 8
  gVSecvid = Rd32(#GPU_RBBM_SECVID_TRUST_CNTL)
  Print("   SECVID_TRUST_CNTL = ")
  PutHex8(gVSecvid)
  PrintNl()
  gSecvidWritable = 0
  PrintN("   Not written, and no build of this program can write it. Secure")
  PrintN("   mode is left for the command processor to exit in milestone 2.")
  Flush()
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  MILESTONE 2 - the command processor.
;
;  Packet encoding. adreno_gpu.h:713-733 and freedreno_pm4.h:54-95.
;  A type-7 header carries the opcode, the dword count that FOLLOWS it,
;  and an odd-parity bit over each - the hardware checks both, so a
;  mis-built header is rejected rather than misexecuted.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i Pm4Parity(v.i)
  Define x.i
  x = v
  x = x ! (x >> 4)
  x = x ! (x >> 8)
  x = x ! (x >> 12)
  x = x ! (x >> 16)
  x = x ! (x >> 20)
  x = x ! (x >> 24)
  x = x ! (x >> 28)
  ProcedureReturn ($9669 >> (x & 15)) & 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Pkt7(opcode.i, cnt.i)
  ProcedureReturn $70000000 | (cnt & $3FFF) | (Pm4Parity(cnt) << 15) | ((opcode & $7F) << 16) | (Pm4Parity(opcode) << 23)
EndProcedure

; RingPut - append one dword. The ring is ours alone and this run never
; wraps (it writes well under a hundred dwords into eight thousand), so
; the wrap case is a loud refusal rather than untested arithmetic.
Procedure RingPut(v.i)
  If gRingPos >= #RING_DWORDS
    Halt(80, "the ring buffer wrapped. This program is not written to wrap and the arithmetic that would do it has never been executed, so it refuses rather than guessing.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  PokeN(gRingBase + gRingPos * 4, v & $FFFFFFFF)
  gRingPos = gRingPos + 1
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 7. THE GPU IOMMU - read it, and refuse if it would fault us.
;
;  Everything the command processor fetches - the ring, the microcode,
;  the buffer it writes a marker into - goes through this block. If it is
;  translating and there are no page tables, the very first fetch faults
;  and the only symptom is a processor that never advances its read
;  pointer, which looks exactly like microcode that did not start.
;
;  So it is read before anything is set up, and a state that would fault
;  is a refusal rather than a puzzle later. The four questions are the
;  ones in section 10 of the vault note.
;
;  Its clock is in the graphics domain and was halted every time we
;  looked, so it is enabled first - and reading this block before doing
;  that is the stall that this whole campaign exists to avoid.
; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================
;  SmmuAuxRead - THE READ-ONLY AUXILIARY SURVEY (section 11t).
;
;  Six registers of the IOMMU's global page that this campaign has never
;  looked at, plus a re-read of the three it has. NOTHING HERE WRITES.
;  It is called twice from step 7 - once before the step attempts any
;  write, once after - so the transcript says whether Anvil's own write
;  sequence moves any of them.
;
;  The registers, and why each is in the list:
;
;    ID7    the MMU-500 revision. arm_mmu500_reset() only clears
;           CACHE_LOCK when MAJOR >= 2, so the revision decides whether
;           the reference driver would even try.
;    sACR   CACHE_LOCK (bit 26) is the one documented mechanism on this
;           IP by which a write is silently discarded, and the driver's
;           comment says the deciding copy is the SECURE one, which the
;           non-secure side cannot clear. Bits 8 and 10 are the two
;           bypass-TLB enables the reference driver sets in the same
;           write.
;    sGFSR  the global fault status. If any of our discarded writes
;           actually raised a global fault rather than vanishing, it is
;           recorded here - and nothing in this campaign has ever looked.
;           It is WRITE-1-TO-CLEAR, which is exactly why it is only read:
;           writing what we just read back would clear the evidence.
;    sCR0   re-read, so the before/after pair is on one screen.
;    SMR0   re-read, same reason.
;    the +0x400 window - sCR0, sACR and sGFSR at their Calxeda
;           non-secure alias offsets (arm-smmu-impl.c:13-25). If this
;           integration provides that alias, the values there will track
;           the secure ones; if it does not, they are something else or
;           nothing.
;
;  The caller must have proven all three IOMMU clocks first. Reading this
;  block unclocked stalls the bus, which is the founding rule of this
;  campaign, so the procedure refuses rather than trusting the caller.
; ======================================================================
Procedure SmmuAuxRead(*when, phase.i)
  Define v.i
  Define major.i

  PrintNl()
  Print("--- sACR survey, ")
  UartWriteStr(*when)
  PrintN(" Anvil's write sequence (READS ONLY) ---")
  Flush()

  If gSmmuClocked <> 1
    PrintN("   refused: the three IOMMU clocks have not been proven running.")
    PrintN("   Reading this block unclocked stalls the bus and costs a power")
    PrintN("   cycle, so nothing was touched.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If gStop <> 0
    PrintN("   refused: an earlier step stopped.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  Announce(7, "SMMU_ID7 (MMU-500 revision)", #SMMU_ID7)
  v = Rd32(#SMMU_ID7)
  Got("ID7", v)
  gId7 = v
  major = (v >> 4) & $F
  Print("   MAJOR = ")
  PrintDec(major)
  Print("   MINOR = ")
  PrintDec(v & $F)
  If major >= 2
    PrintN("  -> r2p0 or later, so the reference driver WOULD clear CACHE_LOCK")
  Else
    PrintN("  -> earlier than r2p0, so the reference driver leaves CACHE_LOCK")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  Announce(7, "SMMU_sACR (auxiliary configuration)", #SMMU_SACR)
  v = Rd32(#SMMU_SACR)
  Got("sACR", v)
  If phase = 0
    gSacrBefore = v
  Else
    gSacrAfter = v
  EndIf
  Print("   CACHE_LOCK(26)=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(v, #SACR_CACHE_LOCK_BIT))
  Print("  S2CRB_TLBEN(10)=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(v, #SACR_S2CRB_TLBEN_BIT))
  Print("  SMTNMB_TLBEN(8)=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(v, #SACR_SMTNMB_TLBEN_BIT))
  PrintNl()
  If BitOf(v, #SACR_CACHE_LOCK_BIT) = 1
    PrintN("   CACHE_LOCK IS SET. Context-bank ACTLR writes will not stick")
    PrintN("   while it is, and the driver's own comment says the copy that")
    PrintN("   decides belongs to the secure world. That is a fact about who")
    PrintN("   holds this block. It is NOT an explanation of the stream-table")
    PrintN("   refusals - those writes happen before impl->reset() in the")
    PrintN("   reference driver and stick anyway.")
  Else
    PrintN("   CACHE_LOCK is CLEAR, so the one documented silent-discard")
    PrintN("   mechanism on this IP is not armed. Whatever is refusing our")
    PrintN("   writes, it is not this bit.")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  Announce(7, "SMMU_sGFSR (global fault status)", #SMMU_SGFSR)
  v = Rd32(#SMMU_SGFSR)
  Got("sGFSR", v)
  If phase = 0
    gSgfsrBefore = v
  Else
    gSgfsrAfter = v
  EndIf
  Print("   USF(1)=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(v, 1))
  PrintN("   (read only - this register is write-1-to-clear and clearing it")
  PrintN("    would destroy the only record of a fault our writes caused)")
  If v = 0
    PrintN("   no global fault is recorded, so the refused writes were")
    PrintN("   DISCARDED rather than faulted.")
  Else
    PrintN("   A GLOBAL FAULT IS RECORDED. The writes were not silently")
    PrintN("   dropped after all - the block reacted to them.")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  Announce(7, "SMMU_sCR0 (re-read alongside sACR)", #SMMU_SCR0)
  Got("sCR0", Rd32(#SMMU_SCR0))
  Announce(7, "SMMU_SMR0 (re-read alongside sACR)", #SMMU_SMR0)
  Got("SMR0", Rd32(#SMMU_SMR0))
  Flush()

  PrintN("   the +0x400 window, at the offsets arm_smmu_gr0_ns uses:")
  Announce(7, "sCR0 at +0x400", #SMMU_NSA_SCR0)
  Got("[0x400] sCR0_NS?", Rd32(#SMMU_NSA_SCR0))
  Announce(7, "sACR at +0x410", #SMMU_NSA_SACR)
  Got("[0x410] sACR_NS?", Rd32(#SMMU_NSA_SACR))
  Announce(7, "sGFSR at +0x448", #SMMU_NSA_SGFSR)
  Got("[0x448] sGFSR_NS?", Rd32(#SMMU_NSA_SGFSR))
  PrintN("   (a mirror of the three secure values means this integration")
  PrintN("    provides the alias; anything else means it does not.)")
  Flush()
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 7r. arm_smmu_device_reset() REPLICATED, IN ITS OWN ORDER.
;
;  Section 11u. This is the last "a state the block must be in for
;  writes to be accepted" variant that has never been tried, and it is
;  the one the sources actually describe. Every previous write to this
;  block was an ad-hoc single register - set CLIENTPD, write one SMR.
;  Linux never does that. It performs a fixed sequence, in a fixed
;  order, and every write in it sticks.
;
;  THE ORDER IS THE POINT. Copied from arm-smmu.c:1656-1717, step by
;  step, with nothing reordered and nothing left out:
;
;    1. read sGFSR, write it back        - write-1-to-clear, FIRST write
;    2. for every mapping group: write S2CR then SMR (arm_smmu_write_sme
;       is write_s2cr THEN write_smr, and the order matters here)
;    3. for every context bank: SCTLR = 0, then FSR = CB_FSR_FAULT
;    4. TLBIALLH, then TLBIALLNSNH, both with the dummy value
;    5. read sCR0 and compute the new value - do NOT write it yet
;    6. impl->reset() = arm_mmu500_reset(): ID7, sACR, then the CPRE
;       loop over every bank's ACTLR
;    7. global TLB sync - write sTLBGSYNC, poll sTLBGSTATUS
;    8. THEN write sCR0
;
;  WHAT MAKES THIS A GOOD MEASUREMENT, and it is better than anything
;  tried before, for two independent reasons:
;
;  sACR. Firmware leaves 0x0000001C. Linux leaves 0x0000051C, measured
;  on this board 2026-09-04 from Linux with the GPU awake. The delta is
;  exactly the two bits this sequence ORs in. So the sACR write has ONE
;  expected value, it is known, and it involves no undefined register:
;  0x0000051C means our write stuck, 0x0000001C means it did not. There
;  is nothing to interpret.
;
;  S2CR. Every S2CR here reads UNDEFINED before it is written - junk,
;  and junk under Linux too for the groups Linux has not written. The
;  reset value is the constant 0x00020000. A junk register that reads
;  back a specific constant after a write is the cleanest writability
;  test available anywhere in this block, and it cannot be faked by a
;  stale-bus read the way a same-address read-back can.
;
;  And sCR0 has an expected value too: 0x002A1C06, which is byte for
;  byte what Linux leaves. If our replication produces that, the
;  sequence was the missing ingredient all along.
;
;  RISK. Every address is inside the aperture this block's own ID
;  registers describe - four mapping groups, five context banks - in a
;  page proven writable-mapped by the translation test of 11q, with all
;  three clocks proven running by the ladder above. The one genuinely
;  new instruction shape is the global TLB sync, and its poll is
;  bounded: Linux only warns on timeout and so does this.
; ======================================================================
Procedure Step7r_LinuxReset(nsmr.i, ncb.i, id2.i)
  Define i.i
  Define v.i
  Define reg.i
  Define major.i
  Define sacr.i
  Define waited.i
  Define stuck.i

  PrintNl()
  Say("--- S7r: arm_smmu_device_reset() replicated, in the source's order ---")
  If gSmmuClocked <> 1
    PrintN("   refused: the three IOMMU clocks have not been proven running.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If gStop <> 0
    PrintN("   refused: an earlier step stopped.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If nsmr < 1 Or nsmr > 16 Or ncb < 1 Or ncb > 16
    Print("   refused: the block reports ")
    PrintDec(nsmr)
    Print(" mapping groups and ")
    PrintDec(ncb)
    PrintN(" context banks, which is outside the range this")
    PrintN("   sequence was written for (1 to 16 of each). Walking a count")
    PrintN("   read out of a register that might be junk is how a probe ends")
    PrintN("   up writing to addresses nobody chose. Nothing was touched.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  gLrRan = 1

  ; ---- 1. clear the global fault status. THE FIRST WRITE. ------------
  Say("   step 1 of 8: read sGFSR and write it back (write-1-to-clear)")
  v = Rd32(#SMMU_SGFSR)
  Print("S7r about to WRITE ")
  PutHex8(v)
  Print(" to sGFSR at ")
  PutHex8(#SMMU_SGFSR)
  PrintN("   (this is the first write the reference driver makes to this block)")
  Flush()
  Wr32(#SMMU_SGFSR, v)
  Got("sGFSR after", Rd32(#SMMU_SGFSR))

  ; ---- 2. every mapping group: S2CR then SMR -------------------------
  Say("   step 2 of 8: reset every stream-mapping group, S2CR then SMR")
  PrintN("   S2CR reset value 00020000 = type FAULT, bank 0. That is")
  PrintN("   s2cr_init_val with the disable-bypass build option this board")
  PrintN("   is configured with, and it is exactly what the group Linux")
  PrintN("   never attaches reads back as.")
  Flush()
  For i = 0 To nsmr - 1
    Print("S7r about to WRITE 00020000 to S2CR[")
    PrintDec(i)
    Print("] at ")
    PutHex8(#SMMU_S2CR0 + i * 4)
    PrintNl()
    Flush()
    Wr32(#SMMU_S2CR0 + i * 4, #SMMU_S2CR_RESET)
    Print("S7r about to WRITE 00000000 to SMR[")
    PrintDec(i)
    Print("] at ")
    PutHex8(#SMMU_SMR0 + i * 4)
    PrintNl()
    Flush()
    Wr32(#SMMU_SMR0 + i * 4, 0)
  Next

  ; ---- 3. every context bank: SCTLR = 0, then FSR = FAULT ------------
  Say("   step 3 of 8: disable every context bank and clear its fault status")
  For i = 0 To ncb - 1
    Print("S7r about to WRITE 0 to CB")
    PrintDec(i)
    Print("_SCTLR at ")
    PutHex8(#SMMU_CB0 + i * #SMMU_CB_STRIDE)
    PrintNl()
    Flush()
    Wr32(#SMMU_CB0 + i * #SMMU_CB_STRIDE, 0)
    Print("S7r about to WRITE C00001FE to CB")
    PrintDec(i)
    Print("_FSR at ")
    PutHex8(#SMMU_CB0 + i * #SMMU_CB_STRIDE + #SMMU_CB_FSR)
    PrintNl()
    Flush()
    Wr32(#SMMU_CB0 + i * #SMMU_CB_STRIDE + #SMMU_CB_FSR, #SMMU_CB_FSR_FAULT)
  Next

  ; ---- 4. invalidate the TLB -----------------------------------------
  Say("   step 4 of 8: invalidate the TLB")
  Print("S7r about to WRITE FFFFFFFF to TLBIALLH at ")
  PutHex8(#SMMU_TLBIALLH)
  PrintNl()
  Flush()
  Wr32(#SMMU_TLBIALLH, #SMMU_DUMMY_VAL)
  Print("S7r about to WRITE FFFFFFFF to TLBIALLNSNH at ")
  PutHex8(#SMMU_TLBIALLNSNH)
  PrintNl()
  Flush()
  Wr32(#SMMU_TLBIALLNSNH, #SMMU_DUMMY_VAL)

  ; ---- 5. compute the new sCR0. NOT written until step 8. ------------
  Say("   step 5 of 8: compute the new sCR0 (the write is step 8, as in the source)")
  reg = Rd32(#SMMU_SCR0)
  Got("sCR0 as found", reg)
  gLrScr0Found = reg
  reg = reg | $02 | $04 | $10 | $20      ; GFRE GFIE GCFGFRE GCFGFIE
  reg = reg | $800 | $1000               ; VMIDPNE PTM
  reg = reg & ($FFFFFFFF - $01)          ; clear CLIENTPD
  reg = reg | $400                       ; USFCFG - this build has disable_bypass
  reg = reg & ($FFFFFFFF - $2000)        ; clear FB
  reg = reg & ($FFFFFFFF - $C000)        ; clear BSU
  If BitOf(id2, 15) = 1
    reg = reg | $80000000                ; VMID16EN
    PrintN("   ID2 says 16-bit VMIDs, so VMID16EN is set, as the driver does")
  EndIf
  ; EXIDENABLE is deliberately absent: ID0 bit 8 (EXIDS) reads 0 on this
  ; part, so the reference driver would not set it either.
  gLrScr0Want = reg
  Print("   computed sCR0 = ")
  PutHex8(reg)
  PrintN("   (Linux leaves 002A1C06 on this board - measured 2026-09-04)")
  Flush()

  ; ---- 6. impl->reset() = arm_mmu500_reset() -------------------------
  Say("   step 6 of 8: the MMU-500 implementation reset")
  v = Rd32(#SMMU_ID7)
  major = (v >> 4) & $F
  Got("ID7", v)
  sacr = Rd32(#SMMU_SACR)
  Got("sACR as found", sacr)
  If major >= 2
    sacr = sacr & ($FFFFFFFF - (1 << #SACR_CACHE_LOCK_BIT))
    PrintN("   MAJOR >= 2, so CACHE_LOCK is cleared in the value, as the driver does")
  EndIf
  sacr = sacr | #SACR_TLBEN_BOTH
  gLrSacrWant = sacr
  Print("S7r about to WRITE ")
  PutHex8(sacr)
  Print(" to sACR at ")
  PutHex8(#SMMU_SACR)
  PrintN("   (THE SHARPEST TEST IN THIS RUN: Linux leaves 0000051C here and")
  PrintN("    firmware leaves 0000001C, so the read-back has one right answer)")
  Flush()
  Wr32(#SMMU_SACR, sacr)
  gLrSacrGot = Rd32(#SMMU_SACR)
  Got("sACR read back", gLrSacrGot)
  If gLrSacrGot = gLrSacrWant
    PrintN("   *** THE sACR WRITE STUCK. This is the first write this campaign")
    PrintN("   *** has ever made to the IOMMU global space that took.")
  Else
    PrintN("   the sACR write did not take, exactly as every other write to")
    PrintN("   this page has not taken.")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  PrintN("   and the prefetcher-disable loop over every bank's ACTLR:")
  Flush()
  For i = 0 To ncb - 1
    v = Rd32(#SMMU_CB0 + i * #SMMU_CB_STRIDE + #SMMU_CB_ACTLR)
    Print("   CB")
    PrintDec(i)
    Print("_ACTLR was ")
    PutHex8(v)
    v = v & ($FFFFFFFF - $02)            ; clear CPRE
    Flush()
    Wr32(#SMMU_CB0 + i * #SMMU_CB_STRIDE + #SMMU_CB_ACTLR, v)
    Print("  wrote ")
    PutHex8(v)
    Print("  reads ")
    PutHex8(Rd32(#SMMU_CB0 + i * #SMMU_CB_STRIDE + #SMMU_CB_ACTLR))
    PrintNl()
    Flush()
  Next
  PrintN("   (Linux leaves CB0_ACTLR = 00000301 with CPRE clear on this board)")
  Flush()

  ; ---- 7. the global TLB sync ----------------------------------------
  Say("   step 7 of 8: the global TLB sync")
  Print("S7r about to WRITE FFFFFFFF to sTLBGSYNC at ")
  PutHex8(#SMMU_STLBGSYNC)
  PrintN("   (then poll sTLBGSTATUS bit 0 until it clears, bounded)")
  Flush()
  Wr32(#SMMU_STLBGSYNC, #SMMU_DUMMY_VAL)
  waited = 0
  While waited < 200000
    If BitOf(Rd32(#SMMU_STLBGSTATUS), 0) = 0
      Break
    EndIf
    QEfiStall(100)
    waited = waited + 100
  Wend
  Print("   sTLBGSTATUS = ")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#SMMU_STLBGSTATUS))
  Print("   after ")
  PrintDec(waited)
  PrintN(" us")
  If BitOf(Rd32(#SMMU_STLBGSTATUS), 0) = 1
    gLrSyncTimedOut = 1
    PrintN("   THE GLOBAL TLB SYNC NEVER WENT IDLE after 200000 us: bit 0 of")
    PrintN("   sTLBGSTATUS at 059A0074 is still set. The reference driver only")
    PrintN("   warns here and carries on, so this does too - but on a block")
    PrintN("   that discards our writes it may simply mean the sync was")
    PrintN("   discarded as well and nothing was ever started.")
  Else
    PrintN("   the sync completed.")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  ; ---- 8. and now sCR0 -----------------------------------------------
  Say("   step 8 of 8: write sCR0")
  Print("S7r about to WRITE ")
  PutHex8(gLrScr0Want)
  Print(" to sCR0 at ")
  PutHex8(#SMMU_SCR0)
  PrintNl()
  Flush()
  Wr32(#SMMU_SCR0, gLrScr0Want)
  gLrScr0Got = Rd32(#SMMU_SCR0)
  Got("sCR0 read back", gLrScr0Got)

  ; ---- the verdict ----------------------------------------------------
  PrintNl()
  Say("   S7r VERDICT - what stuck and what did not:")
  Print("   sACR  wanted ")
  PutHex8(gLrSacrWant)
  Print("  got ")
  PutHex8(gLrSacrGot)
  If gLrSacrGot = gLrSacrWant
    PrintN("   STUCK")
  Else
    PrintN("   discarded")
  EndIf
  Print("   sCR0  found ")
  PutHex8(gLrScr0Found)
  Print("  wanted ")
  PutHex8(gLrScr0Want)
  Print("  got ")
  PutHex8(gLrScr0Got)
  PrintNl()
  ; READ THE COMMENT ON gLrScr0Found BEFORE CHANGING THIS. Bits 4 and 5
  ; read back clear on this device even for the operating system, so an
  ; exact-match test would report a successful write as a refusal.
  If gLrScr0Got = gLrScr0Want
    PrintN("   sCR0 STUCK, exactly as written.")
  ElseIf gLrScr0Got = $002A1C06
    PrintN("   sCR0 STUCK. It does not equal what was written because bits 4")
    PrintN("   and 5 read back clear on this device - and it is now BYTE FOR")
    PrintN("   BYTE the value the operating system leaves here, 002A1C06.")
  ElseIf gLrScr0Got <> gLrScr0Found
    Print("   sCR0 CHANGED but matched neither: bits that moved = ")
    PutHex8(gLrScr0Got ! gLrScr0Found)
    PrintNl()
    PrintN("   Recorded, not interpreted.")
  Else
    PrintN("   sCR0 discarded - it reads exactly as it did before the write.")
  EndIf
  stuck = 0
  For i = 0 To nsmr - 1
    v = Rd32(#SMMU_S2CR0 + i * 4)
    Print("   S2CR[")
    PrintDec(i)
    Print("] = ")
    PutHex8(v)
    If v = #SMMU_S2CR_RESET
      stuck = stuck + 1
      PrintN("   STUCK (took the reset value)")
    Else
      PrintN("   discarded (still undefined)")
    EndIf
  Next
  gLrS2crStuck = stuck
  Print("   ")
  PrintDec(stuck)
  Print(" of ")
  PrintDec(nsmr)
  PrintN(" S2CRs took the reset value.")
  PrintN("   S2CR is the cleanest of these three: it reads UNDEFINED before")
  PrintN("   the write, so a specific constant afterwards cannot be a stale")
  PrintN("   bus read of our own store the way a same-address read-back can.")
  Flush()

  ; ---- test_smr_masks, which the driver runs right after the reset ---
  Say("   and arm_smmu_test_smr_masks, which runs immediately after the reset:")
  PrintN("   ID0 says NUMSIDB = 15, so the driver's test pattern is 00007FFF.")
  Print("S7r about to WRITE 00007FFF to SMR[0] at ")
  PutHex8(#SMMU_SMR0)
  PrintNl()
  Flush()
  Wr32(#SMMU_SMR0, $00007FFF)
  v = Rd32(#SMMU_SMR0)
  Got("SMR[0] after the id pattern", v)
  Print("S7r about to WRITE ")
  PutHex8((v & $FFFF) << 16)
  Print(" to SMR[0] at ")
  PutHex8(#SMMU_SMR0)
  PrintN("   (the mask half of the same test)")
  Flush()
  Wr32(#SMMU_SMR0, (v & $FFFF) << 16)
  Got("SMR[0] after the mask pattern", Rd32(#SMMU_SMR0))
  Print("S7r about to WRITE 0 to SMR[0] at ")
  PutHex8(#SMMU_SMR0)
  PrintN("   (putting the test pattern back to the reset value)")
  Flush()
  Wr32(#SMMU_SMR0, 0)
  Got("SMR[0] restored", Rd32(#SMMU_SMR0))
  Flush()
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 7c. IS THE CONTEXT-BANK READ-BACK REAL, OR A STALE BUS READ?
;
;  Section 11j concluded "THE CONTEXT BANKS ARE OURS" from one piece of
;  evidence: write 0xA5C30000 to CB0_TTBR0, read the SAME ADDRESS
;  immediately, get 0xA5C30000 back. That is also exactly what a read
;  which is not answered by the target and returns the last value driven
;  on the bus would produce, and every other context-bank register reads
;  undefined for us - CB0_SCTLR was 0x14150306 one run and 0x14152306
;  the next, so it VARIES.
;
;  This is not an argument, it is three reads. Put a register with a
;  known, very different, constant value in between: ID7 reads
;  0x00000024 and could not be mistaken for a base address.
;
;    A: read CB0_TTBR0 twice with nothing in between - is it even stable?
;    B: write the pattern
;    C: read ID7 - if the bus is answering with stale data, this is now
;       what a following unanswered read would return
;    D: read CB0_TTBR0 again
;
;  D = the pattern  -> the bank genuinely stored it. 11j stands.
;  D = 0x00000024   -> the read-back is the last bus value. 11j's
;                      conclusion is an artefact and the whole
;                      "context banks are ours" route is void.
;  D = anything else-> neither; report it and do not interpret.
; ======================================================================
Procedure Step7c_CbStaleTest()
  Define a1.i
  Define a2.i
  Define d.i
  Define idv.i

  PrintNl()
  Say("--- S7c: is the context-bank read-back real, or a stale bus read? ---")
  If gSmmuClocked <> 1
    PrintN("   refused: the three IOMMU clocks have not been proven running.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If gStop <> 0
    PrintN("   refused: an earlier step stopped.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  Announce(7, "CB0_TTBR0, read A", #SMMU_CB0 + #SMMU_CB_TTBR0)
  a1 = Rd32(#SMMU_CB0 + #SMMU_CB_TTBR0)
  Got("CB0_TTBR0 read A", a1)
  a2 = Rd32(#SMMU_CB0 + #SMMU_CB_TTBR0)
  Got("CB0_TTBR0 read B", a2)
  If a1 <> a2
    PrintN("   THE SAME REGISTER READ TWICE GAVE TWO ANSWERS. Nothing about")
    PrintN("   this bank can be concluded from a read-back at all, and 11j's")
    PrintN("   conclusion is void on that ground alone.")
    gCbStale = 2
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  PrintN("   the two reads agree, so the register is at least stable.")
  Flush()

  Print("S7c about to WRITE A5C30000 to CB0_TTBR0 at ")
  PutHex8(#SMMU_CB0 + #SMMU_CB_TTBR0)
  PrintN("   (inert - this bank's translation-enable bit is clear)")
  Flush()
  Wr32(#SMMU_CB0 + #SMMU_CB_TTBR0, $A5C30000)

  Announce(7, "SMMU_ID7, the interposed known value", #SMMU_ID7)
  idv = Rd32(#SMMU_ID7)
  Got("ID7 in between", idv)

  Announce(7, "CB0_TTBR0, read D after the interposed read", #SMMU_CB0 + #SMMU_CB_TTBR0)
  d = Rd32(#SMMU_CB0 + #SMMU_CB_TTBR0)
  Got("CB0_TTBR0 read D", d)

  If d = $A5C30000
    gCbStale = 0
    PrintN("   THE BANK REALLY STORED IT. A read of a different register with")
    PrintN("   a completely different value came in between and the pattern")
    PrintN("   survived. Section 11j stands: the context banks are writable.")
  ElseIf d = idv
    gCbStale = 1
    PrintN("   THE READ-BACK WAS A STALE BUS READ. CB0_TTBR0 now returns the")
    PrintN("   value of the register read just before it, not the value")
    PrintN("   written to it. Section 11j's conclusion is an artefact and the")
    PrintN("   context-bank route is void - nothing in this block is ours.")
  Else
    gCbStale = 3
    PrintN("   NEITHER: it is not the pattern and not the interposed value.")
    PrintN("   Recorded and not interpreted.")
  EndIf
  ; put it back whatever happened
  Wr32(#SMMU_CB0 + #SMMU_CB_TTBR0, a1)
  Got("CB0_TTBR0 restored to", Rd32(#SMMU_CB0 + #SMMU_CB_TTBR0))
  Flush()
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 7u. CLEAR USFCFG - the route the 11u measurement handed us.
;
;  Section 11v. M2-S established what is actually refused in this block,
;  and it is two bits, not the block:
;
;    sCR0.CLIENTPD  (bit 0)   - the global disable. Refused.
;    SMR[n].VALID   (bit 31)  - refused: SMR[0] took 00007FFF and it took
;                               7FFF0000, and then refused 80030000.
;
;  Everything else took. sACR took 0000051C. sCR0 took VMIDPNE and PTM
;  and now reads 002A1C06, which is byte for byte what the operating
;  system leaves. Every context bank took its ACTLR and its SCTLR.
;
;  So the GPU cannot be given a stream-match entry, because that needs
;  VALID, and the block cannot be globally disabled, because that needs
;  CLIENTPD. But there is a third door and it needs neither:
;
;    sCR0.USFCFG (bit 10) decides what happens to a stream that matches
;    NOTHING in the table. Set, unmatched streams ABORT. Clear, they
;    BYPASS.
;
;  The GPU matches nothing - that is the whole problem - so with USFCFG
;  clear it bypasses and emits physical addresses, which is exactly the
;  state milestone 2 needs. And USFCFG lives in sCR0, a register we have
;  now written successfully.
;
;  Why it was set in the first place: the operating system's
;  CONFIG_ARM_SMMU_DISABLE_BYPASS_BY_DEFAULT=y, a policy choice for a
;  multi-master system, faithfully copied by the replication above
;  because the reference driver copies it. It is a policy, not a
;  hardware requirement.
;
;  SAFETY. This IOMMU has exactly ONE client in the device tree - the
;  GPU (agatti.dtsi:1725, and nothing else references this controller).
;  The applications IOMMU is a different block at a different address
;  and is untouched. Under UEFI the GPU is not fetching anything yet, so
;  the window in which anything at all is behind this block is the
;  window this program opens on purpose. A reboot restores it - the
;  operating system runs arm_smmu_device_reset at probe and sets USFCFG
;  again from its own config.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i Step7u_Usfcfg()
  Define v.i
  Define want.i

  PrintNl()
  Say("--- S7u: clear USFCFG so unmatched streams BYPASS instead of abort ---")
  If gSmmuClocked <> 1
    PrintN("   refused: the three IOMMU clocks have not been proven running.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If gStop <> 0
    PrintN("   refused: an earlier step stopped.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  v = Rd32(#SMMU_SCR0)
  Got("sCR0 before", v)
  Print("   CLIENTPD=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(v, 0))
  Print("  USFCFG=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(v, 10))
  PrintNl()
  If BitOf(v, 10) = 0
    PrintN("   USFCFG is already clear - unmatched streams already bypass and")
    PrintN("   there is nothing to do here.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  want = v & ($FFFFFFFF - $400)
  Print("S7u about to WRITE ")
  PutHex8(want)
  Print(" to sCR0 at ")
  PutHex8(#SMMU_SCR0)
  PrintN("   (clearing USFCFG only - every other bit is left exactly as read)")
  Flush()
  Wr32(#SMMU_SCR0, want)
  v = Rd32(#SMMU_SCR0)
  Got("sCR0 after", v)
  Print("   CLIENTPD=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(v, 0))
  Print("  USFCFG=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(v, 10))
  PrintNl()
  If BitOf(v, 10) = 0
    gUsfcfgCleared = 1
    PrintN("   *** USFCFG IS CLEAR. Unmatched streams now BYPASS. The GPU")
    PrintN("   *** matches nothing in the stream table, so its transactions")
    PrintN("   *** pass through with physical addresses - which is the state")
    PrintN("   *** the command processor needs, reached without either of the")
    PrintN("   *** two bits this block refuses.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  gUsfcfgCleared = 0
  PrintN("   USFCFG did not clear. So the refusal is not only CLIENTPD and")
  PrintN("   VALID after all: this bit is held too, and the pattern is that")
  PrintN("   every bit which could let a stream through is held while every")
  PrintN("   bit which cannot is ours. Record that shape; it is a sharper")
  PrintN("   description of the owner than anything measured before it.")
  Flush()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure Step7_SmmuBody()
  Define scr0.i
  Define id0.i
  Define id1.i
  Define id2.i
  Define nsmr.i
  Define i.i
  Define smr.i
  Define s2cr.i
  Define hits.i
  Define rc.i
  Define v.i

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  Before ANY further IOMMU write: is that page writable from here at
  ;  all? Compared against a page we have already PROVEN we can write -
  ;  the GPU register block, where a scratch register took a value and
  ;  gave it back. If the two pages differ, the aperture was never the
  ;  story and the fix is a mapping.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("--- S6b: can this environment write those pages at all? ---")
  ReadSctlr()
  Print("   SCTLR_EL1 = ")
  PutHex16(gSctlr)
  Print("   MMU enable M = ")
  PrintDec(gSctlr & 1)
  If (gSctlr & 1) = 0
    PrintN("  -> MMU IS OFF, so every access is direct and no mapping can")
    PrintN("      be refusing anything. The aperture is the only candidate.")
  Else
    PrintN("  -> MMU is ON, so a mapping CAN refuse a write and the")
    PrintN("      translation below is the thing that says whether it does.")
  EndIf
  Flush()
  AtWrite(#GPU_CP_SCRATCH0, "KNOWN GOOD - the GPU scratch register")
  AtWrite(#SMMU_SCR0,       "the IOMMU control register")
  AtWrite(#SMMU_SMR0,       "the IOMMU stream table")
  AtWrite(#SMMU_CB0,        "an IOMMU context bank")
  PrintN("   If the known-good line says permitted and the IOMMU lines say")
  PrintN("   FAULT, the device was never refusing us and every conclusion")
  PrintN("   from the last several rounds needs revisiting.")
  Flush()

  Say("--- S7: the GPU IOMMU ---")
  gSmmuBypass = 0
  If gStop <> 0
    PrintN("   refused: an earlier step stopped.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If gVScratchBack <> #SCRATCH_PATTERN
    PrintN("   refused: the graphics window is not alive.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  ALL THREE OF THE IOMMU'S CLOCKS. This is a fix for a real mistake,
  ;  not caution.
  ;
  ;  The device tree gives this block three clocks - mem, hlos and iface
  ;  (agatti.dtsi:1835-1840) - and an earlier build of this step enabled
  ;  ONE of them and then read the block. The missing one was `iface`,
  ;  GCC_GPU_SNOC_DVM_GFX_CLK: the clock that carries a register access,
  ;  and one that every run so far has measured HALTED.
  ;
  ;  That is this campaign's founding rule walked into backwards. The
  ;  ladder gates every other block on "domain powered AND interface
  ;  clock running" and has refused correctly several times. The IOMMU
  ;  escaped it because it went into the program as a GATE - a thing that
  ;  decides whether the next step may run - rather than as a block that
  ;  has to be brought up like all the others.
  ;
  ;  A block being read for a safety check is still a block being read.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  If EnableBranch(7, "GCC_GPU_MEMNOC_GFX_CLK mem", #GCC_GPU_MEMNOC_GFX_CBCR) = 0
    Halt(70, "the IOMMU memory clock never started: bit 31 of 0143600C was still set 2000 us after its enable bit was written. Reading the IOMMU without all three of its clocks stalls the bus, so nothing further was touched.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If EnableBranch(7, "GPU_CC_HLOS1_VOTE_GPU_SMMU_CLK hlos", #GPUCC_HLOS1_VOTE_SMMU_CBCR) = 0
    Halt(70, "the IOMMU vote clock never started: bit 31 of 05995000 was still set 2000 us after its enable bit was written. Check that the CX power domain is up. Nothing further was touched.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If EnableBranch(7, "GCC_GPU_SNOC_DVM_GFX_CLK iface", #GCC_GPU_SNOC_DVM_CBCR) = 0
    Halt(70, "the IOMMU INTERFACE clock never started: bit 31 of 01436018 was still set 2000 us after its enable bit was written. This is the clock that carries a register access, so reading the IOMMU without it is precisely the stall this ladder exists to avoid. Nothing further was touched.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  PrintN("   all three IOMMU clocks are RUNNING. The block may be read.")
  gSmmuClocked = 1
  Flush()

  ; The read-only survey, BEFORE this step attempts a single write.
  ; Section 11t. Nothing in it writes; see its header.
  SmmuAuxRead("BEFORE", 0)

  Announce(7, "SMMU_ID0", #SMMU_ID0)
  id0 = Rd32(#SMMU_ID0)
  Got("SMMU_ID0", id0)
  Announce(7, "SMMU_ID1", #SMMU_ID1)
  id1 = Rd32(#SMMU_ID1)
  Got("SMMU_ID1", id1)
  ; ID2 is read for one reason: arm_smmu_device_reset() sets VMID16EN in
  ; sCR0 when ID2 bit 15 says the block has 16-bit VMIDs, and step 7r
  ; below computes that value the same way the driver does rather than
  ; hard-coding what Linux happened to leave.
  Announce(7, "SMMU_ID2", #SMMU_ID2)
  id2 = Rd32(#SMMU_ID2)
  Got("SMMU_ID2", id2)
  Print("   VMID16=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(id2, 15))
  Print("  UBS=")
  PrintDec((id2 >> 8) & $F)
  Print("  OAS=")
  PrintDec((id2 >> 4) & $F)
  Print("  IAS=")
  PrintDec(id2 & $F)
  PrintNl()
  Flush()
  nsmr = id0 & $FF
  Print("   stream-match groups = ")
  PrintDec(nsmr)
  Print("   context banks = ")
  PrintDec(id1 & $FF)
  Print("   page size bit = ")
  PrintDec(BitOf(id1, 31))
  PrintNl()
  PrintN("   (the operating system reports 4 groups and 5 banks on this part)")
  Flush()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  sCR1 - the SECURE/NON-SECURE PARTITION OF THE GLOBAL SPACE.
  ;
  ;  On an SMMUv2 with the security extensions this register says how
  ;  much of the global space the non-secure world is allowed to
  ;  program: NSNUMSMRGO is how many stream-match groups it owns and
  ;  NSNUMCBO how many context banks. Writes from the non-secure side to
  ;  a group ABOVE NSNUMSMRGO are ignored - reads work, writes vanish.
  ;
  ;  That is exactly the behaviour measured on this board, and it would
  ;  also explain why the context banks accept writes while the stream
  ;  table does not: the banks are below NSNUMCBO and the groups are not.
  ;
  ;  Linux never reads this register - it is absent from the driver
  ;  entirely - so this is a read the reference implementation cannot
  ;  confirm for us. It is architectural, and it is a read, so it costs
  ;  nothing to look.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Announce(7, "SMMU_sCR1 (secure/non-secure partition)", #SMMU_SCR1)
  v = Rd32(#SMMU_SCR1)
  Got("sCR1", v)
  Print("   NSNUMSMRGO = ")
  PrintDec(v & $FF)
  Print("   (stream-match groups the non-secure world may program)")
  PrintNl()
  Print("   NSNUMCBO   = ")
  PrintDec((v >> 8) & $FF)
  Print("   (context banks the non-secure world may program)")
  PrintNl()
  ; MEASURED AND REFUTED 2026-09-04, section 11t. This used to print
  ; "NSNUMSMRGO IS ZERO ... which is precisely why our writes to them
  ; vanish", with confidence, into the transcript. It cannot be true:
  ; sCR1 reads 0x00000000 UNDER LINUX AS WELL, and Linux's SMR0 and SMR1
  ; writes stick. If a zero here meant the non-secure world owns no
  ; groups, the operating system could not have programmed one either.
  ;
  ; The old text is kept in this comment because a wrong explanation
  ; printed confidently into a transcript is the failure this campaign
  ; keeps paying for, and deleting it silently loses the lesson.
  If (v & $FF) = 0
    PrintN("   NSNUMSMRGO is zero. This is NOT the explanation of the refused")
    PrintN("   writes: sCR1 reads zero under the operating system too, and its")
    PrintN("   stream-match writes stick anyway. Measured 2026-09-04. A zero")
    PrintN("   here is what this integration reports, not a permission.")
  Else
    PrintN("   the non-secure world is reported as owning some groups. That is")
    PrintN("   a change from every previous reading, which measured zero.")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  ; and a look at the region that would hold a non-secure alias of the
  ; global registers, if this integration provides one at all.
  Announce(7, "possible non-secure alias of the global space", #SMMU_NS_ALIAS)
  Got("[0x400]", Rd32(#SMMU_NS_ALIAS))
  Got("[0x404]", Rd32(#SMMU_NS_ALIAS + 4))
  ; CORRECTED 2026-09-04. The earlier text here said the reference driver
  ; knows no such alias. It does: arm_smmu_gr0_ns (arm-smmu-impl.c:13-25)
  ; maps sCR0, sACR, sGFSR and the three sGFSYNR registers to offset +
  ; 0x400 for the Calxeda integration. Our device does not use that hook,
  ; so the alias is not established HERE - but "the driver has never heard
  ; of it" was simply wrong, and it was wrong because a subset was grepped
  ; and the result reported as a general claim.
  PrintN("   (a +0x400 alias of these registers IS a shape this driver knows")
  PrintN("    - arm_smmu_gr0_ns uses it for another integration - but ours")
  PrintN("    does not select it, so these reads are exploratory. The sACR")
  PrintN("    survey below reads the same window at its three real offsets.)")
  Flush()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  SECTION 11u goes here, BEFORE the ad-hoc single-register writes
  ;  below. The whole point of it is the sequence and the order, and a
  ;  block that has already had CLIENTPD poked at is not the state the
  ;  reference driver starts from. Everything after these two calls -
  ;  the sCR0 read, the stream-table walk, the bypass attempt, the
  ;  aperture call - is unchanged, and now reads out the state the
  ;  replication left behind.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Step7r_LinuxReset(nsmr, id1 & $FF, id2)
  Step7c_CbStaleTest()
  Step7u_Usfcfg()

  Announce(7, "SMMU_sCR0", #SMMU_SCR0)
  scr0 = Rd32(#SMMU_SCR0)
  Got("sCR0", scr0)
  Print("   CLIENTPD=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(scr0, 0))
  Print("  USFCFG=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(scr0, 10))
  PrintNl()

  If BitOf(scr0, 0) = 1
    gSmmuBypass = 1
    PrintN("   CLIENTPD is SET: the IOMMU is globally OFF and every master")
    PrintN("   bypasses it. Addresses the GPU emits are physical.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  PrintN("   CLIENTPD is clear, so the IOMMU is ON. Walking the stream")
  PrintN("   table for the three ids this GPU uses (0, 1 and 2):")
  Flush()
  hits = 0
  If nsmr > 16
    nsmr = 16
  EndIf
  For i = 0 To nsmr - 1
    smr = Rd32(#SMMU_SMR0 + i * 4)
    s2cr = Rd32(#SMMU_S2CR0 + i * 4)
    Print("   [")
    PrintDec(i)
    Print("] SMR=")
    PutHex8(smr)
    Print(" S2CR=")
    PutHex8(s2cr)
    If BitOf(smr, 31) = 1
      Print("  VALID id=")
      PutHex8(smr & $FFFF)
      Print(" mask=")
      PutHex8((smr >> 16) & $7FFF)
      Print(" type=")
      PrintDec((s2cr >> 16) & 3)
      hits = hits + 1
    EndIf
    PrintNl()
    Flush()
  Next

  If hits = 0
    PrintN("   no valid stream-match entry at all, so the GPU matches")
    PrintN("   nothing in this table.")
    If BitOf(scr0, 10) = 0
      gSmmuBypass = 1
      PrintN("   USFCFG is clear, so unmatched streams BYPASS. Nothing to do.")
      Flush()
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
    PrintN("   and USFCFG is SET, so unmatched streams ABORT. The command")
    PrintN("   processor could not fetch one word in this state.")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  Put the block in bypass.
  ;
  ;  MEASURED on this board: sCR0 = 0x002A0406, so translation is on and
  ;  unmatched streams abort, and all four stream-match registers are
  ;  zero. Nothing maps the GPU and nothing would.
  ;
  ;  CLIENTPD is the global disable: with it set, every master behind
  ;  this block bypasses. That is a big hammer on a shared IOMMU and a
  ;  reasonable one here, because THIS IOMMU IS NOT SHARED - the device
  ;  tree gives it exactly one client, the GPU (agatti.dtsi:1725, and it
  ;  is the only node referencing this controller). The applications
  ;  IOMMU is a separate block at a separate address and is untouched.
  ;
  ;  The alternative - a stream-match entry per id with a bypass type -
  ;  is more surgical and needs the firmware quirk check first, because
  ;  on some Qualcomm firmware a bypass type reads back as fault. One
  ;  register beats three plus a quirk probe for a first attempt.
  ;
  ;  NOTE the value is built from what was read rather than written as a
  ;  bare constant. The reference driver keeps a dummy value around
  ;  purely because some deployed hypervisor firmware mishandles stores
  ;  made from the zero register, so a computed value is also the safer
  ;  instruction to emit here.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("   putting the IOMMU in bypass:")
  Print("S7 about to WRITE ")
  PutHex8(scr0 | 1)
  Print(" to sCR0 at ")
  PutHex8(#SMMU_SCR0)
  PrintN("   (setting CLIENTPD - this block serves only the GPU)")
  Flush()
  Wr32(#SMMU_SCR0, scr0 | 1)
  QEfiStall(100)
  scr0 = Rd32(#SMMU_SCR0)
  Print("   sCR0 now = ")
  PutHex8(scr0)
  Print("   CLIENTPD=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(scr0, 0))
  PrintNl()
  If BitOf(scr0, 0) = 1
    gSmmuBypass = 1
    PrintN("   THE WRITE STUCK. The IOMMU is bypassed and the GPU emits")
    PrintN("   physical addresses.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  The global disable was REFUSED - measured: wrote 002A0407, read
  ;  back 002A0406, CLIENTPD still clear.
  ;
  ;  Rather than guess why, find out WHAT is writable. There are two
  ;  quite different worlds and they need different work:
  ;
  ;    - the whole global space is owned by something above us (this
  ;      platform has a hypervisor carve-out, and the reference driver
  ;      carries a workaround for firmware that traps and emulates these
  ;      registers), in which case no amount of poking here helps and
  ;      the GPU has to be given a real translation instead;
  ;
  ;    - only sCR0 is protected, and the stream-match table is ours, in
  ;      which case one entry pointed at bypass does the job.
  ;
  ;  A write and a read-back on a stream-match register answers it in one
  ;  step. It is safe: the entry is invalid and unmatched today, so
  ;  nothing routes through it either way, and the value is restored if
  ;  it does stick but the type register does not.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  ASK THE OWNER.
  ;
  ;  The global disable was refused. Before poking anything else, use
  ;  the call whose entire purpose is configuring this GPU's IOMMU
  ;  aperture - the reference driver issues it for every part of this
  ;  generation when the firmware offers it, with context bank 0.
  ;
  ;  Full documentation, written before this was implemented, is section
  ;  9b of the vault note. Two claims are reported separately below,
  ;  because they are two different things: whether the CALL succeeded,
  ;  and whether anything actually CHANGED.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("   the global disable was refused - asking the secure world instead:")
  rc = Smc(#SMC_IS_CALL_AVAIL, $01, #APERTURE_AVAIL_ARG, 0, 0, 0)
  Print("   availability probe -> x0=")
  PutHex8(rc)
  Print("  x1=")
  PutHex8(gSmcR1)
  PrintNl()
  Flush()
  If rc <> 0 Or gSmcR1 <> 1
    PrintN("   the aperture call is NOT available on this firmware. That is")
    PrintN("   an answer, not a fault: this monitor does not offer the one")
    PrintN("   call whose job is configuring the GPU IOMMU.")
    Flush()
  Else
    PrintN("   the call is available. Issuing it for context bank 0:")
    Flush()
    rc = Smc(#SMC_CP_SMMU_APERTURE, #ARGINFO_APERTURE, #APERTURE_CTX_BANK0, #APERTURE_FILL, #APERTURE_FILL, #APERTURE_FILL)
    SmcSay(rc)
    If rc <> #SCM_OK
      Halt(74, "the secure world refused to configure the GPU IOMMU aperture. The code above names the refusal. This was the call whose whole purpose is this, so a refusal here is a real result and not something to retry.")
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
    PrintN("   THE CALL SUCCEEDED. Now re-reading the IOMMU to see whether")
    PrintN("   anything actually changed - the call returns a status only,")
    PrintN("   and an accepted request is not the same as a working GPU.")
    Flush()
    scr0 = Rd32(#SMMU_SCR0)
    Print("   sCR0 = ")
    PutHex8(scr0)
    Print("   CLIENTPD=")
    PrintDec(BitOf(scr0, 0))
    Print("  USFCFG=")
    PrintDec(BitOf(scr0, 10))
    PrintNl()
    For i = 0 To 3
      Print("   SMR[")
      PrintDec(i)
      Print("] = ")
      PutHex8(Rd32(#SMMU_SMR0 + i * 4))
      Print("   S2CR[")
      PrintDec(i)
      Print("] = ")
      PutHex8(Rd32(#SMMU_S2CR0 + i * 4))
      PrintNl()
    Next
    Flush()
    If BitOf(scr0, 0) = 1
      gSmmuBypass = 1
      PrintN("   CLIENTPD is now SET - the block bypasses.")
      Flush()
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
    hits = 0
    For i = 0 To 3
      If BitOf(Rd32(#SMMU_SMR0 + i * 4), 31) = 1
        hits = hits + 1
      EndIf
    Next
    If hits > 0
      PrintN("   stream-match entries appeared. Checking whether any covers")
      PrintN("   the GPU and points at bypass:")
      For i = 0 To 3
        smr = Rd32(#SMMU_SMR0 + i * 4)
        s2cr = Rd32(#SMMU_S2CR0 + i * 4)
        If BitOf(smr, 31) = 1 And ((s2cr >> 16) & 3) = 1
          gSmmuBypass = 1
        EndIf
      Next
      If gSmmuBypass = 1
        PrintN("   one of them is a bypass entry. The GPU can address memory.")
        Flush()
        ProcedureReturn
      EndIf
      PrintN("   entries exist but none is a bypass for the GPU.")
    Else
      PrintN("   NOTHING CHANGED. The call was accepted and the IOMMU reads")
      PrintN("   exactly as before. That is its own finding: the aperture")
      PrintN("   call does something other than what this run needs.")
    EndIf
    Flush()
  EndIf

  Say("   still not bypassing - probing what IS writable:")
  Print("S7 about to WRITE 80030000 to SMR[0] at ")
  PutHex8(#SMMU_SMR0)
  PrintN("   (valid, id 0, mask 3 - covers stream ids 0 through 3)")
  Flush()
  Wr32(#SMMU_SMR0, $80030000)
  smr = Rd32(#SMMU_SMR0)
  Print("   SMR[0] reads back ")
  PutHex8(smr)
  PrintNl()
  If smr <> $80030000
    PrintN("   the stream-match table is not writable either. So the GLOBAL")
    PrintN("   space is owned above us - both the control register and the")
    PrintN("   stream table refused a write.")
    Flush()

    ; ----------------------------------------------------------------
    ;  Are the CONTEXT BANKS ours, though?
    ;
    ;  They are a different address space from the global registers.
    ;  Global space is the first pages; the banks start at page 8, which
    ;  this part's own ID register says is 0x059A8000. Firmware that
    ;  traps and emulates the global registers does not necessarily trap
    ;  the per-context ones - and if the banks are writable, a real
    ;  translation becomes possible even though bypass never will be.
    ;
    ;  One write and one read-back answers it. The register chosen is
    ;  the translation base address, which is inert while this bank's
    ;  translation-enable bit is clear - and it is read first to confirm
    ;  that, so nothing is switched on by accident.
    ; ----------------------------------------------------------------
    Say("   are the CONTEXT BANKS writable, though?")
    Announce(7, "context bank 0 SCTLR", #SMMU_CB0 + #SMMU_CB_SCTLR)
    v = Rd32(#SMMU_CB0 + #SMMU_CB_SCTLR)
    Got("CB0_SCTLR", v)
    Print("   translation enable M=")
    PrintDec(BitOf(v, 0))
    If BitOf(v, 0) = 0
      PrintN("  -> this bank is NOT translating, so the base register is inert")
    Else
      PrintN("  -> this bank IS translating; not touching it")
      Halt(75, "context bank 0 already has translation enabled, so it belongs to something else and this program will not write to it. Nothing downstream was touched.")
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
    Flush()

    Announce(7, "context bank 0 TTBR0", #SMMU_CB0 + #SMMU_CB_TTBR0)
    v = Rd32(#SMMU_CB0 + #SMMU_CB_TTBR0)
    Got("CB0_TTBR0 before", v)
    Print("S7 about to WRITE A5C30000 to CB0_TTBR0 at ")
    PutHex8(#SMMU_CB0 + #SMMU_CB_TTBR0)
    PrintN("   (inert while this bank does not translate - a writability test only)")
    Flush()
    Wr32(#SMMU_CB0 + #SMMU_CB_TTBR0, $A5C30000)
    s2cr = Rd32(#SMMU_CB0 + #SMMU_CB_TTBR0)
    Got("CB0_TTBR0 after", s2cr)
    ; put it back whatever happened
    Wr32(#SMMU_CB0 + #SMMU_CB_TTBR0, v)

    If s2cr = $A5C30000
      PrintN("   THE CONTEXT BANKS ARE OURS. The global space is sealed but")
      PrintN("   the per-context space is not, so a real translation table is")
      PrintN("   possible even though bypass is not. That is the route.")
      PrintN("   Building one is substantial work and is not this run.")
    Else
      PrintN("   the context banks are sealed too. The whole IOMMU belongs to")
      PrintN("   something above this program and the GPU cannot be given any")
      PrintN("   addressing from here at all.")
    EndIf
    Flush()

    ; NOT a halt any more. This used to latch the global stop, which then
    ; refused step 8 before step 8's own (deliberately relaxed) gate was
    ; ever reached - the stop flag beat the warning that replaced it.
    ; The reasoning for continuing is in step 8's gate; repeating the
    ; refusal here would silently override it.
    PrintN("   the IOMMU cannot be reprogrammed from here. Continuing to the")
    PrintN("   command processor anyway - see the note at its gate for why")
    PrintN("   the all-zero stream table is probably not the truth.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  PrintN("   IT STUCK. The stream-match table is ours even though sCR0 is")
  PrintN("   not. Pointing this entry at bypass:")
  Flush()

  Print("S7 about to WRITE 00010000 to S2CR[0] at ")
  PutHex8(#SMMU_S2CR0)
  PrintN("   (type 1 = BYPASS)")
  Flush()
  Wr32(#SMMU_S2CR0, $00010000)
  s2cr = Rd32(#SMMU_S2CR0)
  Print("   S2CR[0] reads back ")
  PutHex8(s2cr)
  Print("   type=")
  PrintDec((s2cr >> 16) & 3)
  PrintNl()
  If ((s2cr >> 16) & 3) = 1
    gSmmuBypass = 1
    PrintN("   BYPASS IS SET for the GPU's stream ids. It emits physical")
    PrintN("   addresses now.")
  Else
    ; the reference driver knows this one: on some firmware a bypass
    ; type reads back as fault, and it then fakes bypass with a context
    ; bank instead. Report it as that rather than as a mystery.
    Halt(73, "the stream-match entry took but its type register did not: S2CR[0] at 059A0C00 was written with type 1 (bypass) and did not read back as bypass. The reference driver knows this firmware behaviour and works around it by pointing the stream at a reserved context bank with translation disabled instead, which is more work than this run. Nothing downstream was touched.")
  EndIf
  Flush()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Step7_Smmu - the body, wrapped so the AFTER survey happens on EVERY
;  path out of it.
;
;  Step7_SmmuBody has fourteen ProcedureReturn statements. Putting the
;  second read at the end of the body would run it on one of those paths
;  and on no other, and the run that mattered would be the one that
;  returned early. The wrapper is not decoration.
;
;  The survey refuses on its own if the clocks were never proven, so an
;  early return before the clock ladder costs nothing and touches
;  nothing.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure Step7_Smmu()
  Step7_SmmuBody()
  SmmuAuxRead("AFTER", 1)
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  SECTION 11w. THE CONTROL ON THE DEBUG PORT.
;
;  WHY THIS EXISTS, and it is the whole point of BUILD M2-U.
;
;  Since section 11l every statement about why milestone 2 stops has
;  rested on one sentence: "the command processor's instruction memory
;  reads zero". That sentence comes from ONE instrument - the indexed
;  debug port at CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_ADDR/DATA - and that instrument has
;  never been checked against anything at all. It has no positive
;  control. Nobody has ever written a value to its address register and
;  read it back.
;
;  This campaign has now been wrong three times in a row in exactly that
;  shape: sACR (11t), the exact-match read-back verdict (11u), and the
;  two-bit sample that became "the block is sealed" (11v). Each time the
;  cheap control was the thing that was never done. So it is done here,
;  first, before one more theory is built on a reading nobody has
;  validated.
;
;  WHAT IT TAKES, in order:
;
;    1. THE ADDRESS-REGISTER CONTROL. Write a pattern to the address
;       half and read it back. If it does not come back, the port is not
;       a window and every "the memory is empty" claim since 11l is void.
;    2. THE AUTO-INCREMENT CONTROL. Section 11l flagged, honestly, that
;       it wrote the address once and read the data six times, assuming
;       an auto-increment nobody had verified. The reference driver's
;       crash dump says the port increments (a6xx_gpu_state.c:1512-1516,
;       "each read increments the internal address by 1"). This asks the
;       silicon.
;    3. A SWEEP, re-writing the address before EVERY read so that
;       whichever way (2) came out cannot confuse the result.
;    4. A SECOND, INDEPENDENT INDEXED PORT - CP_SQE_STAT. If that one
;       answers and the microcode one does not, the broken thing is the
;       instrument and not the memory.
;
;  Every access here is a read except the writes to the two ADDRESS
;  registers, which select and do not command, and one of which this
;  program already wrote on the previous three runs.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i SweepIndex(k.i)
  ; The indices the sweep visits. 0x054F is the LAST dword of the 21,648
  ; byte image, so an image that loaded and stopped short shows up as a
  ; boundary rather than as a uniform zero. 0x7FFF is the last index the
  ; reference driver's own dump reads (a6xx_gpu_state.h:434 declares the
  ; window 0x8000 deep). 0x70D3 is the one index in this window the
  ; driver reads by name - on a7xx it holds a mirrored register value
  ; (a6xx_gpu_state.c:1481-1490), so it is the likeliest place in the
  ; whole space to find something that is not instruction memory.
  Select k
    Case 0  : ProcedureReturn $00000000
    Case 1  : ProcedureReturn $00000001
    Case 2  : ProcedureReturn $00000002
    Case 3  : ProcedureReturn $00000003
    Case 4  : ProcedureReturn $00000100
    Case 5  : ProcedureReturn $0000054F
    Case 6  : ProcedureReturn $00001000
    Case 7  : ProcedureReturn $00002000
    Case 8  : ProcedureReturn $00004000
    Case 9  : ProcedureReturn $000070D3
    Case 10 : ProcedureReturn $00007FFF
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
#SWEEP_COUNT = 11

Procedure.i SqeWindow(*when)
  Define i.i
  Define v.i
  Define idx.i
  Define nz.i
  Define a.i
  Define zero.i

  Print("   --- SQE debug-port reading, ")
  UartWriteStr(*when)
  PrintN(" ---")
  Flush()

  ; ---- 1. THE ADDRESS-REGISTER CONTROL --------------------------------
  Print("S8w about to WRITE ")
  PutHex8(#SQE_DBG_ADDR_PROBE)
  Print(" to CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_ADDR at ")
  PutHex8(#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_ADDR)
  PrintNl()
  Flush()
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_ADDR, #SQE_DBG_ADDR_PROBE)
  a = Rd32(#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_ADDR)
  gDbgAddrGot = a
  Print("   the address register was handed ")
  PutHex8(#SQE_DBG_ADDR_PROBE)
  Print(" and reads back ")
  PutHex8(a)
  PrintNl()
  If a = #SQE_DBG_ADDR_PROBE
    PrintN("   THE PORT IS A WINDOW. The address half stores what it is")
    PrintN("   given, so a zero read from the data half is a real zero and")
    PrintN("   section 11l's central measurement survives its first control.")
  Else
    PrintN("!! THE ADDRESS HALF DID NOT STORE. This port is not a window,")
    PrintN("   and EVERY statement since section 11l that the processor's")
    PrintN("   instruction memory is empty is measured through a broken")
    PrintN("   instrument. Nothing below this line is evidence about memory.")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  ; ---- 2. THE AUTO-INCREMENT CONTROL ----------------------------------
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_ADDR, 0)
  v = Rd32(#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_DATA)
  a = Rd32(#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_ADDR)
  Print("   after one data read from index 0 the address register reads ")
  PutHex8(a)
  PrintNl()
  If a = 1
    gDbgAutoInc = 1
    PrintN("   it auto-increments. Section 11l's six reads were six")
    PrintN("   different words, and its own caveat about that is closed.")
  Else
    gDbgAutoInc = 0
    PrintN("   it does NOT auto-increment. Section 11l read index 0 six")
    PrintN("   times over; its conclusion stands but its six values were")
    PrintN("   never independent evidence, exactly as it warned.")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  ; ---- 3. THE SWEEP ---------------------------------------------------
  nz = 0
  PrintN("   sweeping the window, address rewritten before every read:")
  For i = 0 To #SWEEP_COUNT - 1
    idx = SweepIndex(i)
    Wr32(#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_ADDR, idx)
    v = Rd32(#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_DATA)
    If v <> 0
      nz = nz + 1
    EndIf
    Print("     ucode[")
    PutHex8(idx)
    Print("] = ")
    PutHex8(v)
    If i < 6
      Print("      DRAM[")
      PutHex8(idx)
      Print("] = ")
      PutHex8(PeekN(gSqeBase + idx * 4) & $FFFFFFFF)
    EndIf
    PrintNl()
    Flush()
  Next
  gDbgNonZero = nz
  Print("   ")
  PrintDec(nz)
  Print(" of ")
  PrintDec(#SWEEP_COUNT)
  PrintN(" swept indices read non-zero.")
  If nz = 0
    PrintN("   NOTHING anywhere in the 128 KiB window. Read that together")
    PrintN("   with the address-register control above: a window that")
    PrintN("   stores its address and returns nothing is empty memory; a")
    PrintN("   window that does neither is a dead instrument.")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  ; remember index 0 by name, because it is the one that MUST hold the
  ; version word 017AA049 if the microcode ever arrived
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_ADDR, 0)
  zero = Rd32(#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_DATA)

  ; ---- 4. THE SECOND PORT ---------------------------------------------
  Print("S8w about to read CP_SQE_STAT, ")
  PrintDec(#GPU_CP_SQE_STAT_COUNT)
  Print(" words, through its own indexed port at ")
  PutHex8(#GPU_CP_SQE_STAT_ADDR)
  PrintNl()
  Flush()
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_SQE_STAT_ADDR, 0)
  a = Rd32(#GPU_CP_SQE_STAT_ADDR)
  Print("   its address register was handed 0 and reads back ")
  PutHex8(a)
  PrintNl()
  nz = 0
  For i = 0 To #GPU_CP_SQE_STAT_COUNT - 1
    Wr32(#GPU_CP_SQE_STAT_ADDR, i)
    v = Rd32(#GPU_CP_SQE_STAT_DATA)
    If v <> 0
      nz = nz + 1
      Print("     sqe_stat[")
      PutHex8(i)
      Print("] = ")
      PutHex8(v)
      PrintNl()
    EndIf
  Next
  gSqeStatNonZero = nz
  Print("   ")
  PrintDec(nz)
  Print(" of ")
  PrintDec(#GPU_CP_SQE_STAT_COUNT)
  PrintN(" CP_SQE_STAT words are non-zero (zeros not listed).")
  If nz > 0
    PrintN("   A SECOND indexed port on this processor answers with real")
    PrintN("   content. Indexed debug ports are readable by a bare-metal")
    PrintN("   caller on this part, which is the strongest form of the")
    PrintN("   control this section came for.")
  Else
    PrintN("   this port reads all zero too. Either the processor's state")
    PrintN("   really is blank, or NO indexed port answers us - and that")
    PrintN("   would be a larger finding than the one we came for.")
  EndIf
  Flush()
  ProcedureReturn zero
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE READ-ONLY QUEUE - section 11z, and the one witness this campaign
;  has never called.
;
;  WHY IT EXISTS. Section 11y settled what the four zeroed words of
;  instruction memory mean: starting the processor initialises them
;  itself. The zeros are not a fetch landing, so they are not evidence
;  that anything was ever requested from memory. That leaves the campaign
;  with a claim it has only ever supported NEGATIVELY - "no fault was
;  recorded, therefore no transaction was attempted" - on four registers
;  that all read zero (sGFSR, CB0_FSR, CP_HW_FAULT, RBBM_STATUS3 bit 24).
;
;  Absence of a fault is not presence of a fetch, and it is not absence
;  of one either. The ROQ is the POSITIVE instrument: it holds the dwords
;  the processor has pulled out of the ring buffer, after the read
;  completed and before the packets are parsed. Nothing puts data in
;  there except a completed read of our ring, in DRAM, through GBIF and
;  through the memory-management unit.
;
;    - our ring dwords appear  -> a transaction LEFT THE GPU, crossed the
;      IOMMU and came back with the right data. Every sentence in this
;      note about the processor never asking is wrong, the blocker is
;      downstream of the fetch, and milestone 2 is close.
;    - the queue is empty      -> the fetch really does not happen, and
;      the three held IOMMU bits of section 11v are the whole story.
;
;  Every access is a read except the write to the ADDRESS register, which
;  selects and does not command, and which is the same operation this
;  program has performed on two other ports across three clean runs.
;
;  THE WHOLE DECLARED DEPTH IS SWEPT. Section 11w cost this campaign a
;  retraction for reading six words of a 32,768-word window and calling
;  it empty; the depth here comes from the silicon's own
;  CP_ROQ_THRESHOLDS_2 rather than from a constant in this file.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i RoqWindow(*when)
  Define i.i
  Define v.i
  Define a.i
  Define nz.i
  Define hits.i
  Define thresh.i
  Define depth.i
  Define shown.i
  Define want.i
  Define j.i

  Print("   --- the read-only queue, ")
  Print(*when)
  PrintN(" ---")
  Flush()

  ; ---- 1. how deep is it, according to the hardware -------------------
  thresh = Rd32(#GPU_CP_ROQ_THRESH2)
  depth = (thresh >> #ROQ_SHIFT) & $FFFF
  Print("   CP_ROQ_THRESHOLDS_2 reads ")
  PutHex8(thresh)
  Print("  -> declared depth ")
  PrintDec(depth)
  PrintN(" dwords")
  If depth = 0 Or depth > #ROQ_DEPTH_MAX
    Print("!! that depth is not usable. Sweeping the fallback ")
    PrintDec(#ROQ_DEPTH_FALLBACK)
    PrintN(" instead, and the depth reading is itself a finding.")
    depth = #ROQ_DEPTH_FALLBACK
  EndIf
  gRoqDepth = depth
  Flush()

  ; ---- 2. THE ADDRESS-REGISTER CONTROL --------------------------------
  ;  Without this the sweep below is a rumour. This is the exact control
  ;  whose absence cost sections 11l through 11v.
  Print("S8q about to WRITE ")
  PutHex8(#ROQ_DBG_ADDR_PROBE)
  Print(" to CP_ROQ_DBG_ADDR at ")
  PutHex8(#GPU_CP_ROQ_DBG_ADDR)
  PrintNl()
  PrintN("   (if the board stops here, THIS is the instruction: the ROQ")
  PrintN("    port's address half may not be written from a bare-metal")
  PrintN("    caller, and the queue cannot be read at all.)")
  Flush()
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_ROQ_DBG_ADDR, #ROQ_DBG_ADDR_PROBE)
  a = Rd32(#GPU_CP_ROQ_DBG_ADDR)
  Print("   the address register was handed ")
  PutHex8(#ROQ_DBG_ADDR_PROBE)
  Print(" and reads back ")
  PutHex8(a)
  PrintNl()
  If a = #ROQ_DBG_ADDR_PROBE
    gRoqAddrOk = 1
    PrintN("   THE ROQ PORT IS A WINDOW. A zero read from its data half is")
    PrintN("   a real zero, so an empty queue below means an empty queue.")
  Else
    gRoqAddrOk = 0
    PrintN("!! the address half did not store the pattern. NOTHING read")
    PrintN("   from the data half below is evidence of anything - an empty")
    PrintN("   queue and a dead port look identical, and this run must not")
    PrintN("   be quoted as though it saw an empty queue.")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  ; ---- 3. the sweep, address rewritten before every read --------------
  nz = 0
  shown = 0
  For i = 0 To depth - 1
    Wr32(#GPU_CP_ROQ_DBG_ADDR, i)
    v = Rd32(#GPU_CP_ROQ_DBG_DATA)
    If v <> 0
      nz = nz + 1
      ; the transcript is finite; the count is the measurement and the
      ; first entries are the illustration
      If shown < 24
        shown = shown + 1
        Print("     roq[")
        PutHex8(i)
        Print("] = ")
        PutHex8(v)
        PrintNl()
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Next
  If nz > shown
    Print("     ... ")
    PrintDec(nz - shown)
    PrintN(" further non-zero entries not listed")
  EndIf
  Print("   ")
  PrintDec(nz)
  Print(" of ")
  PrintDec(depth)
  PrintN(" ROQ dwords are non-zero.")
  Flush()

  ; ---- 4. are they OUR dwords? ----------------------------------------
  ;  A non-zero queue is not the answer on its own - power-on state can
  ;  be non-zero, and section 11y established that the instruction memory
  ;  next door is exactly that. What decides it is whether the queue
  ;  holds the dwords THIS PROGRAM put in the ring, which no power-on
  ;  state can invent.
  hits = 0
  If gRingPos > 0
    For j = 0 To gRingPos - 1
      want = PeekN(gRingBase + j * 4) & $FFFFFFFF
      If want <> 0
        For i = 0 To depth - 1
          Wr32(#GPU_CP_ROQ_DBG_ADDR, i)
          v = Rd32(#GPU_CP_ROQ_DBG_DATA)
          If v = want
            hits = hits + 1
            Print("     ring dword ")
            PrintDec(j)
            Print(" = ")
            PutHex8(want)
            Print(" FOUND in the queue at roq[")
            PutHex8(i)
            PrintN("]")
            Break
          EndIf
        Next
      EndIf
    Next
    gRoqFoundRing = hits
    Print("   ")
    PrintDec(hits)
    Print(" of ")
    PrintDec(gRingPos)
    PrintN(" ring dwords were found in the read-only queue.")
    If hits > 0
      PrintN("   THE PROCESSOR FETCHED THE RING. A read left the GPU,")
      PrintN("   crossed the memory-management unit, reached DRAM and came")
      PrintN("   back with the right data. Every statement in this")
      PrintN("   campaign that the processor never asks for memory is")
      PrintN("   WRONG, and the blocker is downstream of the fetch.")
    Else
      PrintN("   none of them. Read together with the address-register")
      PrintN("   control above: a window that stores its address and holds")
      PrintN("   nothing of ours is a queue the processor never filled.")
    EndIf
  Else
    PrintN("   the ring is empty at this point, so there is nothing to")
    PrintN("   look for yet. This reading is the baseline.")
  EndIf
  Flush()
  ProcedureReturn nz
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 8. THE COMMAND PROCESSOR: microcode, ring, and the first packet.
;
;  This is the subset of the reference driver's initialisation that the
;  command processor needs in order to fetch and execute at all. It is
;  deliberately NOT the whole of it - no clock gating, no protection
;  ranges, no interrupts, none of the graphics blocks - because every
;  register written here is one more thing that can be wrong on the run
;  that first tries to make the processor tick.
;
;  Register protection is left OFF on purpose. Turning it on before the
;  processor is proven means the first mistake arrives as a protection
;  fault on hardware that cannot yet report anything.
; ======================================================================
Procedure Step8_Cp()
  Define n.i
  Define i.i
  Define waited.i
  Define rptr.i
  Define wptr.i
  Define hdr.i

  Say("--- S8: the command processor ---")
  gCpAlive = 0
  If gStop <> 0
    PrintN("   refused: an earlier step stopped.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If gSmmuBypass = 0
    ; ----------------------------------------------------------------
    ;  THIS USED TO REFUSE HERE. It now warns and continues, and the
    ;  reason is a measurement taken from the operating system.
    ;
    ;  The bare-metal reading says the IOMMU is translating with no
    ;  valid stream-match entry, which would mean the processor cannot
    ;  fetch. But Linux, on this same board, puts the GPU's IOMMU group
    ;  in IDENTITY mode - passthrough - and its graphics stack drives
    ;  this GPU perfectly. Both cannot be true of the same hardware.
    ;
    ;  The reconciliation is that the global registers are almost
    ;  certainly TRAPPED AND EMULATED by the layer that owns them: reads
    ;  return a sanitised view (all-zero stream table), writes are
    ;  silently dropped, and the real routing is elsewhere and invisible
    ;  to us. That also explains why the CONTEXT banks accept writes
    ;  while the global space does not - trapping global space and
    ;  handing out context banks is exactly how such a thing is built.
    ;
    ;  So the all-zero stream table is not evidence that the GPU cannot
    ;  fetch. It is evidence that we cannot SEE whether it can.
    ;
    ;  WHY IT IS SAFE TO JUST TRY. An IOMMU translation fault is the
    ;  GPU's transaction failing, not the processor's. The CPU is not the
    ;  faulting master, so it does not stall - unlike the unclocked-block
    ;  reads that cost this campaign its power cycles. The worst case is
    ;  a read pointer that never moves, and the fault registers read
    ;  afterwards say whether that was a translation fault or something
    ;  else. Every wait below is bounded.
    ; ----------------------------------------------------------------
    PrintN("!! the IOMMU reads as translating with no stream entry, which")
    PrintN("   would mean the processor cannot fetch. CONTINUING ANYWAY, on")
    PrintN("   purpose: the operating system puts this GPU's group in")
    PrintN("   IDENTITY mode on this same board and drives it fine, so the")
    PrintN("   all-zero table we can see is most likely an emulated view")
    PrintN("   rather than the truth. A translation fault stalls the GPU,")
    PrintN("   not this processor, so trying costs a timeout and not a")
    PrintN("   power cycle. The fault registers are read afterwards.")
    Flush()
  EndIf

  ; ---- align the buffers -------------------------------------------
  gSqeBase    = (@gSqe[0] + $FFF) & (-4096)
  gRingBase   = (@gRing[0] + $7FFF) & (-32768)
  gRptrShadow = (@gAux[0] + $FFF) & (-4096)
  gMarker     = gRptrShadow + 64

  ; ---- PROVE EVERY ALIGNED BLOCK STILL FITS INSIDE ITS ARRAY -------
  ;  Aligning a base upward eats space, and if the array was sized for
  ;  the payload alone the tail runs off the end into other globals.
  ;  That happened here: the ring array was 40960 for a 32768 ring
  ;  aligned to 32768, and on a bad placement the ring started so late
  ;  that clearing it wrote 24 KiB past the array. The only visible
  ;  symptom was a transcript that began with a few hundred NUL bytes,
  ;  which reads like a corrupt file rather than a bug in this program.
  ;  Cheap to check, and it turns a silent corruption into a refusal.
  If (gRingBase + #RING_BYTES) > (@gRing[0] + 65536)
    Halt(83, "the ring buffer does not fit inside its array after alignment. Aligning the base upward consumed more of the array than the slack allowed, so clearing the ring would write past the end and over other globals. Raise the array to the ring size plus the alignment minus one.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If (gSqeBase + #SQE_LOAD_LEN) > (@gSqe[0] + 32768)
    Halt(83, "the microcode does not fit inside its array after alignment. Same fault as the ring: raise the array to the payload plus the alignment minus one.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If (gMarker + 64) > (@gAux[0] + 8192)
    Halt(83, "the auxiliary block does not fit inside its array after alignment.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  PrintN("   every aligned block verified inside its own array.")
  Print("   microcode at ")
  PutAddr(gSqeBase)
  Print("   ring at ")
  PutAddr(gRingBase)
  PrintNl()
  Print("   read-pointer shadow at ")
  PutAddr(gRptrShadow)
  Print("   marker at ")
  PutAddr(gMarker)
  PrintNl()
  Flush()

  ; ---- read the microcode off the boot partition --------------------
  Say("   S8.1 reading the microcode:")
  n = QEspRead("\EFI\anvil\a702_sqe.fw", @gSqe[0] + 4096, 32768 - 4096)
  Print("   read ")
  PrintDec(n)
  PrintN(" bytes")
  If n <> #SQE_FILE_LEN
    Halt(81, "the microcode is not the length this program was written against. Check that the repository copy - version 0.49, 21652 bytes - was staged onto the boot partition, and not the operating system's own copy, which is version 0.47 and 20388 bytes. The two differ in their jump table and a run must say which it used.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  Print("   version word = ")
  PutHex8(PeekN(@gSqe[0] + 4096 + 4) & $FFFFFFFF)
  ; the version nibbles are HEX. Printing them as decimal turned 0x049
  ; into "0.73" in the transcript - a wrong answer about which firmware
  ; was loaded, in the one line whose whole job is saying which firmware
  ; was loaded.
  Print("  -> version 0.")
  PutHexN((PeekN(@gSqe[0] + 4096 + 4) & $FFF), 3)
  PrintNl()
  PrintN("   (the FIRST dword of the file is not microcode and is skipped -")
  PrintN("    loading the file verbatim puts the whole image one dword out)")
  Flush()

  ; move the microcode down to its aligned base, minus the 4-byte header
  i = 0
  While i < #SQE_LOAD_LEN
    PokeB(gSqeBase + i, PeekB(@gSqe[0] + 4096 + #SQE_SKIP + i) & $FF)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  DcCleanRange(gSqeBase, gSqeBase + #SQE_LOAD_LEN + 64)
  PrintN("   microcode staged and cleaned to the point of coherency.")

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  PROVE THE COPY. Everything after this assumes the right bytes are
  ;  at the right address, and the previous run reported an illegal
  ;  instruction - which is exactly what a wrong copy looks like.
  ;
  ;  The reference driver's own version check reads the FIRST DWORD OF
  ;  THE LOADED BUFFER (a6xx_gpu.c:979-1046, reading buf[0] of the object
  ;  it created from fw->data+4). So the first dword here must be the
  ;  file's SECOND dword. If it is the file's first - zero - the skip did
  ;  not happen and the whole image is one word out.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Print("   loaded image at ")
  PutAddr(gSqeBase)
  PrintN(" begins:")
  For i = 0 To 3
    Print("     [")
    PrintDec(i)
    Print("] = ")
    PutHex8(PeekN(gSqeBase + i * 4) & $FFFFFFFF)
    PrintNl()
  Next
  PrintN("   expected: 017AA049 010014A4 88020001 A8020080")
  If (PeekN(gSqeBase) & $FFFFFFFF) = $017AA049
    PrintN("   the first dword is the version word, so the 4-byte skip took")
    PrintN("   and the image is where the processor was told it is.")
  Else
    Halt(82, "the loaded microcode does not begin with the version word 017AA049. Either the 4-byte header skip did not happen, or the copy landed somewhere other than the address written to the processor. Everything downstream would be meaningless, so nothing further was touched.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  Flush()

  ; ---- clear the ring and the markers -------------------------------
  i = 0
  While i < #RING_BYTES
    PokeN(gRingBase + i, 0)
    i = i + 4
  Wend
  PokeN(gRptrShadow, 0)
  PokeN(gMarker, 0)
  gRingPos = 0

  ; ---- the initialisation the processor needs -----------------------
  ; ==================================================================
  ;  S8.2 - SECTION 11ab. Every write here is now READ BACK.
  ;
  ;  This block has made twenty-eight writes on every run since M2-R and
  ;  printed one line about all of them. Section 11aa narrowed the
  ;  failure to the SQE's own microcode fetch - the GPU's FIRST memory
  ;  transaction - and every register that decides whether that
  ;  transaction can happen, and with what address width, is written
  ;  here and has never been checked.
  ; ==================================================================
  gCfgWrites = 0
  gCfgTook   = 0
  gCfgZero   = 0
  gCfgOther  = 0
  gCfgAddrMode = 0
  Say("   S8.2 the register init the processor needs - EVERY WRITE IS")
  Say("   READ BACK THIS RUN (section 11ab). Twenty-eight writes have")
  Say("   been made here since M2-R and none has ever been checked.")

  ; ---- the GBIF halt pair, and its acknowledgements -----------------
  ; The reference driver clears these FIRST, at the very top of hw_init
  ; (a6xx_gpu.c:1078-1091), for every part that has GBIF. They decide
  ; whether GPU-initiated memory transactions may leave the block at
  ; all, and a halted interface drops them SILENTLY - no fault register
  ; anywhere records it. That is exactly the signature 11aa describes.
  Announce(8, "GBIF_HALT_ACK (new this build)", #GPU_GBIF_HALT_ACK)
  gGbifAckBefore = Rd32(#GPU_GBIF_HALT_ACK)
  Got("GBIF_HALT_ACK before", gGbifAckBefore)
  gRbbmAckBefore = Rd32(#GPU_RBBM_GBIF_HALT_ACK)
  Got("RBBM_GBIF_HALT_ACK before", gRbbmAckBefore)

  gRbbmGbifHaltGot = CfgWr("RBBM_GBIF_HALT     ", #GPU_RBBM_GBIF_HALT, 0)
  gGbifHaltGot     = CfgWr("GBIF_HALT          ", #GPU_GBIF_HALT, 0)

  gGbifAckAfter = Rd32(#GPU_GBIF_HALT_ACK)
  Got("GBIF_HALT_ACK after", gGbifAckAfter)
  gRbbmAckAfter = Rd32(#GPU_RBBM_GBIF_HALT_ACK)
  Got("RBBM_GBIF_HALT_ACK after", gRbbmAckAfter)
  Say("   The ack pair is an OBSERVATION, not a verdict. The reference")
  Say("   driver only reads it to confirm a halt it REQUESTED, and")
  Say("   mesa's crash dump shows HALT 0 with ACK 3, so a non-zero ack")
  Say("   beside a zero halt is a state the hardware reaches. Only the")
  Say("   two halt registers' own read-back above is allowed to decide.")

  CfgWr("SECVID_TSB_CNTL    ", #GPU_SECVID_TSB_CNTL, 0)
  CfgWr("SECVID_TSB_TR_BASE ", #GPU_SECVID_TSB_TR_BASE, 0)
  CfgWr("SECVID_TSB_TR_BAS+4", #GPU_SECVID_TSB_TR_BASE + 4, 0)
  CfgWr("SECVID_TSB_TR_SIZE ", #GPU_SECVID_TSB_TR_SIZE, 0)

  ; ---- 64-bit addressing for every block ----------------------------
  ; NOT OPTIONAL HERE, and this is the candidate section 11ab names
  ; first: the firmware placed our buffers ABOVE 4 GB, the microcode
  ; sits at 0x1_0A1F8000, and a block left in 32-bit mode fetches from
  ; 0x0A1F8000 instead - a valid-looking address holding something
  ; else, no fault, junk in the instruction memory, and an illegal
  ; instruction one millisecond after the start bit. That is the M2-W
  ; transcript exactly.
  CfgWrAddrMode("CP_ADDR_MODE_CNTL  ", #GPU_CP_ADDR_MODE_CNTL)
  CfgWrAddrMode("VSC_ADDR_MODE_CNTL ", #GPU_VSC_ADDR_MODE)
  CfgWrAddrMode("GRAS_ADDR_MODE_CNTL", #GPU_GRAS_ADDR_MODE)
  CfgWrAddrMode("RB_ADDR_MODE_CNTL  ", #GPU_RB_ADDR_MODE)
  CfgWrAddrMode("PC_ADDR_MODE_CNTL  ", #GPU_PC_ADDR_MODE)
  CfgWrAddrMode("HLSQ_ADDR_MODE_CNTL", #GPU_HLSQ_ADDR_MODE)
  CfgWrAddrMode("VFD_ADDR_MODE_CNTL ", #GPU_VFD_ADDR_MODE)
  CfgWrAddrMode("VPC_ADDR_MODE_CNTL ", #GPU_VPC_ADDR_MODE)
  CfgWrAddrMode("UCHE_ADDR_MODE_CNTL", #GPU_UCHE_ADDR_MODE)
  CfgWrAddrMode("SP_ADDR_MODE_CNTL  ", #GPU_SP_ADDR_MODE)
  CfgWrAddrMode("TPL1_ADDR_MODE_CNTL", #GPU_TPL1_ADDR_MODE)
  CfgWrAddrMode("SECVID_TSB_ADDR_MOD", #GPU_SECVID_TSB_ADDR_MODE)

  ; the a610-family bus settings and the a702 fifo sizes
  CfgWr("GBIF_QSB_SIDE0     ", #GPU_GBIF_QSB_SIDE0, $00071620)
  CfgWr("GBIF_QSB_SIDE1     ", #GPU_GBIF_QSB_SIDE0 + 4, $00071620)
  CfgWr("GBIF_QSB_SIDE2     ", #GPU_GBIF_QSB_SIDE0 + 8, $00071620)
  CfgWr("GBIF_QSB_SIDE3     ", #GPU_GBIF_QSB_SIDE0 + 12, $00071620)
  CfgWr("RBBM_GBIF_QOS_CNTL ", #GPU_RBBM_GBIF_QOS_CNTL, 3)

  CfgWr("UCHE_WR_RANGE_MAX  ", #GPU_UCHE_WRITE_RANGE_MAX, $FFFFFFC0)
  CfgWr("UCHE_WR_RANGE_MAX+4", #GPU_UCHE_WRITE_RANGE_MAX + 4, $0001FFFF)
  CfgWr("UCHE_TRAP_BASE     ", #GPU_UCHE_TRAP_BASE, $FFFFF000)
  CfgWr("UCHE_TRAP_BASE+4   ", #GPU_UCHE_TRAP_BASE + 4, $0001FFFF)
  CfgWr("UCHE_WRITE_THRU    ", #GPU_UCHE_WRITE_THRU_BASE, $FFFFF000)
  CfgWr("UCHE_WRITE_THRU+4  ", #GPU_UCHE_WRITE_THRU_BASE + 4, $0001FFFF)
  CfgWr("UCHE_FILTER_CNTL   ", #GPU_UCHE_FILTER_CNTL, $804)
  CfgWr("UCHE_CACHE_WAYS    ", #GPU_UCHE_CACHE_WAYS, 4)
  CfgWr("UCHE_CLIENT_PF     ", #GPU_UCHE_CLIENT_PF, $81)

  Print("   S8.2 read-back tally: ")
  PrintDec(gCfgTook)
  Print(" took, ")
  PrintDec(gCfgZero)
  Print(" read zero, ")
  PrintDec(gCfgOther)
  Print(" neither, of ")
  PrintDec(gCfgWrites)
  PrintN(" writes")
  Print("   of the twelve ADDR_MODE_CNTL registers, ")
  PrintDec(gCfgAddrMode)
  PrintN(" read back 1")
  ; THIS BRANCH WAS WRONG ON THE M2-X RUN AND THE FIX IS THE POINT.
  ; It used to fire on "fewer than twelve read back 1" and announce
  ; "AT LEAST ONE BLOCK IS STILL IN 32-BIT ADDRESSING MODE ... this is
  ; the answer". That merges the two cases section 11ab deliberately kept
  ; apart: a register that READ BACK ZERO (the write did not take, which
  ; IS evidence) and one whose READ DID NOT LAND (which is evidence of
  ; nothing). On M2-X the two shortfalls were both DEAFBEAD, and the
  ; summary dressed an undecided reading up as a finding in the loudest
  ; voice available - inside the very build whose section pre-registered
  ; a row to stop exactly that. Undecided is now reported as undecided.
  If gCfgZero > 0
    Say("!! A BLOCK IS STILL IN 32-BIT ADDRESSING MODE - a register that")
    Say("   must read 1 read back ZERO, and our buffers are above 4 GB.")
    Say("   The fetch goes to the wrong address, returns something, and")
    Say("   the processor rejects it. Nothing faults. That is a finding.")
  ElseIf gCfgAddrMode < 12
    Say("   fewer than twelve confirmed, but NONE read back zero - the")
    Say("   shortfall is reads that did not land, not writes that were")
    Say("   refused. UNDECIDED for those registers, and undecided is all")
    Say("   it is. See section 11ac before quoting this run.")
  EndIf
  If gGbifHaltGot <> 0 Or gRbbmGbifHaltGot <> 0
    Say("!! A GBIF HALT REGISTER WILL NOT CLEAR. Every GPU-initiated")
    Say("   memory transaction is blocked at the block's own bus")
    Say("   interface, silently and with no fault anywhere - which is")
    Say("   the signature section 11aa describes. Who holds it is the")
    Say("   next question, and it joins the IOMMU's three held bits.")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  Wr32(#GPU_CP_ROQ_THRESH2, $00800060)
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_ROQ_THRESH1, $40201B16)
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_MEM_POOL_SIZE, 64)
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_MEM_POOL_DBG, 63)
  Wr32(#GPU_PC_DBG_ECO_CNTL, $0000C000)
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_AHB_CNTL, 1)
  Wr32(#GPU_RBBM_PERFCTR_CNTL, 1)
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_CHICKEN_DBG, 1 << 24)
  Wr32(#GPU_RBBM_HANG_INT_CNTL, $4003FFFF)

  ; expanded privilege - this part has the quirk, so the processor may
  ; use the hardware read-pointer shadow
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_APRIV_CNTL, $6E)

  ; protection deliberately OFF, see the header
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_PROTECT_CNTL, 0)
  PrintN("   done. Register protection is deliberately left OFF for this run.")
  Flush()

  ; ---- point the processor at the microcode and the ring ------------
  Say("   S8.3 microcode base, ring base, ring control:")
  Print("S8 about to WRITE the microcode base ")
  PutAddr(gSqeBase)
  Print(" to ")
  PutHex8(#GPU_CP_SQE_INSTR_BASE)
  PrintNl()
  Flush()
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_SQE_INSTR_BASE, gSqeBase & $FFFFFFFF)
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_SQE_INSTR_BASE + 4, (gSqeBase >> 32) & $FFFFFFFF)

  Wr32(#GPU_CP_RB_BASE, gRingBase & $FFFFFFFF)
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_RB_BASE + 4, (gRingBase >> 32) & $FFFFFFFF)
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_RB_RPTR_ADDR, gRptrShadow & $FFFFFFFF)
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_RB_RPTR_ADDR + 4, (gRptrShadow >> 32) & $FFFFFFFF)
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_RB_CNTL, #RB_CNTL_VALUE)

  ; READ THEM BACK. A 64-bit base is two separate registers and the high
  ; word is the one that gets forgotten - and these buffers are ABOVE
  ; 4 GB, so a missing high word points the processor at a completely
  ; different address with no complaint from anything.
  Print("   CP_SQE_INSTR_BASE reads back ")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPU_CP_SQE_INSTR_BASE + 4))
  Print(":")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPU_CP_SQE_INSTR_BASE))
  Print("   (wrote ")
  PutAddr(gSqeBase)
  PrintN(")")
  Print("   CP_RB_BASE        reads back ")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPU_CP_RB_BASE + 4))
  Print(":")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPU_CP_RB_BASE))
  Print("   (wrote ")
  PutAddr(gRingBase)
  PrintN(")")
  Print("   CP_RB_RPTR_ADDR   reads back ")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPU_CP_RB_RPTR_ADDR + 4))
  Print(":")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPU_CP_RB_RPTR_ADDR))
  PrintNl()
  Print("   CP_APRIV_CNTL     reads back ")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPU_CP_APRIV_CNTL))
  PrintN("   (wrote 0000006E)")
  Print("   CP_RB_CNTL = ")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPU_CP_RB_CNTL))
  Print("   CP_RB_RPTR = ")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPU_CP_RB_RPTR))
  Print("   CP_RB_WPTR = ")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPU_CP_RB_WPTR))
  PrintNl()
  Flush()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  SECTION 11w, READING ONE OF THREE. The window is read BEFORE the
  ;  start bit is written. Two things can come out of it and both are
  ;  worth the four hundred microseconds it costs:
  ;
  ;   - it is already non-zero. Then something loaded this processor
  ;     before us - the firmware, or the secure world when the zap
  ;     authenticated - and the load is not ours to do at all.
  ;   - it is zero. Then the "after" reading has a baseline, which no
  ;     run in this campaign has ever had. Until now every statement of
  ;     the form "the load did not happen" has compared one reading
  ;     against an assumption about what came before it.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  gUcode0Before = SqeWindow("BEFORE the start bit")

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  SECTION 11x. IS THE INSTRUCTION MEMORY WRITABLE FROM HERE?
  ;
  ;  M2-U established that the debug port is a real window: its address
  ;  half stores what it is given and auto-increments, and its data half
  ;  returns content that is not all zero. It reads.
  ;
  ;  Whether it WRITES is a different question and a much bigger one. If
  ;  it does, the microcode does not have to be fetched by the graphics
  ;  block at all - the processor can push all 5,412 words in over the
  ;  configuration bus, which never goes through GBIF and therefore never
  ;  meets the memory-management unit whose three held bits section 11v
  ;  measured. That is how every Adreno before this generation was
  ;  loaded, one word at a time from the CPU.
  ;
  ;  THE RISK, because it is a write to a register whose write behaviour
  ;  is not in any source available here - the reference driver only ever
  ;  reads it:
  ;
  ;    - the block is powered, clocked and PROVEN ALIVE. The scratch
  ;      register takes A5C3F096 and gives it back on every run. This is
  ;      not the unclocked-block access that cost this campaign its power
  ;      cycles.
  ;    - CP_AHB_CNTL was set to 1 in S8.2, which makes the configuration
  ;      bus answer an out-of-range access with an ERROR rather than a
  ;      stall. That is precisely the register whose absence turns a bad
  ;      access into a dead board.
  ;    - register protection is off, so it cannot raise a protection
  ;      fault.
  ;    - it is not the secure-mode register, and the emitted assembly is
  ;      checked for that separately.
  ;    - the worst realistic outcome is a command processor executing
  ;      rubbish, which is what it is already doing.
  ;
  ;  The write is announced and the transcript flushed BEFORE it happens,
  ;  so if this is the instruction that kills the board, the last line of
  ;  the file names it.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("   S8.3b section 11x - can the CPU write instruction memory?")
  Print("S8x about to WRITE ")
  PutHex8(#SQE_DBG_DATA_PAT0)
  Print(" to instruction memory index 0 through CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_DATA at ")
  PutHex8(#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_DATA)
  PrintNl()
  PrintN("   (this register has never been written by this program or by")
  PrintN("    the reference driver. If the board stops here, THIS is the")
  PrintN("    instruction, and the answer is that it may not be written.)")
  Flush()
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_ADDR, 0)
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_DATA, #SQE_DBG_DATA_PAT0)
  PrintN("   it did not stop. Writing index 1 as well.")
  Flush()
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_ADDR, 1)
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_DATA, #SQE_DBG_DATA_PAT1)

  ; read both back with the address rewritten before each read, so
  ; whatever the auto-increment does cannot be mistaken for a result
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_ADDR, 0)
  gImemWrote = Rd32(#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_DATA)
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_ADDR, 1)
  gImemWrote1 = Rd32(#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_DATA)
  Print("   index 0: wanted ")
  PutHex8(#SQE_DBG_DATA_PAT0)
  Print("  found ")
  PutHex8(gImemWrote)
  Print("   index 1: wanted ")
  PutHex8(#SQE_DBG_DATA_PAT1)
  Print("  found ")
  PutHex8(gImemWrote1)
  PrintNl()
  Print("   (before this write they held ")
  PutHex8(gUcode0Before)
  PrintN(" and 04801180 on the M2-U run)")
  If gImemWrote = #SQE_DBG_DATA_PAT0 And gImemWrote1 = #SQE_DBG_DATA_PAT1
    PrintN("   THE PORT WRITES. The command processor's instruction memory")
    PrintN("   is loadable from the CPU over the configuration bus, which")
    PrintN("   never passes through the memory-management unit. The whole")
    PrintN("   microcode can be pushed in this way and the next build does")
    PrintN("   exactly that.")
  ElseIf gImemWrote = 0 And gImemWrote1 = 0
    PrintN("!! the port took an address and discarded the data - the same")
    PrintN("   shape as the three held bits of the memory-management unit.")
  Else
    PrintN("!! the data half is read-only. No route this way; the M2-U")
    PrintN("   reading stands as a pure observation.")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  ; ---- start the microcode ------------------------------------------
  Say("   S8.4 starting the microcode:")
  Print("S8 about to WRITE 1 to CP_SQE_CNTL at ")
  PutHex8(#GPU_CP_SQE_CNTL)
  PrintN("   (this is the instruction that sets the processor running)")
  Flush()
  ; one last clean immediately before the processor is let loose. The
  ; region has not been touched since the earlier one, so this is belt
  ; and braces rather than a fix - but the cost is microseconds and the
  ; failure it would hide costs a bench trip.
  DcCleanRange(gSqeBase, gSqeBase + #SQE_LOAD_LEN + 64)

  ; SECTION 11z. The reading immediately before the start bit. Together
  ; with the cold one at step 5 and the one a millisecond later, this
  ; makes the interrupt status a TIMELINE instead of a single number
  ; quoted at the end of the run.
  gIntStatPreStart = Rd32(#GPU_CP_INTERRUPT_STAT)
  gRbbmPreStart = Rd32(#GPU_RBBM_STATUS)
  Print("   immediately BEFORE the start bit: CP_INTERRUPT_STATUS = ")
  PutHex8(gIntStatPreStart)
  Print("   RBBM_STATUS = ")
  PutHex8(gRbbmPreStart)
  PrintNl()
  Flush()

  Wr32(#GPU_CP_SQE_CNTL, 1)
  QEfiStall(1000)
  Print("   RBBM_STATUS = ")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPU_RBBM_STATUS))
  Print("   CP_SQE_CNTL = ")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPU_CP_SQE_CNTL))
  PrintNl()

  ; SECTION 11z. One millisecond after the start bit and BEFORE the ring
  ; is handed over. No run has ever read this register between those two
  ; writes, so "the illegal-instruction bit" has never been attributable
  ; to either of them.
  gIntStat1ms = Rd32(#GPU_CP_INTERRUPT_STAT)
  Print("   one millisecond after the start bit: CP_INTERRUPT_STATUS = ")
  PutHex8(gIntStat1ms)
  PrintNl()
  If gIntStat1ms <> gIntStatPreStart
    PrintN("   IT CHANGED ON THE START BIT. The command processor began")
    PrintN("   executing when it was started - before it was given any")
    PrintN("   ring at all - and what it executed it rejected. The SQE")
    PrintN("   runs; what it runs is not our microcode.")
  Else
    PrintN("   unchanged by the start bit. Whatever this register holds,")
    PrintN("   writing CP_SQE_CNTL did not cause it.")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  WHAT DOES THE PROCESSOR ACTUALLY HOLD?
  ;
  ;  This is the measurement the previous run needed and did not have.
  ;  The illegal-instruction error says the processor executed something
  ;  that was not a valid instruction - but it does not say whether it
  ;  read the right memory and the memory was wrong, or read the wrong
  ;  memory, or read nothing and got zeros.
  ;
  ;  The debug port walks the processor's own instruction memory. If it
  ;  reads back the same words that are in DRAM, the fetch path works and
  ;  the fault is elsewhere. If it reads zeros or rubbish while DRAM is
  ;  correct, the fetch is the problem - and that would point straight
  ;  back at the addressing or the cache, whatever the fault registers
  ;  claim.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  SECTION 11w, READING TWO OF THREE. This is where every previous run
  ;  took its ONE reading. It is kept at the same point in the sequence
  ;  so the number is comparable, and it now arrives with a baseline
  ;  before it, a control beside it and a later reading after it.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  SECTION 11x, THE SECOND HALF, and it is the part that decides
  ;  between the two candidates M2-U left open.
  ;
  ;  M2-U saw exactly four words of instruction memory - indices 0 to 3,
  ;  which is one 128-bit bus beat - go from content to ZERO the moment
  ;  the start bit was written. Two things explain that and this run can
  ;  tell them apart, because index 0 now holds a pattern the hardware
  ;  cannot have invented:
  ;
  ;    - the pattern is GONE  -> something inside the processor writes
  ;      index 0 when it starts. With one beat of zeros. That is a fetch
  ;      that returned nothing, and "no fault was recorded, so the
  ;      processor never asked" (section 11v) is wrong.
  ;    - the pattern SURVIVES -> nothing wrote index 0. The four zeroed
  ;      words in M2-U were not written by any fetch, and starting the
  ;      processor simply clears its first words.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_ADDR, 0)
  gImemAfterStart = Rd32(#GPU_CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_DATA)
  Print("   S8x index 0 held ")
  PutHex8(gImemWrote)
  Print(" before the start bit and reads ")
  PutHex8(gImemAfterStart)
  PrintN(" after it")
  If gImemWrote = #SQE_DBG_DATA_PAT0
    If gImemAfterStart = #SQE_DBG_DATA_PAT0
      PrintN("   THE PATTERN SURVIVED. Nothing wrote instruction memory when")
      PrintN("   the processor started, so the four zeroed words on the M2-U")
      PrintN("   run were not written by any fetch. Starting the processor")
      PrintN("   clears its first words, and the fetch really does not")
      PrintN("   happen - section 11v's reading of the fault registers")
      PrintN("   stands and the question is why nothing is ever requested.")
    ElseIf gImemAfterStart = 0
      PrintN("   THE PATTERN WAS OVERWRITTEN WITH ZERO. Something inside the")
      PrintN("   processor wrote index 0 when it started, and what it wrote")
      PrintN("   was nothing. That is a fetch that came back empty, so 'no")
      PrintN("   fault was recorded, therefore the processor never asked' is")
      PrintN("   WRONG - a terminated read that returns zeros records no")
      PrintN("   fault and looks exactly like this.")
    Else
      PrintN("   the pattern was replaced by something else again - neither")
      PrintN("   outcome this run decided in advance. Read the body.")
    EndIf
  Else
    PrintN("   (the write did not take, so this reading decides nothing.)")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  Say("   what the processor actually holds, read through its debug port:")
  gUcode0After = SqeWindow("one millisecond AFTER the start bit")
  PrintN("   matching pairs mean the fetch path works and the fault is")
  PrintN("   elsewhere; zeros or rubbish on the left mean it never read")
  PrintN("   what we wrote - PROVIDED the address-register control above")
  PrintN("   says the port is a window at all.")
  Flush()

  ; SECTION 11z. The queue BEFORE the ring exists - the baseline that
  ; says what the ROQ holds at power-on, so that anything found in it
  ; after the hand-over can be attributed to the hand-over.
  gRoqNzStart = RoqWindow("one millisecond AFTER the start bit, ring not yet built")

  ; ---- build the first packets --------------------------------------
  Say("   S8.5 building the first packets the processor will ever run:")

  ; CP_ME_INIT exactly as a6xx_cp_init() sends it (a6xx_gpu.c:895-922)
  hdr = Pkt7(#CP_ME_INIT, 8)
  Print("   CP_ME_INIT header = ")
  PutHex8(hdr)
  PrintNl()
  RingPut(hdr)
  RingPut($0000002F)
  RingPut($00000003)
  RingPut($20000000)
  RingPut($00000000)
  RingPut($00000000)
  RingPut($00000000)
  RingPut($00000000)
  RingPut($00000000)

  ; leaving secure mode is the PROCESSOR's job on a board with a signed
  ; image, not the CPU's - the register is never written at all
  hdr = Pkt7(#CP_SET_SECURE_MODE, 1)
  Print("   CP_SET_SECURE_MODE header = ")
  PutHex8(hdr)
  PrintNl()
  RingPut(hdr)
  RingPut($00000000)

  ; and the marker: the processor writes a value into memory that the
  ; processor cannot fake and the CPU then reads back
  hdr = Pkt7(#CP_MEM_WRITE, 3)
  Print("   CP_MEM_WRITE header = ")
  PutHex8(hdr)
  Print("  marker ")
  PutHex8(#MARKER_VALUE)
  Print(" -> ")
  PutAddr(gMarker)
  PrintNl()
  RingPut(hdr)
  RingPut(gMarker & $FFFFFFFF)
  RingPut((gMarker >> 32) & $FFFFFFFF)
  RingPut(#MARKER_VALUE)

  ; pad to the 32-byte block the ring control register declares
  While (gRingPos & 7) <> 0
    RingPut(Pkt7(#CP_NOP, 0))
  Wend

  Print("   ring holds ")
  PrintDec(gRingPos)
  PrintN(" dwords")
  DcCleanRange(gRingBase, gRingBase + gRingPos * 4 + 64)
  DcCleanRange(gRptrShadow, gRptrShadow + 128)
  Flush()

  ; ---- and let it go -------------------------------------------------
  Say("   S8.6 handing the packets to the processor:")
  wptr = gRingPos
  Print("S8 about to WRITE write-pointer ")
  PrintDec(wptr)
  Print(" to CP_RB_WPTR at ")
  PutHex8(#GPU_CP_RB_WPTR)
  PrintNl()
  Flush()
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_RB_WPTR, wptr)

  waited = 0
  rptr = 0
  While waited < 500000
    DcCleanRange(gRptrShadow, gRptrShadow + 64)
    rptr = PeekN(gRptrShadow) & $FFFFFFFF
    gRptrSeen = rptr
    If rptr >= wptr
      Break
    EndIf
    QEfiStall(1000)
    waited = waited + 1000
  Wend

  Print("   read-pointer shadow = ")
  PrintDec(rptr)
  Print("  register CP_RB_RPTR = ")
  PrintDec(Rd32(#GPU_CP_RB_RPTR))
  Print("  write-pointer = ")
  PrintDec(wptr)
  Print("  after ")
  PrintDec(waited)
  PrintN(" us")
  Print("   RBBM_STATUS = ")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPU_RBBM_STATUS))
  Print("  CP_HW_FAULT = ")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPU_CP_HW_FAULT))
  Print("  CP_INTERRUPT_STATUS = ")
  gIntStatAfter = Rd32(#GPU_CP_INTERRUPT_STAT)
  PutHex8(gIntStatAfter)
  PrintNl()
  Print("   the interrupt-status timeline: cold ")
  PutHex8(gIntStatS5)
  Print("  pre-start ")
  PutHex8(gIntStatPreStart)
  Print("  at 1 ms ")
  PutHex8(gIntStat1ms)
  Print("  after the ring ")
  PutHex8(gIntStatAfter)
  PrintNl()
  If gIntStatS5 = gIntStatAfter
    PrintN("!! IT NEVER MOVED. This register reads the same cold as it")
    PrintN("   does after the whole run, so it is power-on state and it")
    PrintN("   is not evidence of anything the run did. Every earlier")
    PrintN("   section that quoted CP_ILLEGAL_INSTR_ERROR as a result of")
    PrintN("   the run is corrected by this line.")
  Else
    PrintN("   IT MOVED, so the run caused it, and the point in the")
    PrintN("   timeline where it changed names which write did.")
  EndIf
  Print("   RBBM_INT_0_STATUS = ")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPU_RBBM_INT_0_STATUS))
  PrintNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  THE GPU'S OWN ANSWER TO THE QUESTION FIVE ROUNDS HAVE INFERRED.
  ;
  ;  RBBM_STATUS3 bit 24 is SMMU_STALLED_ON_FAULT (a6xx.xml:582). This
  ;  campaign has spent since section 11g deducing whether the graphics
  ;  block is stalled on a translation fault by reading the IOMMU's fault
  ;  registers from the other side of the interface. The GPU has a bit
  ;  that just says so, and nobody has read it after a failure.
  ;
  ;  Set, and section 11v's "the processor is not asking" is wrong on the
  ;  GPU's own evidence, sGFSR notwithstanding. Clear, and that finding
  ;  gains a second independent witness.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  gStatus3After = Rd32(#GPU_RBBM_STATUS3)
  Print("   RBBM_STATUS3 = ")
  PutHex8(gStatus3After)
  Print("   SMMU_STALLED_ON_FAULT (bit 24) = ")
  PrintDec(BitOf(gStatus3After, 24))
  PrintNl()
  If BitOf(gStatus3After, 24) = 1
    PrintN("   THE GPU SAYS IT IS STALLED ON A TRANSLATION FAULT. It did")
    PrintN("   ask for memory and it was refused, whatever sGFSR reads.")
  Else
    PrintN("   the GPU does not report a translation stall.")
  EndIf
  Print("   CP_PROTECT_STATUS = ")
  PutHex8(Rd32(#GPU_CP_PROTECT_STATUS))
  PrintN("   (register protection is off this run, so this must be zero)")
  Flush()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  SECTION 11z, THE DECIDING READING. The ring has been handed over
  ;  and half a second has passed. If the command processor ever fetched
  ;  it, the dwords are in the read-only queue. This is the only POSITIVE
  ;  test for a completed transaction this program has ever run -
  ;  everything before it has been fault registers reading zero, which
  ;  cannot tell "nothing was asked" from "something was asked and
  ;  nothing recorded it".
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  gRoqNzWptr = RoqWindow("after the ring was handed over and the 500 ms wait")

  If rptr >= wptr
    gCpAlive = 1
    PrintN("   THE READ POINTER REACHED THE WRITE POINTER. The command")
    PrintN("   processor consumed the packets.")
  Else
    PrintN("!! the read pointer did not reach the write pointer. The")
    PrintN("   processor did not consume the ring.")
    ; ask the IOMMU whether IT is why, rather than guessing. A context
    ; fault names the address that was refused, which distinguishes
    ; "translation refused the ring" from "the microcode never started".
    PrintN("   asking the IOMMU whether it refused a transaction:")
    Print("   global fault status sGFSR = ")
    PutHex8(Rd32(#SMMU_BASE + #SMMU_GR0_SGFSR))
    PrintNl()
    Print("   CB0 fault status FSR = ")
    PutHex8(Rd32(#SMMU_CB0 + #SMMU_CB_FSR))
    Print("   faulting address FAR = ")
    PutHex8(Rd32(#SMMU_CB0 + #SMMU_CB_FAR))
    Print(" / ")
    PutHex8(Rd32(#SMMU_CB0 + #SMMU_CB_FAR + 4))
    PrintNl()
    Print("   FSYNR0 = ")
    PutHex8(Rd32(#SMMU_CB0 + #SMMU_CB_FSYNR0))
    PrintNl()
    PrintN("   a non-zero fault status here means translation refused the")
    PrintN("   fetch; all zero means the processor never asked, which points")
    PrintN("   at the microcode or the ring setup instead.")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  SECTION 11w, READING THREE OF THREE. Half a second has passed since
  ;  the start bit. Reading two was taken one MILLISECOND after it, and
  ;  no run in this campaign has ever looked again.
  ;
  ;  If the window has changed between reading two and reading three,
  ;  the fetch is SLOW, not absent, and every conclusion drawn from a
  ;  one-millisecond look is a conclusion about an instrument that was
  ;  too impatient. That would be the cheapest possible explanation for
  ;  the last four rounds and it has never been excluded.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  gUcode0Late = SqeWindow("half a second AFTER the start bit")
  Print("   index 0 across the run:  before = ")
  PutHex8(gUcode0Before)
  Print("   at 1 ms = ")
  PutHex8(gUcode0After)
  Print("   at 500 ms = ")
  PutHex8(gUcode0Late)
  PrintNl()
  If gUcode0Late <> gUcode0After
    PrintN("   IT CHANGED between the two readings. The fetch is slow, not")
    PrintN("   absent, and every earlier reading was simply taken too early.")
  ElseIf gUcode0Late = $017AA049
    PrintN("   index 0 holds the version word. The microcode IS loaded.")
  Else
    PrintN("   unchanged across half a second.")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  ; ---- the marker, which is the real proof ---------------------------
  Say("   S8.7 the marker the processor was asked to write:")
  DcCleanRange(gMarker, gMarker + 64)
  n = PeekN(gMarker) & $FFFFFFFF
  gMarkerLanded = n
  Print("   memory at ")
  PutAddr(gMarker)
  Print(" reads ")
  PutHex8(n)
  PrintNl()
  If n = #MARKER_VALUE
    PrintN("   IT LANDED. The GPU wrote a value into memory and the CPU")
    PrintN("   read it back. That is a thing the CPU cannot have done for")
    PrintN("   it and a status bit cannot fake.")
  Else
    PrintN("   the marker did not land.")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  ; ---- did secure mode actually leave? -------------------------------
  Say("   S8.8 secure mode, read back (a READ is safe; the write is not):")
  gSecvidAfterCp = Rd32(#GPU_RBBM_SECVID_TRUST_CNTL)
  Print("   RBBM_SECVID_TRUST_CNTL = ")
  PutHex8(gSecvidAfterCp)
  PrintNl()
  If gSecvidAfterCp = 0
    PrintN("   SECURE MODE HAS LEFT, and the command processor did it -")
    PrintN("   the CPU never touched this register.")
  Else
    PrintN("   still 1. The processor did not leave secure mode.")
  EndIf
  Flush()
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 4. The gate in front of the graphics register window.
;
;  Two conditions, and BOTH must hold. The interface clock carries the
;  register accesses; the CX domain powers the logic that answers them.
;  Either one missing is a stall, not an error.
; ======================================================================
Procedure Step4_Gate()
  Say("--- S4: may the graphics register window be read? ---")
  gGpuOk = 0
  If gStop <> 0
    PrintN("   refused: an earlier step stopped.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If BitOf(gVCxStatus, #GDSCR_PWR_ON_BIT) = 0
    Print("   CX status at 05991540 read ")
    PutHex8(gVCxStatus)
    PrintN("  - bit 31 (PWR_ON) is clear")
    Halt(4, "the CX power domain is COLLAPSED, so the graphics registers would stall the bus rather than answer. Check that the collapse bit was cleared at 0599106C, and that the status was polled at 05991540 rather than at the control register - this domain reports through its hardware-control register and the control register's own bit 31 reads plausibly and means nothing.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If gVGpuccAhb = 0
    Print("   GPU_CC_AHB_CLK at 05991078 read ")
    PutHex8(Rd32(#GPUCC_AHB_CBCR))
    PrintNl()
    Halt(4, "the graphics interface clock is not running, so nothing would carry a register access even though the power domain is up. Check that bit 0 of 05991078 is set, and that the CX domain really came up - an enabled branch still reads halted while its domain is collapsed, which is the trap this ladder exists for.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  gGpuOk = 1
  PrintN("   the domain is powered and the interface clock is running.")
  PrintN("   The graphics window may be read.")
  Flush()
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 5. The graphics block. Read, then write-and-read-back, then the
;  secure-mode question.
; ======================================================================
Procedure Step5_Gpu()
  Define back.i
  Say("--- S5: the graphics register window ---")
  If gGpuOk = 0
    PrintN("   refused: step 4 did not clear it.")
    Flush()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  Announce(5, "RBBM_STATUS", #GPU_RBBM_STATUS)
  gVRbbmStatus = Rd32(#GPU_RBBM_STATUS)
  Got("RBBM_STATUS", gVRbbmStatus)
  If gVRbbmStatus = 0
    PrintN("   all zeroes. Either the block is idle with every busy bit")
    PrintN("   clear, which is legitimate, or it is dark. The scratch")
    PrintN("   test below is what tells the two apart.")
  EndIf
  If gVRbbmStatus = $FFFFFFFF
    PrintN("   all ones. That is what an absent block reads like.")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  Announce(5, "RBBM_STATUS1", #GPU_RBBM_STATUS1)
  Got("RBBM_STATUS1", Rd32(#GPU_RBBM_STATUS1))
  Announce(5, "RBBM_STATUS2", #GPU_RBBM_STATUS2)
  Got("RBBM_STATUS2", Rd32(#GPU_RBBM_STATUS2))
  Announce(5, "RBBM_STATUS3", #GPU_RBBM_STATUS3)
  Got("RBBM_STATUS3", Rd32(#GPU_RBBM_STATUS3))
  Announce(5, "RBBM_INT_0_STATUS", #GPU_RBBM_INT_0_STATUS)
  Got("RBBM_INT_0_STATUS", Rd32(#GPU_RBBM_INT_0_STATUS))

  ; The clock-gating master switch. If this already reads the value the
  ; reference driver writes for this exact part, something initialised
  ; the block before we got here - which would be a real finding.
  Announce(5, "RBBM_CLOCK_CNTL", #GPU_RBBM_CLOCK_CNTL)
  gVClockCntl = Rd32(#GPU_RBBM_CLOCK_CNTL)
  Got("RBBM_CLOCK_CNTL", gVClockCntl)
  If gVClockCntl = #A702_CLOCK_CNTL_ON
    PrintN("   this is EXACTLY the value the reference driver writes for")
    PrintN("   this part. Something has already run the initialisation")
    PrintN("   sequence. Do not assume a cold block.")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  Announce(5, "CP_SQE_CNTL", #GPU_CP_SQE_CNTL)
  Got("CP_SQE_CNTL", Rd32(#GPU_CP_SQE_CNTL))

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  SECTION 11z, THE CONTROL THAT WAS NEVER TAKEN.
  ;
  ;  CP_INTERRUPT_STATUS has read $80 - CP_ILLEGAL_INSTR_ERROR - at the
  ;  end of every run since M2-T, and three sections have quoted it as
  ;  though the run caused it. NO RUN HAS EVER READ IT BEFORE THE START
  ;  BIT. If it already reads $80 here - cold, before the secure step,
  ;  before the IOMMU and before the whole of step 8 - then it is a
  ;  power-on artefact and it has never meant anything.
  ;
  ;  This is the same shape of omission as the debug port's missing
  ;  address-register control (section 11w) and the two-bit sample that
  ;  became "the block is sealed" (section 11v). An instrument with no
  ;  control is a rumour, and this campaign has paid for that lesson
  ;  three times.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Announce(5, "CP_INTERRUPT_STATUS", #GPU_CP_INTERRUPT_STAT)
  gIntStatS5 = Rd32(#GPU_CP_INTERRUPT_STAT)
  Got("CP_INTERRUPT_STATUS", gIntStatS5)
  If gIntStatS5 = 0
    PrintN("   CLEAR at cold. So anything this register reads later was")
    PrintN("   caused by this run, and $80 at the end would mean the")
    PrintN("   command processor executed something and rejected it.")
  Else
    PrintN("!! ALREADY SET, before this program has touched the command")
    PrintN("   processor at all. Whatever it reads at the end of the run")
    PrintN("   is NOT evidence that the run caused it, and every earlier")
    PrintN("   section that quoted this register needs correcting.")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  Announce(5, "CP_RB_RPTR", #GPU_CP_RB_RPTR)
  Got("CP_RB_RPTR", Rd32(#GPU_CP_RB_RPTR))
  Announce(5, "CP_RB_WPTR", #GPU_CP_RB_WPTR)
  Got("CP_RB_WPTR", Rd32(#GPU_CP_RB_WPTR))

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  THE ONE TEST THAT SEPARATES "RESPONDING" FROM "ALIVE".
  ;
  ;  A read can come back plausible from a bus that is merely completing
  ;  transactions. A value that was written and then read back has to
  ;  have been stored by real logic. The command processor's scratch
  ;  registers are the right place: eight of them, no side effects, and
  ;  nothing downstream reads them.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("   write / read-back on a scratch register:")
  Print("S5 about to WRITE ")
  PutHex8(#SCRATCH_PATTERN)
  Print(" to CP_SCRATCH(0) at ")
  PutHex8(#GPU_CP_SCRATCH0)
  PrintNl()
  Flush()
  Wr32(#GPU_CP_SCRATCH0, #SCRATCH_PATTERN)
  back = Rd32(#GPU_CP_SCRATCH0)
  gVScratchBack = back
  Got("read back", back)
  If back = #SCRATCH_PATTERN
    PrintN("   MATCH. The block stored a value and returned it. This is")
    PrintN("   the strongest evidence this probe can produce that the")
    PrintN("   graphics block is powered, clocked and alive.")
  Else
    PrintN("!! MISMATCH. The window answers but does not store. Do not")
    PrintN("   treat any other reading in this section as meaningful.")
  EndIf
  Flush()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  THE SECURE-MODE QUESTION - the deliverable of this milestone.
  ;
  ;  The reference driver's fallback, when it has no signed image to
  ;  hand the secure world, is to write zero to this register itself -
  ;  and its own comment says that if it guessed wrong, the access is
  ;  blocked and a permissions violation follows. So the register's
  ;  behaviour IS the answer to "is the secure image needed".
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("   secure mode:")
  Announce(5, "RBBM_SECVID_TRUST_CNTL", #GPU_RBBM_SECVID_TRUST_CNTL)
  gVSecvid = Rd32(#GPU_RBBM_SECVID_TRUST_CNTL)
  Got("SECVID_TRUST_CNTL", gVSecvid)
  PrintN("   NOT WRITTEN, and no build can write it - see the note in the")
  PrintN("   secure-world step. The register is read and left alone.")
  Flush()
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  The summary. Everything in one block at the end of the transcript, so
;  a reader does not have to reassemble it from the running commentary.
; ======================================================================
Procedure Summary()
  PrintNl()
  Say("================ SUMMARY ================")
  Print("stopped: ")
  If gStop = 0
    PrintN("no - the ladder ran to the end")
  Else
    Print("YES, at step ")
    PrintDec(gStopWhy)
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  Print("GCC GPU config AHB branch  : ")
  PutHex8(gVGccAhb)
  PrintNl()
  Print("GPUCC interface clock      : ")
  If gVGpuccAhb = 1
    PrintN("running")
  Else
    PrintN("not running / not reached")
  EndIf
  Print("CX domain status register  : ")
  PutHex8(gVCxStatus)
  Print("   PWR_ON=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(gVCxStatus, #GDSCR_PWR_ON_BIT))
  PrintNl()
  Print("GX domain CFG register     : ")
  PutHex8(gVGxCfg)
  Print("   POWER_UP_COMPLETE=")
  PrintDec(BitOf(gVGxCfg, #CFG_GDSCR_PWR_UP_BIT))
  PrintNl()
  Print("RBBM_STATUS                : ")
  PutHex8(gVRbbmStatus)
  PrintNl()
  Print("RBBM_CLOCK_CNTL            : ")
  PutHex8(gVClockCntl)
  PrintNl()
  Print("scratch read-back          : ")
  PutHex8(gVScratchBack)
  If gVScratchBack = #SCRATCH_PATTERN
    PrintN("   MATCH - the block is alive")
  Else
    PrintN("   no match")
  EndIf
  Print("SECVID_TRUST_CNTL          : ")
  PutHex8(gVSecvid)
  Print("   writable=")
  PrintDec(gSecvidWritable)
  PrintNl()
  Print("signed image authenticated : ")
  PrintDec(gZapOk)
  Print("   (reached stage ")
  PrintDec(gZapStage)
  PrintN(" of 8)")
  Print("IOMMU passes addresses     : ")
  PrintDec(gSmmuBypass)
  PrintNl()
  ; The sACR reading. -1 is the never-read sentinel and prints as that,
  ; never as a value, because "CACHE_LOCK clear" on a run that never
  ; reached the block is the kind of summary line a reader in a hurry
  ; believes.
  Print("IOMMU sACR before/after    : ")
  If gSacrBefore = -1
    Print("NOT READ")
  Else
    PutHex8(gSacrBefore)
    Print("  CACHE_LOCK=")
    PrintDec(BitOf(gSacrBefore, #SACR_CACHE_LOCK_BIT))
  EndIf
  Print("  /  ")
  If gSacrAfter = -1
    PrintN("NOT READ")
  Else
    PutHex8(gSacrAfter)
    Print("  CACHE_LOCK=")
    PrintDec(BitOf(gSacrAfter, #SACR_CACHE_LOCK_BIT))
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  Print("IOMMU ID7 (MMU-500 rev)    : ")
  If gId7 = -1
    PrintN("NOT READ")
  Else
    PutHex8(gId7)
    Print("   MAJOR=")
    PrintDec((gId7 >> 4) & $F)
    Print("  MINOR=")
    PrintDec(gId7 & $F)
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  Print("IOMMU sGFSR before/after   : ")
  If gSgfsrBefore = -1
    Print("NOT READ")
  Else
    PutHex8(gSgfsrBefore)
  EndIf
  Print("  /  ")
  If gSgfsrAfter = -1
    PrintN("NOT READ")
  Else
    PutHex8(gSgfsrAfter)
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  ; ---- section 11u, the replicated reset -----------------------------
  Print("Linux reset replicated     : ")
  If gLrRan = 0
    PrintN("NOT RUN")
  Else
    PrintN("ran to the end")
    Print("   sACR  wanted/got        : ")
    PutHex8(gLrSacrWant)
    Print(" / ")
    PutHex8(gLrSacrGot)
    If gLrSacrGot = gLrSacrWant
      PrintN("   STUCK")
    Else
      PrintN("   discarded")
    EndIf
    Print("   sCR0 found/wanted/got   : ")
    PutHex8(gLrScr0Found)
    Print(" / ")
    PutHex8(gLrScr0Want)
    Print(" / ")
    PutHex8(gLrScr0Got)
    If gLrScr0Got = gLrScr0Want
      PrintN("   STUCK")
    ElseIf gLrScr0Got = $002A1C06
      PrintN("   STUCK (bits 4,5 read clear here, as they do for the OS)")
    ElseIf gLrScr0Got <> gLrScr0Found
      PrintN("   CHANGED but matched neither")
    Else
      PrintN("   discarded")
    EndIf
    Print("   S2CRs that took the reset value: ")
    PrintDec(gLrS2crStuck)
    PrintNl()
    Print("   global TLB sync         : ")
    If gLrSyncTimedOut = 1
      PrintN("TIMED OUT")
    Else
      PrintN("completed")
    EndIf
  EndIf
  Print("USFCFG cleared (11v)       : ")
  If gUsfcfgCleared = -1
    PrintN("NOT TRIED")
  ElseIf gUsfcfgCleared = 1
    PrintN("YES - unmatched streams BYPASS, so the GPU passes through")
  Else
    PrintN("NO - the bit is held, like CLIENTPD and SMR.VALID")
  EndIf
  Print("context-bank read-back     : ")
  If gCbStale = -1
    PrintN("NOT TESTED")
  ElseIf gCbStale = 0
    PrintN("REAL - the bank stored the pattern across an interposed read")
  ElseIf gCbStale = 1
    PrintN("A STALE BUS READ - section 11j's conclusion is an artefact")
  ElseIf gCbStale = 2
    PrintN("UNUSABLE - the same register read twice gave two answers")
  Else
    PrintN("NEITHER outcome - see the body, not interpreted")
  EndIf
  Print("command processor consumed : ")
  PrintDec(gCpAlive)
  Print("   read pointer reached ")
  PrintDec(gRptrSeen)
  PrintNl()
  Print("marker in memory           : ")
  PutHex8(gMarkerLanded)
  If gMarkerLanded = #MARKER_VALUE
    PrintN("   LANDED - the GPU wrote it")
  Else
    PrintN("   did not land")
  EndIf
  Print("secure mode after the CP   : ")
  If gSecvidAfterCp = -1
    PrintN("NOT READ - the command processor step did not get that far")
  ElseIf gSecvidAfterCp = 0
    PutHex8(gSecvidAfterCp)
    PrintN("   LEFT")
  Else
    PutHex8(gSecvidAfterCp)
    PrintN("   still on")
  EndIf

  ; ---- section 11w. The control on the instrument itself, and it is
  ;      deliberately the LAST thing the summary says, because if the
  ;      port is not a window then the three lines above about the
  ;      command processor describe an instrument and not a GPU.
  PrintN("---- the debug port, and whether it can be believed ----")
  Print("SQE ucode ADDR wrote/read  : ")
  PutHex8(#SQE_DBG_ADDR_PROBE)
  Print(" / ")
  If gDbgAddrGot = -1
    PrintN("NOT READ - step 8 did not get that far")
  Else
    PutHex8(gDbgAddrGot)
    If gDbgAddrGot = #SQE_DBG_ADDR_PROBE
      PrintN("   THE PORT IS A WINDOW")
    Else
      PrintN("   NOT A WINDOW - everything since 11l is void")
    EndIf
  EndIf
  Print("  auto-increments          : ")
  If gDbgAutoInc = -1
    PrintN("NOT TESTED")
  Else
    PrintDec(gDbgAutoInc)
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  Print("  non-zero swept indices   : ")
  PrintDec(gDbgNonZero)
  Print(" of ")
  PrintDec(#SWEEP_COUNT)
  PrintNl()
  Print("  CP_SQE_STAT non-zero     : ")
  PrintDec(gSqeStatNonZero)
  Print(" of ")
  PrintDec(#GPU_CP_SQE_STAT_COUNT)
  PrintNl()
  Print("SQE ucode[0] before/1ms/500ms: ")
  If gUcode0Before = -1
    PrintN("NOT READ - step 8 did not reach the debug port")
  Else
    PutHex8(gUcode0Before)
    Print(" / ")
    PutHex8(gUcode0After)
    Print(" / ")
    PutHex8(gUcode0Late)
    Print("   (must be 017AA049 if loaded)")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  Print("SQE imem write A5C3F096    : ")
  If gImemWrote = -1
    PrintN("NOT TRIED - step 8 did not get that far")
  Else
    PutHex8(gImemWrote)
    Print(" / ")
    PutHex8(gImemWrote1)
    If gImemWrote = #SQE_DBG_DATA_PAT0 And gImemWrote1 = #SQE_DBG_DATA_PAT1
      PrintN("   THE PORT WRITES - imem is loadable from the CPU")
    Else
      PrintN("   the data half did not take it")
    EndIf
    Print("  and after the start bit  : ")
    PutHex8(gImemAfterStart)
    If gImemWrote <> #SQE_DBG_DATA_PAT0
      PrintN("   (undecidable - the write never took)")
    ElseIf gImemAfterStart = #SQE_DBG_DATA_PAT0
      PrintN("   SURVIVED - nothing wrote imem at the start")
    ElseIf gImemAfterStart = 0
      PrintN("   ZEROED - a fetch came back empty")
    Else
      PrintN("   replaced by a third value - read the body")
    EndIf
  EndIf
  Print("RBBM_STATUS3 after         : ")
  PutHex8(gStatus3After)
  Print("   SMMU_STALLED_ON_FAULT=")
  If gStatus3After = -1
    PrintN("not read")
  Else
    PrintDec(BitOf(gStatus3After, 24))
    PrintNl()
  EndIf

  PrintN("---- section 11z: is the command processor executing? ----")
  Print("CP_INTERRUPT_STATUS timeline: cold ")
  PutHex8(gIntStatS5)
  Print(" / pre-start ")
  PutHex8(gIntStatPreStart)
  Print(" / 1 ms ")
  PutHex8(gIntStat1ms)
  Print(" / after ring ")
  PutHex8(gIntStatAfter)
  PrintNl()
  If gIntStatS5 = -1
    PrintN("  the cold control was NOT TAKEN - do not quote this register")
  ElseIf gIntStatS5 = gIntStatAfter
    PrintN("  NEVER MOVED - power-on state, not a result of the run")
  ElseIf gIntStat1ms <> gIntStatPreStart
    PrintN("  changed ON THE START BIT - the SQE executed and rejected it")
  Else
    PrintN("  changed only after the ring - the hand-over caused it")
  EndIf
  Print("RBBM_STATUS pre-start      : ")
  PutHex8(gRbbmPreStart)
  PrintNl()
  Print("ROQ address-half control   : ")
  If gRoqAddrOk = 1
    PrintN("STORED AND RETURNED - the port is a window")
  ElseIf gRoqAddrOk = 0
    PrintN("DID NOT STORE - the sweep below decides nothing")
  Else
    PrintN("not tried")
  EndIf
  Print("ROQ declared depth         : ")
  PrintDec(gRoqDepth)
  PrintN(" dwords (CP_ROQ_THRESHOLDS_2 >> 14)")
  Print("ROQ non-zero  start/after  : ")
  PrintDec(gRoqNzStart)
  Print(" / ")
  PrintDec(gRoqNzWptr)
  PrintNl()
  Print("ring dwords found in ROQ   : ")
  PrintDec(gRoqFoundRing)
  Print(" of ")
  PrintDec(gRingPos)
  If gRoqFoundRing > 0
    PrintN("   THE PROCESSOR FETCHED THE RING")
  ElseIf gRoqAddrOk = 1
    PrintN("   none - and the port is a window, so the queue is genuinely empty")
  Else
    PrintN("   none, but the port failed its control - this says nothing")
  EndIf
  PrintN("  (11aa: an empty ROQ does NOT convict the fetch path on its own.")
  PrintN("   A processor that faulted at instruction zero never reached the")
  PrintN("   point of asking for the ring, and the timeline above says it")
  PrintN("   did. That is why this build measures the configuration block.)")

  PrintN("---- section 11ab: does the block accept its configuration? ----")
  Print("S8.2 writes read back      : ")
  PrintDec(gCfgTook)
  Print(" took / ")
  PrintDec(gCfgZero)
  Print(" zero / ")
  PrintDec(gCfgOther)
  Print(" neither, of ")
  PrintDec(gCfgWrites)
  PrintNl()
  Print("64-bit ADDR_MODE switches  : ")
  PrintDec(gCfgAddrMode)
  Print(" of 12 read back 1")
  If gCfgAddrMode = 12
    PrintN("   all took")
  ElseIf gCfgAddrMode = -1
    PrintN("   step 8.2 never ran")
  ElseIf gCfgZero > 0
    PrintN("   !! A WRITE WAS REFUSED - buffers are above 4 GB")
  Else
    PrintN("   shortfall is unlanded reads, not refused writes - UNDECIDED")
  EndIf
  Print("GBIF_HALT       wrote 0 -> ")
  PutHex8(gGbifHaltGot)
  If gGbifHaltGot = 0
    PrintN("   cleared")
  Else
    PrintN("   !! WILL NOT CLEAR")
  EndIf
  Print("RBBM_GBIF_HALT  wrote 0 -> ")
  PutHex8(gRbbmGbifHaltGot)
  If gRbbmGbifHaltGot = 0
    PrintN("   cleared")
  Else
    PrintN("   !! WILL NOT CLEAR")
  EndIf
  Print("GBIF_HALT_ACK      bef/aft: ")
  PutHex8(gGbifAckBefore)
  Print(" / ")
  PutHex8(gGbifAckAfter)
  PrintN("   (observation only - see 11ab)")
  Print("RBBM_GBIF_HALT_ACK bef/aft: ")
  PutHex8(gRbbmAckBefore)
  Print(" / ")
  PutHex8(gRbbmAckAfter)
  PrintN("   (observation only - see 11ab)")
  If gCfgWrites > 0 And gCfgTook = gCfgWrites
    PrintN("  EVERY ONE OF THE STEP-8.2 WRITES TOOK. The whole")
    PrintN("  configuration block is eliminated in one run, and the")
    PrintN("  remaining suspects are the interconnect vote and the")
    PrintN("  IOMMU's three held bits. A negative result, and a real one.")
  EndIf

  PrintN("NOTE: no chip id was read, because this part has no chip-id")
  PrintN("register in the open register map. See the vault plan.")
  Print("transcript flushes: ")
  PrintDec(gFlushes)
  PrintNl()
  ; A sink that silently truncates is a silent wrong answer. On the first
  ; silicon run this buffer filled at 2048 bytes and the log simply
  ; stopped mid-sentence - which from a board that can hang the bus looks
  ; exactly like a hang. Print the counter so the question is never open.
  Print("bytes DROPPED (0 is what you want): ")
  PrintDec(gQTrunc)
  PrintNl()
  Print("buffer used: ")
  PrintDec(gQOutLen)
  Print(" of ")
  PrintDec(#Q_OUT_MAX)
  PrintNl()
  PrintN("========================================")
  Flush()
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
Procedure.i Main()
  Define ih.i
  Define st.i

  ; ---- CAPTURE x0/x1 FIRST (UEFI ImageHandle / SystemTable) ----------
  ASM
    adrp x9, global_gqih
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gqih
    str  x0, [x9]
    adrp x9, global_gqst
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gqst
    str  x1, [x9]
  EndASM
  ih = gQIh
  st = gQSt

  If QEfiInit(ih, st) = 0
    ; No validated SystemTable means no transcript and no way to report.
    ; Touching a single hardware register in that state would be
    ; unobservable if it went wrong, which is the one thing this whole
    ; program exists to avoid.
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  QOutReset()
  Say("== Arduino UNO Q - GPU bring-up probe, BUILD M2-X ==")
  Say("Written to qgpu11.log. The name is bumped EVERY generation: a")
  Say("deleted-then-partly-rewritten file on this boot partition left a")
  Say("remnant that looked like a transcript and was not one.")
  Say("If these lines are all there is, the image ran and stopped before")
  Say("touching any hardware - this flush is deliberately ahead of every")
  Say("register access so that case is distinguishable from a bus stall.")
  Say("Read the vault note 'Adreno 702 bring-up plan' before this log.")
  Say("THIS BUILD IS SECTION 11ab, and it asks ONE question: DOES THE")
  Say("BLOCK ACCEPT THE CONFIGURATION IT IS GIVEN?")
  Say("What 11aa settled, from the M2-W transcript:")
  Say("CP_INTERRUPT_STATUS is 00000000 COLD and 00000000 immediately")
  Say("before the start bit, and 00000080 one millisecond after it. The")
  Say("illegal-instruction error is caused by CP_SQE_CNTL = 1 itself,")
  Say("before the ring exists. The processor runs and rejects the first")
  Say("thing it runs. And the read-only queue is genuinely empty - its")
  Say("address half stored and returned 000001A7, the depth came off the")
  Say("silicon, all 512 dwords were swept and 0 of our 16 ring dwords")
  Say("are there. But those two facts are NOT INDEPENDENT: a processor")
  Say("that faulted at instruction zero never got as far as asking for")
  Say("the ring, so an empty queue does not convict the fetch path.")
  Say("What the failure IS now located to: the SQE fetches its own")
  Say("microcode from CP_SQE_INSTR_BASE when the start bit is set. That")
  Say("is the GPU's FIRST memory transaction, earlier than the ring and")
  Say("invisible in the ROQ. The pointer is right (reads back")
  Say("00000001:0A1F8000), the microcode is in DRAM at that address and")
  Say("the CPU can read its header, and the instruction memory never")
  Say("receives it. Nothing faults, anywhere, either time.")
  Say("SO THIS BUILD ADDS NO WRITE AT ALL. It reads back every one of")
  Say("the twenty-eight writes step 8.2 has made on every run since")
  Say("M2-R and never once checked - printing one line, 'done.' Those")
  Say("writes include the two GBIF halt registers, which decide whether")
  Say("GPU-initiated transactions may leave the block at all, and the")
  Say("twelve *_ADDR_MODE_CNTL switches that put every block into")
  Say("64-bit addressing. Our buffers are ABOVE 4 GB. A block left in")
  Say("32-bit mode fetches from 0A1F8000 instead of 1_0A1F8000 - a")
  Say("valid-looking address holding something else, no fault, junk in")
  Say("the instruction memory, and an illegal instruction one")
  Say("millisecond after the start bit. That is the M2-W transcript")
  Say("exactly, and it has never been ruled out.")
  Say("The two GBIF HALT_ACK registers are read too, before and after,")
  Say("but as an OBSERVATION: the reference driver only reads them to")
  Say("confirm a halt it requested, and mesa's crash dump shows HALT 0")
  Say("with ACK 3. Only the halt registers' own read-back may decide.")
  Say("This is the fourth control this campaign has had to add to")
  Say("something it was already relying on, after the two-bit IOMMU")
  Say("sample, the debug port's address register and the ROQ's address")
  Say("half. Everything else is M2-W unchanged.")
  Say("IGNORE THE PARAGRAPH BELOW - it is M2-U's and is kept only so the")
  Say("two transcripts can be diffed line for line:")
  Say("THIS BUILD IS SECTION 11w, and it changes NO register that build")
  Say("M2-T did not change. It adds one instrument, and that instrument")
  Say("is a CONTROL ON ANOTHER INSTRUMENT.")
  Say("Since section 11l every statement about why milestone 2 stops has")
  Say("rested on 'the command processor's instruction memory reads zero'.")
  Say("That comes from the indexed debug port at CP_SQE_UCODE_DBG_ADDR")
  Say("and DATA - a port nobody has ever written a value to and read")
  Say("back. It has never had a positive control of any kind.")
  Say("So: 00001234 goes into its address register and is read back;")
  Say("the auto-increment section 11l flagged and could not check is")
  Say("checked; eleven indices are swept across the whole 0x8000-deep")
  Say("window with the address rewritten before every read; and a SECOND")
  Say("indexed port, CP_SQE_STAT, is read as an independent witness.")
  Say("All of it THREE times - before the start bit, one millisecond")
  Say("after it, and half a second after it. No run in this campaign has")
  Say("ever looked twice, and a slow fetch and an absent one look")
  Say("identical if you only look once.")
  Say("It also reads RBBM_STATUS3 bit 24, SMMU_STALLED_ON_FAULT - the")
  Say("GPU's own answer to the question five rounds have inferred from")
  Say("the IOMMU's side of the interface.")
  Say("Everything else is M2-T unchanged: section 11v's USFCFG attempt,")
  Say("which is refused, and section 11u's arm_smmu_device_reset()")
  Say("replicated in the reference source's own order, byte for byte,")
  Say("with the test_smr_masks pattern and the context-bank stale-read")
  Say("test. That sequence ran clean three times and it is kept exactly")
  Say("so that sCR0 is again 002A1C06 with GFRE and GFIE set, which is")
  Say("what makes the fault registers mean anything at step 8.")
  If #GPUPROBE_ENABLE = 0
    Say("mode: OBSERVE ONLY. Nothing is written to any clock or power")
    Say("      register. The only writes are to a scratch register and")
    Say("      to the secure-mode register, both inside the graphics")
    Say("      block, and both only after the ladder has cleared them.")
  Else
    Say("mode: ENABLE. This build may bring the domain up.")
  EndIf
  PrintNl()
  Flush()

  Step1_GccPath()
  Step2_Gate()
  Step3_Gpucc()
  CompilerIf #GPUPROBE_ENABLE = 1
    CxPowerUp()
    GxPowerUp()
    Step3e_Clocks()
  CompilerEndIf
  Step4_Gate()
  Step5_Gpu()
  CompilerIf #GPUPROBE_ZAP = 1
    Step6_Zap()
  CompilerEndIf
  CompilerIf #GPUPROBE_CP = 1
    Step7_Smmu()
    Step8_Cp()
  CompilerEndIf
  Summary()

  Say("probe complete - handing the board back to Debian")

  ; The board is a one-shot boot entry. If this image does not return,
  ; the board does not come back, and there is no console to notice.
  QEfiReset()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
